/* Generated by scripts/check.mjs --write from the lesson pages and glossary-terms.js. Don't edit by hand. */
window.SEARCH_INDEX = {"lessons":[{"slug":"how-the-web-works","num":"1.1","title":"How the Web Works","desc":"Browsers, servers and URLs, what happens when a page loads, and setting up your editor.","props":["URL","404","file://"],"intro":"A website is a folder of files on someone else's computer. Your browser asks for those files one at a time and puts the page together. Knowing that explains most \"it worked on my laptop\" problems.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-a-website-is-a-folder-of-files","title":"A website is a folder of files","kind":"Read","text":"When you visit a site, your browser is the client. Somewhere else, a server is a computer holding a folder of files: HTML, CSS, images. Your browser asks for a file, the server sends it back, and the browser builds the page. That's the whole web, repeated billions of times a day. The address you type is a URL, and every part of it means something: https://trails.example.com/hikes/mesa.html └─┬─┘ └───────┬───────┘└────────┬──────┘ protocol domain path to the file The domain name finds the server. The path says which file you want from its folders. If the path ends in a folder, like /hikes/, the server sends that folder's index.html. The server answers every request with an HTTP status code. 200 means \"here it is.\" 404 means there's no file at that path. 301 means it moved, and here's the new address. A page is never just one file. The HTML arrives first. As the browser reads it, it finds a stylesheet, a script and some images, and asks for each one separately. If one of those requests fails, the rest of the page still shows up without it. That's why a typo in a file name gives you a page with no styles instead of a blank screen. Tip Most servers care about capital letters, even though your laptop may not. Style.css and style.css are different files online. Use lowercase file names and you'll never chase this bug. Play"},{"id":"sec-read-a-url","title":"Read a URL","kind":"Play","text":"Every address has the same parts. Click each part to see what it does. Take apart any URL This uses the same URL parser your browser uses. Play"},{"id":"sec-one-page-many-requests","title":"One page, many requests","kind":"Play","text":"Load the page and watch the log. The browser gets the HTML first, reads it, and only then discovers the CSS, images and fonts it also needs. Break something to see what happens. Load the page Break something The HTML links Style.css, but the file is style.css The photo is a 5 MB file straight off a phone The home page is named Home.html, not index.html Request times are made up, but the order is real. Open DevTools and its Network tab on any site to see the real list. Network log trailclub.github.io/ Play"},{"id":"sec-set-up-your-computer","title":"Set up your computer","kind":"Play","text":"You don't need a server to start. A text editor and a browser are enough. 1 Install a code editor VS Code is free and works on every computer. It colors your code and closes tags for you. Don't use Word or TextEdit's rich text mode: they add hidden formatting. 2 Make one project folder Put every file for the site inside it, with index.html at the top level. Open the whole folder in your editor (File → Open Folder), not single files. 3 Open the page in a browser Double-click index.html, or drag it into a browser window. Save in the editor, then refresh the browser to see each change. 4 Optional: a local server The VS Code extension Live Server serves your folder at http://127.0.0.1:5500 and refreshes the page every time you save. It behaves more like the real web than double-clicking. Address bar starts with Where the files come from Watch out for file:///Users/you/site/ Straight from your own disk. No server involved. Paths starting with / point to the root of your hard drive, so they break. http://127.0.0.1:5500/ A small server running on your own computer (127.0.0.1 always means \"this computer\"). Only you can see it. It stops when you close the editor. https://you.github.io/site/ A real web server anyone can reach. File names are case-sensitive here, even if they weren't on your laptop. Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-a-full-url","title":"Build a full URL","kind":"Practice","text":"A relative link only works on your own site. To link somewhere else, you need every part of the address."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The browser is the client. The computer holding the files is the server. The browser sends a request, and the server sends back a response with a status code. 200 means OK. 404 means the server has no file at that path. 301 means it moved. Each file is its own request. The HTML arrives first, and the browser finds the rest by reading it. A domain name like github.io is a human-friendly label. DNS looks up the server's numeric address behind it. When a URL ends in a folder (/hikes/), the server sends that folder's index.html. https encrypts the connection. Modern browsers warn visitors about plain http sites."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Editing a file, forgetting to save, and wondering why the browser hasn't changed. Opening a single file in the editor instead of the project folder, then saving new files in the wrong place. Testing only with file://, then finding broken images online because of capital letters in file names. Thinking the page is one file. If the CSS or an image fails to load, the HTML still shows up without it. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["how-the-web-works/quiz"],"exercises":["how-the-web-works/ex-url"]},{"slug":"html-syntax","num":"1.2","title":"HTML Syntax","desc":"Elements, tags and attributes, plus how whitespace and special characters behave.","props":["<tag>","attribute","&amp;"],"intro":"HTML is made of elements, and elements are made of tags, attributes and content. Learn the names for each part, then see how the browser treats spaces and special characters.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-elements-tags-and-attributes","title":"Elements, tags and attributes","kind":"Read","text":"An HTML page is a set of elements, and each element tells the browser what a piece of content is: a heading, a paragraph, a link. Most elements are written as an opening tag, some content, and a closing tag with a slash: <a href=\"trails.html\" class=\"button\">See all trails</a> Here <a> is the opening tag and </a> the closing tag. href=\"trails.html\" and class=\"button\" are attributes: extra information that always goes inside the opening tag, written as name=\"value\". \"See all trails\" is the content, the part you see. A few elements have no content and no closing tag, like <img> and <br>. Those are empty elements. Elements go inside other elements, which is called nesting. Close them in the reverse order you opened them, like boxes inside boxes: <p><strong>Go</strong></p>, not <p><strong>Go</p></strong>. Two things surprise everyone. First, whitespace collapses: any run of spaces, tabs and line breaks shows as a single space, so you can't line things up by typing spaces. Second, some characters mean something to HTML. A < starts a tag, so to show one as text you write a character entity: &lt; for < and &amp; for &. Watch out Code pasted from a word processor often has curly “smart quotes” around attribute values. Browsers don't treat them as quotes, so the attribute quietly breaks. Always type code in a code editor. Play"},{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-an-element","title":"Anatomy of an element","kind":"Play","text":"Click any part, or pick a name below. Empty (void) elements have no closing tag <img><br><hr><input><meta><link> They can't hold content, so there's nothing to close. Writing <img /> with a slash is allowed but not needed. Play"},{"id":"sec-whitespace-collapses","title":"Whitespace collapses","kind":"Play","text":"Type extra spaces, tabs and blank lines. The browser squashes every run of whitespace into a single space. What you type in your HTML What the browser shows To start a new paragraph, use a new <p>. Use CSS for spacing, not lots of spaces or <br>s. Play"},{"id":"sec-special-characters","title":"Special characters","kind":"Play","text":"Some characters mean something to HTML, like < which starts a tag. To show them as text, write an entity. Type below to see what needs escaping. Text you want to show Write this in your HTML Common entities Shows Write Why < &lt; Would start a tag > &gt; Pairs with &lt; & &amp; Would start an entity \" &quot; Inside attribute values © &copy; Easier to type &nbsp; A space that never breaks the line Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-broken-tags","title":"Fix the broken tags","kind":"Practice","text":"The browser guesses its way around each of these mistakes. Fix them so it doesn't have to."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Elements nest like boxes inside boxes. Close the inner one before the outer one. Attributes go in the opening tag, as name=\"value\". Always quote the value. Tag and attribute names aren't case-sensitive, but write them in lowercase. Comments look like <!-- like this --> and don't appear on the page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting a closing tag, so everything after it gets the same formatting. Curly \"smart quotes\" pasted from a word processor around attribute values. Spaces around the equals sign in odd places, or missing quotes around values with spaces. Using lots of &nbsp; or <br> to push things around. That's CSS's job. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["html-syntax/quiz"],"exercises":["html-syntax/ex-syntax"]},{"slug":"document-structure","num":"1.3","title":"Document Structure","desc":"The skeleton every page needs: doctype, head, body, and how they nest.","props":["<!DOCTYPE>","<head>","<meta>"],"intro":"Every HTML page starts with the same skeleton. Click any line to learn what it does, then remove it to see what breaks.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-skeleton-every-page-needs","title":"The skeleton every page needs","kind":"Read","text":"Every HTML page starts with the same few lines. You'll type them so often that they'll become automatic, but each one is there for a reason: <!DOCTYPE html> <html lang=\"en\"> <head> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> <title>Boulder Trail Club</title> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"> </head> <body> <h1>Boulder Trail Club</h1> </body> </html> The DOCTYPE tells the browser to use modern standards mode. Without it, browsers fall back to \"quirks mode\" and imitate the bugs of 1990s browsers. Everything else lives inside <html>, whose lang attribute tells screen readers and translation tools which language the page is in. Inside it are two parts: The head holds information about the page: its character set, its title for the browser tab, and links to stylesheets. Nothing in the head shows on the page. The body holds everything visitors see: headings, paragraphs, images, links. The viewport meta tag deserves a special mention. Without it, phones pretend to be a wide desktop screen and shrink your whole page until it's unreadable. With it, the page fits the phone's real width, which is what makes responsive design possible. Tip Indent each nested element by two spaces, as above. The browser ignores the indents, but they let you see at a glance what's inside what, and spot a tag that was never closed. Play"},{"id":"sec-take-the-skeleton-apart","title":"Take the skeleton apart","kind":"Play","text":"Click any line to learn what it does, then remove it and see what breaks. On a phone How it nests <html> <head> info about the page, not shown meta · title · link <body> everything people see h1 · p · img · nav · … Practice"},{"id":"sec-write-a-complete-page","title":"Write a complete page","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn two lines of content into a full HTML document, from doctype to closing tag."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"<head> holds information about the page. <body> holds what visitors see. Every element you open, you close, and tags close in the reverse order they opened. Indent nested elements so you can see the structure at a glance. Save the file as index.html for the main page of a site. Servers look for that name first."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Putting visible content like <h1> inside <head>. Having two <body> elements, or content after </html>. Leaving out the viewport meta tag, so phones show a tiny zoomed-out page. Linking a stylesheet with the wrong path, like href=\"style.css\" when the file is in a css/ folder. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["document-structure/quiz"],"exercises":["document-structure/ex-skeleton"]},{"slug":"head-metadata","num":"1.4","title":"The Head & Metadata","desc":"Page titles, favicons, meta descriptions, and the preview card when a link is shared.","props":["<title>","favicon","og:image"],"intro":"Nothing in the <head> shows on the page, but it decides how your site looks everywhere else: the browser tab, search results, and the preview card when someone shares a link.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-the-head-controls","title":"What the head controls","kind":"Read","text":"Nothing in the head appears on your page, but it decides how your site shows up everywhere else: in the browser tab, in bookmarks, in search results, and in the little preview card when someone pastes your link into a chat. Four things do most of the work: <title> is the text in the tab and the clickable headline in search results. Make it specific, most important part first: \"Royal Arch Trail | Boulder Trail Club\". The meta description is a one-sentence summary, about 150 characters, that search engines may show under the title. A favicon is the small icon in the tab, linked with <link rel=\"icon\">. Open Graph tags, written og:, control the preview card in messages and social posts: its title, description and image. <title>Royal Arch Trail | Boulder Trail Club</title> <meta name=\"description\" content=\"A steep 3.4-mile hike to a sandstone arch above Boulder.\"> <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\"> <meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://trails.example.com/img/royal-arch.jpg\"> Notice that the og:image is a full address starting with https://. The preview card is built by another company's server, which has no idea where your page lives, so a relative path like img/royal-arch.jpg won't work there. Watch out When you copy one page to start another, the head comes along too. It's easy to end up with ten pages all titled \"Home\". Change the title and description on every new page. Play"},{"id":"sec-see-your-head-everywhere","title":"See your head everywhere","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the title, description, icon and Open Graph tags, and watch the tab, the search result and the share card update. <head> <title> meta description link rel=\"icon\" Open Graph tags Browser tab New Tab Search result Shared in a chat or social post Copy Favicons"},{"id":"sec-make-the-little-tab-icon","title":"Make the little tab icon","kind":"Favicons","text":"A favicon is a small square image. One SVG covers most browsers. Add a PNG for phones that save your site to the home screen. 1. Make a square image Use a simple shape or one or two letters. Fine detail disappears at 16 × 16 pixels. Save it as favicon.svg, or as a 32 × 32 favicon.png. 2. Link it in the head <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\" type=\"image/svg+xml\"> 3. Add a home-screen icon <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" href=\"icon-180.png\"> A 180 × 180 PNG, used when someone adds your site to their phone's home screen. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fill-in-the-head","title":"Fill in the head","kind":"Practice","text":"Nothing in the head shows on the page, but it decides how the page looks in tabs, search results and shared links."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Give every page its own <title>, most specific part first: \"Royal Arch Trail | Boulder Trail Club\". It's also what bookmarks and screen readers announce. The meta description is a one-sentence pitch of about 150 characters. Search engines may show it under your title. Open Graph (og:) tags control the preview card in chats and social posts. Without them, apps guess, or show a bare link. og:image needs a full https:// URL, not a relative path, because the image is fetched by another company's server. A good share image is 1200 × 630 pixels."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Copying one page to start another and leaving the same title on every page. Putting <meta> or <link> tags in the body. They only work in the head. Expecting <meta name=\"keywords\"> to help. Search engines have ignored it for years. Changing the favicon and not seeing it. Browsers cache favicons hard: try a private window. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["head-metadata/quiz"],"exercises":["head-metadata/ex-head"]},{"slug":"text-semantics","num":"1.5","title":"Text Semantics","desc":"Headings, strong vs b, em vs i, quotes, code and more, chosen for meaning.","props":["<h1>–<h6>","<strong>","<blockquote>"],"intro":"Elements like <strong> and <em> say what text means, and the look comes from that meaning. Click any highlighted text in this trail report to see which element it uses and why.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-choose-elements-for-meaning","title":"Choose elements for meaning","kind":"Read","text":"HTML has two dozen elements for text, and the trick is to choose them for what the text means, not how it looks. The look is CSS's job, and you can always change it. The meaning is what screen readers, search engines and browser reading modes rely on. Start with headings. They form an outline of the page: one <h1> for the page's main title, <h2> for each section, <h3> for parts of a section. Screen reader users jump between headings to scan a page, the way you skim bold titles, so don't skip levels just because <h4> looks the right size. Pick the right level, then resize it with CSS. <h1>Royal Arch Trail</h1> <h2>Before you go</h2> <p><strong>Bring water.</strong> There's none on the trail.</p> <h2>The route</h2> Inside paragraphs, pairs of elements look alike but mean different things: <strong> means important, and <b> just draws attention without extra importance. <em> means stressed, the word you'd say louder, and <i> sets text apart, like a scientific name or a book title in another language. For quotations, use <blockquote> for a long, standalone quote and <q> for a short one inside a sentence. <cite> marks the title of a work, and <code> marks a piece of code. Tip If you're reaching for an element because of its default look, stop and ask what the text is. The answer tells you the element; CSS handles the rest. This is the core idea of semantic HTML. Play"},{"id":"sec-inspect-a-trail-report","title":"Inspect a trail report","kind":"Play","text":"Click any highlighted text to see which element it uses and why. Show the tags Break the headings Heading outline (what screen reader users skim) Practice"},{"id":"sec-which-element","title":"Which element?","kind":"Practice","text":"Practice"},{"id":"sec-give-the-text-meaning","title":"Give the text meaning","kind":"Practice","text":"This notice looks like a heading and some bold text, but its HTML says nothing about what anything is."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"One <h1> per page, then <h2> for sections and <h3> inside those. Don't skip levels. <strong> means important and <em> means stressed. <b> and <i> only change the look. Pick elements for meaning, then change the look with CSS. A heading can be small; strong text doesn't have to be bold. Use <p> for each paragraph instead of <br><br> between them."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Picking <h4> because you want smaller text. Making a fake heading with bold text in a <p>. Using <blockquote> just to indent something. Wrapping whole paragraphs in <strong>. When everything is important, nothing is."}],"quizzes":["text-semantics/quiz"],"exercises":["text-semantics/ex-semantics"]},{"slug":"lists","num":"1.6","title":"Lists","desc":"Unordered, ordered and description lists, nesting, and styling markers.","props":["<ul>","<ol>","<dl>"],"intro":"HTML has three kinds of list. Choose the one that matches your content, then use CSS to change how the markers look, or remove them to build a menu.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-three-kinds-of-list","title":"Three kinds of list","kind":"Read","text":"Lists are everywhere on the web, often where you wouldn't expect them. A row of links in a site's menu is a list. So is a set of steps, a set of product features, or a glossary. HTML gives you three kinds: <ul>, an unordered list, when the order doesn't matter, like gear to pack. <ol>, an ordered list, when it does, like directions. The browser numbers the items for you. <dl>, a description list, for pairs of terms and descriptions, with <dt> and <dd>. In the first two, every item is an <li>, and <li>s are the only things allowed directly inside. To make a sub-list, put the inner list inside an item, after its text: <ul> <li>Water <ul> <li>Two liters in summer</li> </ul> </li> <li>Map</li> </ul> Never type your own numbers or dashes into list items. Let the browser draw the markers, then change them with CSS: list-style-type picks the marker, and ::marker lets you color it. Navigation menus are usually a <ul> inside a <nav>, with the bullets removed and the items laid out in a row. It's still a list of links, so screen readers announce \"list, 4 items\" and visitors know what to expect. Watch out list-style: none removes the bullets but not the indent. Lists also have a default padding-left, so set padding: 0 too. Play"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-right-list","title":"Pick the right list","kind":"Play","text":"<ul> <ol> <dl> <ol> attributes start type 1, 2, 3 a, b, c A, B, C i, ii, iii I, II, III reversed Include a nested list Play"},{"id":"sec-style-the-markers","title":"Style the markers","kind":"Play","text":"The same unordered list, restyled with CSS. The last option turns it into a navigation menu, which is how most site menus are built. list-style-type disc circle square decimal lower-alpha upper-roman \"→ \" (any text) none list-style-position outside inside padding-left Color the markers with ::marker Turn it into a menu Trails Maps Conditions Gear checklist Practice"},{"id":"sec-turn-lines-into-lists","title":"Turn lines into lists","kind":"Practice","text":"Lines separated by look like a list, but the browser can't tell. Make them real lists."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"<ul> when order doesn't matter, <ol> when it does, <dl> for terms and their descriptions. A list's only direct children are <li>s (or <dt>/<dd> in a dl). To nest a list, put the inner list inside an <li>, after its text. Navigation menus are usually a <ul> inside a <nav>, with bullets removed."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Putting a nested <ul> directly inside the outer <ul> instead of inside an <li>. Typing your own numbers or dashes into list items. Removing bullets but not the default padding-left, leaving a mysterious indent. Using <br> between lines instead of real list items. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["lists/quiz"],"exercises":["lists/ex-lists"]},{"slug":"semantic-elements","num":"1.7","title":"Semantic Elements","desc":"When to use header, nav, main, section, and article instead of div.","props":["<main>","<nav>","<section>"],"intro":"Semantic elements describe what content is, not how it looks. They look the same as a plain div, but screen readers, search engines and other developers can tell a navigation menu from a footer.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-say-what-each-part-of-the-page-is","title":"Say what each part of the page is","kind":"Read","text":"Most pages have the same parts: a header with the logo, a navigation menu, the main content, maybe a sidebar, and a footer. You could build all of them out of <div>s, and they'd look exactly the same. But a <div> means only \"a box,\" and semantic HTML has an element for each part that says what it is: <header>logo and site name</header> <nav>main menu</nav> <main> <article>one trail report</article> <aside>related trails</aside> </main> <footer>contact and copyright</footer> Why bother, if they look the same? Because browsers turn these elements into landmarks. A screen reader user can jump straight to the main content or the navigation instead of listening to the whole page from the top. Search engines use them to find what matters, and the next developer to open your code can read its structure at a glance. A few rules of thumb: Use one <main> per page, for the content that's unique to that page. <article> is something that would make sense on its own, like a blog post or a product card. <section> is a themed part of something bigger, and usually has a heading. Use <nav> for major navigation, not every group of links. Reach for <div> when nothing else fits, such as a wrapper you only need for layout. That's its job. Note Semantic elements don't change how anything looks. You still style them with CSS, exactly as you would a div. Play"},{"id":"sec-semantic-html-or-div-soup","title":"Semantic HTML or div soup?","kind":"Play","text":"Switch between the two versions of the same page, then click any region to see what it means and how a screen reader announces it. Semantic HTML \"Div soup\" Click a region to see what it means. Practice"},{"id":"sec-which-element-fits","title":"Which element fits?","kind":"Practice","text":"Pick the best element for each piece of content. Practice"},{"id":"sec-replace-the-divs","title":"Replace the divs","kind":"Practice","text":"Every box here is a div. Swap in elements that say what each part of the page is."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Use <div> only when no other element describes the content. It means \"a box, nothing more.\" One <main> per page, holding the content that's unique to that page. <article> makes sense on its own (a blog post, a product card). <section> is a themed part of something bigger and usually has a heading. Semantic elements don't change how things look. You still style them with CSS."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Choosing an element for its default look, like <h3> because the text is small. Style the size with CSS instead. Making a clickable <div> instead of a <button>. Keyboards and screen readers can't use it. Wrapping every group of links in <nav>. Save it for major navigation. Skipping heading levels (h1 to h4) to get a smaller font."}],"quizzes":["semantic-elements/quiz"],"exercises":["semantic-elements/ex-landmarks"]},{"slug":"links-paths","num":"1.8","title":"Links & Paths","desc":"Relative paths, ../, and why a link works on your laptop but not online.","props":["href","../","#anchor"],"intro":"A relative path is directions from the file you're writing in to the file you want. Pick where you are, click where you want to go, then try writing the path yourself.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-directions-from-here-to-there","title":"Directions from here to there","kind":"Read","text":"Every link has an href that says where it goes. Links to other sites use an absolute path, the full address: https://www.nps.gov/romo/. Links between your own files use a relative path, which is directions from the file you're writing in to the file you want. Picture this project: trail-club/ ├── index.html ├── about.html ├── css/ │ └── style.css └── hikes/ ├── mesa.html └── photos/ └── mesa.jpg From index.html, the path to the Mesa page is hikes/mesa.html: into the hikes folder, then the file. From mesa.html, the photo is photos/mesa.jpg. And from mesa.html back to the home page is ../index.html. The ../ means \"go up one folder\", and you use it once for each level you need to climb. Two details catch everyone. A path inside a CSS file, like url(...), starts from the CSS file's folder, not the page's. And web paths always use forward slashes and are case-sensitive once they're online. Finally, make the link text say where it goes. \"Read the Mesa Trail guide\" tells a screen reader user, who may be skimming a list of every link on the page, exactly what they'll get. \"Click here\" tells them nothing. Watch out A path like C:\\Users\\you\\Desktop\\mesa.jpg or file:///… points at your own computer. It works for you and for nobody else, so the image breaks as soon as the site is online. Play"},{"id":"sec-find-the-path","title":"Find the path","kind":"Play","text":"Pick the file you're writing in, click the file you want, and see the path. Then try writing one yourself. I'm writing code in index.html projects/trail-app.html projects/notes/week1.html css/style.css Click a file to link to it Type the path you'd write Show the answer How to read it Reference"},{"id":"sec-kinds-of-links","title":"Kinds of links","kind":"Reference","text":"Relative path images/logo.png../about.html Starts from the current file's folder. Use this for files in your own site. Absolute URL https://developer.mozilla.org The full address. Use it for other websites. Root-relative /images/logo.png Starts at the top of the domain. Breaks on GitHub Pages project sites and when opening files directly, so avoid it in this class. Same-page anchor #contact Jumps to the element with id=\"contact\" on this page. Email mailto:you@example.com Opens the visitor's email app, if they have one set up. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-paths","title":"Fix the paths","kind":"Practice","text":"This page lives in a subfolder, so each link needs a path that starts from there."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"../ means \"go up one folder.\" Use it once per level you need to climb. A path in a CSS file, like url(...), starts from the CSS file's folder, not the HTML page's. Link text should say where the link goes. \"Read the trail guide\" beats \"click here.\" Opening a link in a new tab with target=\"_blank\" is fine for outside sites, but don't do it for every link."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Wrong capitalization: Trail-Photo.jpg isn't Trail-Photo.JPG. Your laptop may forgive it, but web servers usually won't. Spaces in file names, which turn into %20 and break easily. Use dashes. Paths from your own computer, like C:\\Users\\... or file:///. They only work on your machine. Backslashes. Web paths always use forward slashes /. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["links-paths/quiz"],"exercises":["links-paths/ex-paths"]},{"slug":"tables","num":"1.9","title":"Tables","desc":"Header cells, spanning cells and footers for real data, and when not to use a table.","props":["<th>","colspan","scope"],"intro":"Tables are for data with rows and columns, like a schedule or a comparison. Build up this table one feature at a time, and click any cell to hear what a screen reader says.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-tables-are-for-data","title":"Tables are for data","kind":"Read","text":"A table is the right element when your data has rows and columns that both mean something, like a hike schedule or a comparison of trails. Each row is a <tr>, and each row holds cells: <th> for headers, <td> for data. <table> <caption>Boulder-area trails</caption> <thead> <tr><th scope=\"col\">Trail</th><th scope=\"col\">Miles</th></tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr><th scope=\"row\">Mesa</th><td>6.7</td></tr> </tbody> </table> Every piece of that markup helps someone. The <caption> is the table's title. <thead> and <tbody> group the header row and the data. And scope tells a screen reader which cells a header labels. Without it, a listener moving through the table hears \"6.7\" and has to remember which trail and which column that was. With it, they hear \"Mesa, Miles: 6.7\". Sometimes a cell needs to stretch: colspan=\"2\" makes it two columns wide, and rowspan=\"2\" makes it two rows tall. Every row still has to add up to the same number of columns, or the cells after it shift out of line. Tables used to be how whole pages were laid out, because there was nothing better. Now there is. If your \"rows and columns\" are really a grid of cards or a page layout, use Flexbox or CSS Grid, and keep tables for data. Tip Wide tables don't fit on phones. Wrap the table in a <div> with overflow-x: auto so it scrolls sideways instead of stretching the whole page. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-table-one-feature-at-a-time","title":"Build a table one feature at a time","kind":"Play","text":"Turn on each feature, and click any cell to hear what a screen reader says about it. HTML Header cells use <th scope> Add a <caption> CSS border-collapse (merge double borders) padding in cells Zebra stripes with :nth-child(even) Right-align numbers Turn everything on Click a cell in the table. Play"},{"id":"sec-cells-that-span-footers-and-column-groups","title":"Cells that span, footers and column groups","kind":"Play","text":"Some cells need to stretch across columns or down rows. Turn each feature on to see the problem it solves. rowspan=\"2\" on the long hike colspan=\"2\" on the closure Add a <tfoot> <colgroup> to highlight Sunday Practice"},{"id":"sec-table-or-not","title":"Table or not?","kind":"Practice","text":"Tables used to be used for page layout. Now they're only for data. Decide for each one. Practice"},{"id":"sec-structure-a-table","title":"Structure a table","kind":"Practice","text":"This table has the right data but no structure, so screen readers can't tell headers from data."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Structure: <table> holds rows (<tr>), and rows hold cells (<td> for data, <th> for headers). Group rows with <thead> and <tbody>. scope=\"col\" or scope=\"row\" tells screen readers which cells a header labels. colspan and rowspan merge cells. Every row must still add up to the same number of columns. Wrap wide tables in a container with overflow-x: auto so they scroll on phones instead of breaking the page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using a table to lay out a page or a grid of cards. Use Flexbox or Grid. Making header cells bold <td>s instead of real <th>s. A different number of cells in each row, which shifts columns out of line. Putting a border on the table only and expecting the cells to get one too."}],"quizzes":["tables/quiz"],"exercises":["tables/ex-table"]},{"slug":"forms","num":"1.10","title":"Forms & Inputs","desc":"Labels, input types, fieldsets, validation rules, and where the data goes.","props":["<label>","<fieldset>","method"],"intro":"Picking the right input type gets you validation, phone keyboards and date pickers for free. Connecting each label to its input makes the form usable for everyone.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-ask-for-information-the-right-way","title":"Ask for information the right way","kind":"Read","text":"A form is a conversation with your visitor, and HTML does a surprising amount of the work for you if you choose the right pieces. Start with the input type. type=\"email\" gives phones a keyboard with an @ key and checks the address before sending. type=\"date\" brings up a date picker. type=\"number\" shows a number pad. Use type=\"text\" for everything and you get none of that. Every input needs a label, connected to it by matching the label's for to the input's id: <label for=\"email\">Email</label> <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required> That connection matters more than it looks. Clicking the label focuses the input, which gives everyone a bigger target, and a screen reader reads the label aloud when someone reaches the field. A placeholder is not a label: it disappears as soon as you type, leaving people guessing what the field was for. For a group of choices, like radio buttons for trail difficulty, wrap them in a <fieldset> and put the question in its <legend>. Radio buttons in a group share the same name, which is how the browser knows only one can be picked. When the form is sent, each field travels as name=value. The form's action says where to send it, and method says how: get puts it in the URL, post sends it out of sight. A field without a name isn't sent at all. Note Attributes like required and min make the browser check answers before sending. That helps visitors, but it doesn't protect a server: anyone can bypass it. Real sites check again on the server. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-each-input-type","title":"Try each input type","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a type and see the keyboard, picker and validation you get for free. Pick an input type What you get for free Labels"},{"id":"sec-click-each-label","title":"Click each label","kind":"Labels","text":"A label connected with for and id moves focus to its input when clicked. Screen readers also read it aloud when the input is focused. Connected Your name Email me updates Click the label text, not the box. Not connected Your name Email me updates Click the label text, not the box. Validation"},{"id":"sec-let-the-browser-check-it","title":"Let the browser check it","kind":"Validation","text":"Attributes like required, type=\"email\" and min make the browser block bad submissions before your code runs. Try submitting this with empty or wrong values. Name * Email * Age (13 to 120) Sign up Grouping"},{"id":"sec-fieldset-and-legend","title":"fieldset and legend","kind":"Grouping","text":"A group of radio buttons or checkboxes needs a question. Wrap the group in a <fieldset> and put the question in its <legend>. Focus a radio button in each version to hear the difference. With fieldset and legend Trail difficulty Easy Moderate Hard Focus a radio button. Without Trail difficulty Easy Moderate Hard Focus a radio button. More controls"},{"id":"sec-datalist-progress-and-meter","title":"datalist, progress and meter","kind":"More controls","text":"<datalist> Trail name Suggestions appear as you type, but people can still enter anything. <progress> Upload progress 40% Shows how far a task has got. Leave out value for an \"in progress, unknown amount\" bar. <meter> Trail difficulty: 5 / 10 A measurement within a known range. low, high and optimum make the browser color it good, okay or bad. Validation attributes"},{"id":"sec-set-the-rules-let-the-browser-explain-them","title":"Set the rules, let the browser explain them","kind":"Validation attributes","text":"Type in each field. The messages below come straight from your browser. Username required minlength=\"3\" maxlength=\"15\" ZIP code pattern=\"[0-9]{5}\" Group size type=\"number\" min=\"1\" max=\"12\" Sending data"},{"id":"sec-where-does-the-data-go","title":"Where does the data go?","kind":"Sending data","text":"A form's action says where to send the data and method says how. Each field is sent as name=value, so a field with no name is left out. Fill this in and watch the request it would make. Nothing is actually sent from this page. method=\"get\" method=\"post\" Trail name=\"trail\" Mesa Trail Royal Arch Date name=\"date\" Group size name=\"size\" Notes (no name attribute) The request Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-the-form-usable","title":"Make the form usable","kind":"Practice","text":"This sign-up form works with a mouse, barely. Label it, name it, and group it so it works for everyone."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Every input needs a label. The label's for must match the input's id exactly. The name attribute is what the data is called when the form is sent. Radio buttons in the same group share one name, so only one can be picked. Wrap the group in a <fieldset> with a <legend>. Use <button type=\"submit\"> to send a form and type=\"button\" for anything else."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using placeholder instead of a label. It disappears when you type and is often low-contrast. Typing type=\"text\" for everything and missing the free validation and keyboards. Reusing the same id twice, so a label points at the wrong input. Forgetting that browser validation doesn't protect a server. Real apps check on the server too. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["forms/quiz"],"exercises":["forms/ex-form"]},{"slug":"images","num":"1.11","title":"Images & Alt Text","desc":"Adding images, writing useful alt text, and captioning with figure and figcaption.","props":["<img>","alt","<figure>"],"intro":"The alt attribute is what people hear when they can't see an image. Pick alt text for each image in this article, then switch to the screen reader view to hear the result.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-every-image-needs-a-text-version","title":"Every image needs a text version","kind":"Read","text":"Adding an image takes one element: <img>. It's an empty element, so there's no closing tag, and everything goes in attributes: <img src=\"photos/royal-arch.jpg\" alt=\"Hikers standing under the sandstone arch at the top of the trail\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\"> src is the path to the file. width and height tell the browser the image's shape before it downloads, so the page doesn't jump around while it loads. CSS can still resize it. The most important attribute is alt. Alt text is what a screen reader reads aloud, what search engines index, and what appears if the image fails to load. Good alt text describes what the image means on this page, not every detail. Ask yourself: if I were reading this page aloud to a friend, what would I say about this picture? A photo in a trail report: describe what it shows that matters to the report. An image inside a link, like a logo linking home: describe where the link goes, \"Boulder Trail Club home\". A purely decorative image, like a background flourish: use an empty alt=\"\" so screen readers skip it. Don't leave alt out, or many screen readers read the file name instead. When an image needs a visible caption, wrap it in <figure> and put the caption in <figcaption>. The browser then knows the two belong together. Tip Don't start alt text with \"Image of\" or \"Photo of\". Screen readers already announce that it's an image, so the words just slow people down. Play"},{"id":"sec-write-alt-text-for-an-article","title":"Write alt text for an article","kind":"Play","text":"Choose alt text for each image, then switch to the screen reader view to hear the result. Choose alt text What you see What a screen reader says Image failed to load Captions"},{"id":"sec-figure-and-figcaption","title":"figure and figcaption","kind":"Captions","text":"When an image needs a visible caption, wrap the image and its caption in a <figure> and put the caption in <figcaption>. That ties them together for screen readers and for CSS. img + p figure + figcaption The markup What a screen reader says alt text vs caption alt replaces the image for people who can't see it. A caption is visible to everyone and adds information, like who took the photo or when. Don't repeat the same words in both: a screen reader would read them twice. Practice"},{"id":"sec-write-useful-alt-text","title":"Write useful alt text","kind":"Practice","text":"Each image needs alt text that fits its job on the page, and the photo needs a visible caption."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Describe what the image means in context, not every detail. Ask: what would I say if I read this page aloud? Purely decorative images get an empty alt=\"\" so screen readers skip them. Don't leave alt out entirely. For an image inside a link, describe where the link goes (\"Trailhead home\"), not the picture. For charts, give the main takeaway in the alt and put the full data in nearby text or a table. Use <figure> and <figcaption> when an image has a visible caption. Always set width and height attributes so the page doesn't jump around while images load."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Starting with \"Image of…\". Screen readers already announce that it's an image. Using the file name, like IMG_4031.jpg, as the alt text. Missing alt completely. Many screen readers then read out the file name. Wrong paths: src=\"images/photo.JPG\" won't load photo.jpg on most servers, because file names are case-sensitive. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["images/quiz"],"exercises":["images/ex-alt"]},{"slug":"image-formats","num":"1.12","title":"Image Formats","desc":"JPG vs PNG vs SVG vs WebP, real file sizes, and resizing before you upload.","props":["JPG","SVG","WebP"],"intro":"The same picture can be a 30 KB file or a 900 KB file depending on its format. Photos, logos and screenshots each have a format that suits them best.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-right-format-for-the-job","title":"The right format for the job","kind":"Read","text":"The same photo can be a 60 KB file or a 6 MB file depending on how you save it, and that difference decides whether your page loads in a blink or makes a visitor on a phone wait. Four formats cover nearly everything: JPG for photos. It's lossy: it throws away detail your eye won't miss, which makes files small. Save it too low and you get smudgy blocks. PNG for screenshots and images with sharp edges or see-through backgrounds. It's lossless, so nothing is thrown away, but photos saved as PNG are huge. SVG for logos and icons. It isn't pixels at all, but shapes described in code, so it stays sharp at any size and is often just a few KB. WebP does the jobs of both JPG and PNG, usually in a smaller file. Every modern browser supports it. The format is only half of it. A photo straight off a phone is often 4000 pixels wide. If it shows 800 pixels wide on your page, you're making every visitor download five times more than they can see. Before uploading: Resize to about twice the width it appears on screen, so it stays sharp on high-density screens. Compress it. A free tool like Squoosh lets you compare quality settings side by side. Aim for under 200 KB for a large photo, and a few KB for icons and logos. Watch out Renaming photo.png to photo.jpg changes the name, not the format. To change format, open the image and export it again. Play"},{"id":"sec-same-image-different-files","title":"Same image, different files","kind":"Play","text":"Your browser is really encoding these images right now, so the file sizes are real. Pick a kind of image, then compare the formats. The zoom shows the damage compression does. kind of image photo logo screenshot quality (JPG and WebP) PNG is lossless: it keeps every pixel exactly. JPG and WebP are lossy: they throw away detail you probably won't notice, and lower quality throws away more. file size · 400 × 250 px Full image Zoomed in 4× Cheat sheet"},{"id":"sec-which-format-when","title":"Which format, when","kind":"Cheat sheet","text":"JPG .jpg · lossy Use for photos. Avoid for logos, text and anything needing a transparent background. JPG has no transparency. PNG .png · lossless Use for screenshots, flat graphics with text, and images with transparent backgrounds. Avoid for photos. They come out 5–10× bigger than a JPG. WebP .webp · either Use for almost anything. Usually 25–35% smaller than JPG or PNG at the same quality, and supports transparency. Note Every current browser supports it. Some older desktop apps can't open it. AVIF .avif · either Use for photos when you want the smallest file. Often half the size of a JPG. Note Slower to export, and not every editor can save it yet. Offer a JPG fallback with <picture>. SVG .svg · vector Use for logos, icons and simple illustrations. It's math, not pixels, so it stays sharp at any size and is often tiny. Avoid for photos. They can't be drawn as shapes. GIF .gif · old Use for almost nothing now. Only 256 colors. Instead for an animation, use a short muted MP4 in a <video>. It's often 10× smaller. Play"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-format","title":"Pick the format","kind":"Play","text":"Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-right-format","title":"Pick the right format","kind":"Practice","text":"Each image has been exported in the wrong format. Pick the one that suits what's in it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Resize before you upload. Export about twice the width the image appears on screen, so it stays sharp on high-density screens. A photo shown 600px wide needs about 1200px, not the 4000px from your phone. Then compress it. Free tools like Squoosh (squoosh.app) and ImageOptim let you compare quality settings side by side. Quality 70–80 is usually indistinguishable from 100. A good target for a large photo is under 200 KB. Most icons and logos should be a few KB. An SVG can go in an <img> like any other image, or be pasted into the HTML directly so CSS can recolor it."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Saving a logo as JPG, which leaves blurry smudges around the letters and a white box instead of transparency. Saving a photo as PNG \"for quality,\" and getting a file several times bigger with no visible difference. Renaming photo.png to photo.jpg. That changes the name, not the format. Re-export it instead. Re-saving the same JPG over and over. Each save loses a little more detail. Keep an original and export copies."}],"quizzes":["image-formats/quiz"],"exercises":["image-formats/ex-formats"]},{"slug":"video-audio","num":"1.13","title":"Video & Audio","desc":"Player attributes, why autoplay gets blocked, and adding captions.","props":["<video>","controls","<track>"],"intro":"The <video> and <audio> elements play media without any plugins. Their attributes control what visitors see and whether anything plays by itself. This player is a simulation that follows the same rules as a real one.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-media-without-plugins","title":"Media without plugins","kind":"Read","text":"Browsers play video and audio on their own, with two elements that work almost the same way: <video controls poster=\"mesa-cover.jpg\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\"> <source src=\"mesa.webm\" type=\"video/webm\"> <source src=\"mesa.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"> <track kind=\"captions\" src=\"mesa.vtt\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English\"> </video> controls adds the play button, timeline and volume. Leave it out and visitors can't pause or mute, so always include it unless you build your own. poster shows an image until the video starts, instead of a black box. And listing more than one <source> lets the browser play the first format it supports. You've probably noticed videos that start silently, or not at all. Browsers block autoplay with sound, because nobody wants a page to start talking at them. Autoplay only works when the video is also muted, and even then, use it sparingly. A background video that loops forever with no way to stop it is distracting, and a problem for people with motion sensitivity. Captions live in a separate text file in WebVTT format, linked with <track>. They're essential for people who can't hear the audio, and useful for everyone watching on mute in a quiet room. For audio-only content, like a podcast, provide a transcript. <audio controls> works the same way, without the picture. Tip Video files are big. Compress them before uploading, or host long videos on YouTube or Vimeo and embed them instead. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-each-attribute","title":"Try each attribute","kind":"Play","text":"Turn attributes on and off and watch how the player behaves. It follows the same rules as a real browser. <video> attributes controls poster=\"cover.jpg\" autoplay muted loop Child elements <track kind=\"captions\"> Reload the page Captions"},{"id":"sec-the-captions-file","title":"The captions file","kind":"Captions","text":"Captions live in a separate WebVTT text file, linked with <track>. Each cue has a start time, an end time and the text. The current cue is highlighted while the video plays. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-media-players","title":"Fix the media players","kind":"Practice","text":"This video starts blaring on its own and can't be paused. Make it polite and accessible."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Always include controls unless you build your own play button. Without it, people can't pause or adjust volume. Browsers block autoplay with sound. Autoplay only works with muted, and should be rare. List more than one <source> (like MP4 and WebM) and the browser plays the first one it supports. Add captions for videos and a transcript for audio, for people who can't hear it and everyone watching on mute. <audio controls> works the same way, without the picture."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Uploading a huge video file straight from a phone. Compress it, or host it on YouTube or Vimeo and embed it. Expecting autoplay to work without muted. No poster, so the player shows a black box until it loads. Background videos that loop forever with no way to pause them. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["video-audio/quiz"],"exercises":["video-audio/ex-media"]},{"slug":"embeds","num":"1.14","title":"Embeds & iframes","desc":"Put YouTube videos and Google Maps on your page, and make them fit on phones.","props":["<iframe>","title","aspect-ratio"],"intro":"An <iframe> is a window in your page that shows another web page. It's how you put a YouTube video, a Google Map or a CodePen demo on your site without hosting it yourself.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-a-window-into-another-page","title":"A window into another page","kind":"Read","text":"An iframe puts a whole separate web page inside yours. It's how you show a YouTube video, a Google Map or a CodePen demo without hosting any of it. The service gives you the code to embed, usually from a Share or Embed button: <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID\" title=\"Hiking the Mesa Trail, a 3-minute tour\" loading=\"lazy\"></iframe> Three attributes are worth adding to whatever you paste: title says what's inside. A screen reader announces it, so people can decide whether to go in. loading=\"lazy\" waits until the visitor scrolls near it. A YouTube player downloads close to 1 MB before anyone presses play, so this matters. Responsive sizing. Embed code usually arrives with width=\"560\" height=\"315\", which sticks out of the page on a phone. Instead, set it in CSS: iframe { width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; } Because an iframe holds a separate document, your CSS can't reach inside it, and its CSS can't leak out. You can size and position the frame, but you can't restyle the video player or the map within it. Watch out The src must be the embed address, like youtube.com/embed/…, not the page you'd visit, like youtube.com/watch?v=…. The ordinary page refuses to load inside a frame. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-an-embed","title":"Build an embed","kind":"Play","text":"Sites hand you embed code with a fixed width and height. Make the page narrow like a phone, then fix the embed so it fits and is accessible. embed YouTube video Google Map CodePen demo page width fix it title=\"…\" responsive CSS loading=\"lazy\" The embeds here are pictures of the real thing, so this page works offline. Hike of the week: Royal Arch Copy Play"},{"id":"sec-a-page-inside-a-page","title":"A page inside a page","kind":"Play","text":"An iframe holds a completely separate document. Your CSS doesn't reach inside it, and its CSS doesn't leak out. Style the outer page and watch. your page Trail Club This paragraph is on your page. Embedded page This paragraph lives in a different document, inside the iframe. \"> Apply p, h3 { color: red; text-transform: uppercase } Click the button to add a rule to your page's stylesheet. This is why you can't restyle a YouTube player with your own CSS. You can only size and position the iframe box itself. Play"},{"id":"sec-where-to-get-the-code","title":"Where to get the code","kind":"Play","text":"YouTube Open the video and click Share. Choose Embed and copy the code. Paste it into your HTML, then add a title and the responsive CSS. Google Maps Search for the place and click Share. Pick the Embed a map tab and copy the HTML. It already has loading=\"lazy\". Change the title and remove the fixed width. When embedding is refused Many sites, including most banks, social sites and Google Search, tell browsers not to show them inside iframes. You'll see a blank box or \"refused to connect.\" Link to them instead. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-the-embed-responsive","title":"Make the embed responsive","kind":"Practice","text":"This map is a fixed 600 by 450 box. Make it accessible, lazy, and fit any screen."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Give every iframe a title that says what's in it. Screen readers announce it so people can decide whether to go in. To make an embed responsive, set width: 100%, height: auto and an aspect-ratio that matches the content, like 16 / 9 for video. loading=\"lazy\" waits until the visitor scrolls near the embed. A YouTube player downloads close to 1 MB before anyone presses play. Each embed is a full web page with its own scripts and tracking. Use them where they add real value."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Pasting the code with width=\"560\" and never testing on a phone, so the video sticks out of the page. Pasting the page address (youtube.com/watch?v=…) as the src. It must be the embed address (youtube.com/embed/…). Trying to style what's inside the iframe with your own CSS. Embedding a video you could simply host yourself with <video>, or vice versa: uploading a huge video file to GitHub Pages instead of YouTube. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["embeds/quiz"],"exercises":["embeds/ex-embed"]},{"slug":"review-html","num":"1.15","title":"Review: HTML Foundations","desc":"Build a trail page from plain text. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole part together. Skim the recap, build a complete trail page from plain text, then test yourself with questions that mix every lesson.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part was about structure and meaning. Before any styling, HTML says what each piece of a page is, and browsers, search engines and screen readers all rely on that. How the Web Works: The browser requests files from a server by URL, and the server answers with a status code. HTML Syntax: Elements nest like boxes, and attribute values always go in quotes. Document Structure: Every page has a doctype, then a head for information and a body for content. The Head & Metadata: The title, description and Open Graph tags decide how a page appears in tabs, searches and shared links. Text Semantics: One h1, headings in order, and strong or em for meaning rather than looks. Lists: ul when order doesn't matter, ol when it does, dl for terms and descriptions. Semantic Elements: header, nav, main, article and footer name the parts of a page. Links & Paths: Relative paths start from the current file, and ../ climbs one folder. Tables: Tables are for data, with th and scope to label rows and columns. Forms & Inputs: Every input needs a label, a name and the right type. Images & Alt Text: Alt text describes what an image means in context, and decoration gets alt=\"\". Image Formats: JPEG or WebP for photos, SVG for logos, PNG for screenshots, and resize before you upload. Video & Audio: Players need controls, several sources, and captions. Embeds & iframes: An iframe needs a title, lazy loading, and an aspect-ratio to fit phones. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-a-trail-page","title":"Mini project: a trail page","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn plain text into a complete, meaningful page. Every check is something you learned in this part. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-html/quiz"],"exercises":["review-html/ex-project"]},{"slug":"css-basics","num":"2.1","title":"How CSS Is Applied","desc":"The parts of a rule, and external vs internal vs inline styles.","props":["selector","declaration","<link>"],"intro":"A CSS rule picks some elements and gives them styles. Learn the names for each part of a rule, then see the three places CSS can live and which one wins when they disagree.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-rules-and-where-they-live","title":"Rules, and where they live","kind":"Read","text":"HTML says what things are. CSS says how they look. A stylesheet is a list of rules, and every rule has the same shape: h1 { color: #0b6b5e; font-size: 2rem; } The selector, h1, picks which elements the rule applies to. Inside the braces are declarations, each a property and a value separated by a colon and ending with a semicolon. This rule says: make every h1 teal and twice the normal text size. CSS can live in three places: An external stylesheet, a separate .css file linked from the head with <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">. One file can style every page of a site, so changing a color once changes it everywhere. A <style> element in the head, which styles only that one page. An inline style, a style=\"\" attribute on a single element. It's handy for quick tests, but it's hard to find later and it beats your stylesheet, which makes it hard to override. For real projects, use an external stylesheet. When the same element gets styles from more than one place, the browser has rules for deciding which wins, which you'll explore in the playground and in Cascade & Inheritance. Tip If none of your styles show up, the stylesheet probably didn't load. Open DevTools, check the Network tab for a red style.css, and compare the href with the file's real name and folder. Play"},{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-a-rule","title":"Anatomy of a rule","kind":"Play","text":"Click any part of the rule, or pick a name below. Play"},{"id":"sec-three-places-to-put-css","title":"Three places to put CSS","kind":"Play","text":"All three below try to color the same heading. Turn them on and off and swap the order of the two in the head. In the <head>, the Swap order Trail Report Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-stylesheet","title":"Fix the stylesheet","kind":"Practice","text":"One missing character can wipe out a whole rule. Find the three mistakes."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Use an external stylesheet for real projects. One file can style every page of the site. Each declaration ends with a semicolon. The last one technically doesn't need it, but always add it. Comments look like /* like this */. Use them to label sections of your stylesheet. CSS ignores extra spaces and line breaks, so format rules one declaration per line for readability."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A <link> with the wrong path, so no styles load at all. Check the Network tab in DevTools. Writing CSS inside the <body> without a <style> tag, so it shows up as text on the page. Using inline style=\"\" everywhere, which is hard to change and beats your stylesheet. HTML-style comments (<!-- -->) inside a CSS file. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["css-basics/quiz"],"exercises":["css-basics/ex-rules"]},{"slug":"selectors","num":"2.2","title":"Selectors & Specificity","desc":"Classes, IDs, combinators and attribute selectors, and why one rule beats another.","props":[".class","h2 + p","[href]"],"intro":"A selector picks which elements a rule applies to. Type one below and the matching elements light up. When two rules disagree, specificity decides which one wins.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-picking-the-right-elements","title":"Picking the right elements","kind":"Read","text":"A selector is how a rule says which elements it styles. Three kinds do most of the work: A type selector like p picks every paragraph. A class selector like .card picks every element with class=\"card\". Classes are reusable, so they're what you'll use most. An ID selector like #top picks the one element with id=\"top\". You can combine them. A combinator describes how elements relate: .card .title { } /* a .title anywhere inside a .card */ .card > h2 { } /* an h2 that's a direct child of .card */ h2 + p { } /* the p right after an h2 */ .tag.hard { } /* one element with both classes */ Look closely at the first and last lines. A space means \"inside\", and no space means \"both at once\". That one space is behind a lot of confusing bugs. An attribute selector, like a[href$=\".pdf\"], picks elements by their attributes: here, links to PDF files. When two rules set the same property on the same element, specificity decides which wins. The browser scores each selector by counting IDs, then classes, then element types, and compares column by column. One ID outranks any number of classes, and one class outranks any number of types. Only if the scores tie does the rule written later win. Tip Style with classes and keep selectors short. IDs and long chains score so high that the next time you want to change something, you'll need an even stronger selector to beat them. Play"},{"id":"sec-type-a-selector","title":"Type a selector","kind":"Play","text":"Type any selector and the matching elements light up. Try the suggestions, then write your own. Selector Try these Mesa Trail 6.7 miles Moderate Water Bear spray Map Details PDF map Royal Arch 3.4 miles Hard Details Trail data updated Tuesday Reference"},{"id":"sec-combinators-and-attribute-selectors","title":"Combinators and attribute selectors","kind":"Reference","text":"Each of these is in the \"Try these\" list above. Combinators: how elements relate .card p space Descendant: a p anywhere inside .card .card > a > Child: an a directly inside .card h2 + p + Adjacent sibling: the p right after an h2 h2 ~ a ~ General sibling: any a after an h2, same parent Attribute selectors [href] has the attribute [type=\"email\"] exactly equals [href^=\"https\"] starts with [href$=\".pdf\"] ends with [class*=\"card\"] contains anywhere Specificity"},{"id":"sec-which-rule-wins","title":"Which rule wins?","kind":"Specificity","text":"Both rules below try to color the same heading. The browser compares their scores column by column, from left to right. If the scores tie, the rule written later wins. Rule A (written first) Rule B (written second) Mesa Trail Practice"},{"id":"sec-write-the-selectors","title":"Write the selectors","kind":"Practice","text":"The declarations are done. Write a selector for each rule so it matches only what the comment describes."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"A space means \"inside, at any depth.\" > means \"a direct child only.\" No space between parts means \"both at once\": .tag.hard is an element with both classes. A comma makes a list: h2, .btn matches either one. + and ~ only look forward at later siblings, never back up or into children. Score order: IDs beat classes, classes beat elements. One ID outranks any number of classes. Inline style=\"\" beats all selectors, and !important beats that. Avoid both when you can."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting the dot: card looks for a <card> element, not class=\"card\". Writing .card.title when you meant .card .title. One space changes everything. Styling with IDs everywhere, then needing even stronger selectors to override them. Reaching for !important instead of finding out why a rule is losing. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["selectors/quiz"],"exercises":["selectors/ex-select"]},{"slug":"pseudo-classes","num":"2.3","title":"Pseudo-classes & States","desc":"Hover, focus and link states, nth-child, and building a nav menu.","props":[":hover",":focus-visible",":nth-child"],"intro":"A pseudo-class, written with one colon, selects an element when it's in a certain state or position. Hover, click, tab and type on the controls below and watch which states switch on.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-styling-states-and-positions","title":"Styling states and positions","kind":"Read","text":"A pseudo-class selects an element when it's in a certain state, or in a certain position. It's written with one colon after a normal selector: .btn:hover, .btn:focus-visible { background: #1a2fa8; } li:nth-child(even) { background: #f2f4fa; } State pseudo-classes respond to what the visitor is doing. :hover applies while the mouse is over an element, :active while it's being pressed, and :focus when it's ready for keyboard input. Forms add more, like :checked and :invalid. Notice that the first rule styles :hover and :focus-visible together. Many people use a keyboard instead of a mouse, pressing Tab to move between links and buttons. :focus-visible shows them where they are, the way hover feedback shows a mouse user. Style the two together and nobody misses out. And since phones have no hover at all, never hide anything important behind :hover alone. Position pseudo-classes pick elements by where they sit among their siblings. :first-child and :last-child do what they say. :nth-child() takes a pattern: odd, even, a number, or a formula like 3n for every third item. Links have four states, and their order in your stylesheet matters because later rules win: :link, :visited, :hover, :active. A handy way to remember it is \"LoVe HAte\". Watch out Never remove the focus outline with outline: none unless you add a clear :focus-visible style in its place. Without it, keyboard users can't see where they are on the page. Play"},{"id":"sec-interactive-states","title":"Interactive states","kind":"Play","text":"button Save trail button disabled Sold out aView the trail map input type=\"email\" required input type=\"checkbox\" Dogs allowed Point at or tab to a control Try clicking the button with the mouse, then reaching it with the Tab key. Only the keyboard shows the :focus-visible ring. Play"},{"id":"sec-position-nth-child","title":"Position: :nth-child()","kind":"Play","text":"The formula an+b is calculated for n = 0, 1, 2, 3... and every result picks an item. li:nth-child( ) Play"},{"id":"sec-styling-links-and-building-a-menu","title":"Styling links and building a menu","kind":"Play","text":"Links have four states. Write them in this order, or later ones get overridden: :link, :visited, :hover, :active. A common memory trick is \"LoVe HAte.\" Then add :focus-visible for keyboard users. Build up a menu one step at a time below. Remove bullets and indent Put the links in a row (flex) Make each link a padded block :hover and :focus-visible styles Mark the current page with aria-current Turn everything on Home Trails Maps Gear Practice"},{"id":"sec-style-the-states","title":"Style the states","kind":"Practice","text":"Stripes, hover feedback and a first-item highlight, all without adding a single class."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Pseudo-classes use one colon (:hover). Pseudo-elements like ::before use two. Style :hover and :focus-visible together so keyboard users get the same feedback as mouse users. Phones have no hover. Never hide something important behind :hover alone. :nth-child(odd) and (even) are great for striped tables and lists. For security, browsers only let :visited change colors, nothing else."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Removing focus outlines with outline: none and adding nothing back, so keyboard users get lost. A space before the colon: a :hover means \"any hovered element inside an a.\" Expecting p:first-child to pick the first p. It picks a p only if it's the very first child of its parent. Showing :invalid styles before the user has typed anything. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["pseudo-classes/quiz"],"exercises":["pseudo-classes/ex-states"]},{"slug":"pseudo-elements","num":"2.4","title":"Pseudo-elements","desc":"Decorate with ::before and ::after, drop caps, placeholders and selections.","props":["::before","content","::first-letter"],"intro":"Pseudo-elements, written with two colons, style a part of an element, or add decorative content before or after it, without changing your HTML. Switch each one on and off to see what it adds.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-style-a-part-or-add-a-decoration","title":"Style a part, or add a decoration","kind":"Read","text":"A pseudo-element lets you style a part of an element, or add decoration to it, without touching your HTML. It's written with two colons, which is how you tell it apart from a pseudo-class like :hover. The two you'll use most are ::before and ::after. They insert a box at the start or end of an element's content: .quote::before { content: \"“\"; font-size: 3rem; color: #b4410c; } Every ::before and ::after needs a content property, or it doesn't appear at all. Often the content is empty, content: \"\", and the pseudo-element is just a box you style into an underline, an arrow or a colored bar. Others select a part of what's already there: ::first-letter styles the first letter, for a drop cap. ::first-line styles the first line, however long it ends up being. ::marker styles a list item's bullet or number. ::selection styles text the visitor highlights. ::placeholder styles the hint text in an input. Pseudo-elements are for decoration. Text in content can't be selected or copied, and screen readers don't treat it consistently, so anything visitors need to read belongs in the HTML. Note ::before and ::after go inside the element, as its first and last child. That's why they don't work on <img> or <input>: those elements have no inside to put them in. Play"},{"id":"sec-switch-each-one-on","title":"Switch each one on","kind":"Play","text":"Turn each pseudo-element on and off to see exactly what it adds. Practice"},{"id":"sec-decorate-with-before-and-after","title":"Decorate with ::before and ::after","kind":"Practice","text":"Add three decorations with CSS alone. None of them belongs in the HTML."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"::before and ::after need a content property, even if it's empty (content: \"\"), or they don't appear. They're inserted inside the element, as its first and last child. Use them for decoration: quote marks, icons, underlines, arrows. Don't put important information in them. Two colons mark a pseudo-element (::before). One colon is a pseudo-class (:hover)."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting content, then wondering why ::after does nothing. Trying ::before on an <img> or <input>. Elements with no content inside can't have them. Putting real text in content. It can't be selected and some screen readers skip it. Expecting ::first-letter to work on an inline element like a span. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["pseudo-elements/quiz"],"exercises":["pseudo-elements/ex-pseudo"]},{"slug":"cascade","num":"2.5","title":"Cascade & Inheritance","desc":"Which styles pass down to children, and how the browser settles conflicts.","props":["inherit","!important","order"],"intro":"Some styles pass down from parent to child automatically, and some don't. When several rules disagree, the cascade decides which one wins.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-passing-styles-down-and-settling-arguments","title":"Passing styles down, and settling arguments","kind":"Read","text":"Two systems decide the final style of every element. Inheritance passes styles down from parent to child. The cascade settles arguments when several rules disagree. Inheritance is why you can set a font once and have the whole page use it: body { font-family: \"Atkinson Hyperlegible\", sans-serif; color: #161a2b; line-height: 1.6; } Every element inside body inherits those, because text properties inherit: color, font-*, line-height, text-align. Box properties don't. A border on a <section> doesn't put a border on every paragraph inside it, and neither do padding, margin, background or width. If a child looks like it inherited a background, it's actually transparent, and you're seeing the parent behind it. When rules disagree about the same property on the same element, the cascade checks, in order: Importance: a declaration marked !important beats normal ones. Specificity: IDs beat classes, classes beat element types. Source order: if everything else ties, the rule that comes later wins. That last rule includes the order of your <link> tags. Link a general stylesheet after a specific one, and the general one can win. Tip Buttons and inputs don't inherit your font and color. Add button, input, select, textarea { font: inherit; color: inherit; } near the top of your stylesheet, and forms match the rest of the page. Play"},{"id":"sec-what-gets-inherited","title":"What gets inherited?","kind":"Play","text":"Set a property on the article. The badges read what the browser actually computed for each child. Set on article button, input { font: inherit; color: inherit } article h3 Mesa Trail p Rolling foothills with views of the Flatirons. aTrail map button Save trail input Play"},{"id":"sec-which-rule-wins","title":"Which rule wins?","kind":"Play","text":"Three rules all try to color the same heading, <h3 class=\"title\" id=\"top\">. Change the selectors, reorder the rules, or add !important. Mesa Trail The browser checks in this order !important beats everything without it. Then higher specificity wins (IDs, then classes, then elements). If it's still a tie, the rule that comes later in the CSS wins. Practice"},{"id":"sec-let-it-inherit","title":"Let it inherit","kind":"Practice","text":"This stylesheet sets the same font and color three times. Set them once and let inheritance do the rest."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Text properties inherit: color, font-*, line-height, text-align. Box properties don't: border, padding, margin, background, width. Set your fonts and text color once on body and let everything inherit them. Any property can be forced to inherit with the value inherit."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Wondering why buttons and inputs ignore your font and color. They don't inherit them unless you add font: inherit and color: inherit. Thinking a background was inherited. Children are transparent, so you're seeing the parent through them. Fixing a losing rule with !important instead of checking order and specificity in DevTools. Linking two stylesheets in the wrong order, so the general one overrides the specific one. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["cascade/quiz"],"exercises":["cascade/ex-inherit"]},{"slug":"box-model","num":"2.6","title":"The Box Model","desc":"Content, padding, border, margin, and why your 300px box is 352px wide.","props":["padding","margin","box-sizing"],"intro":"Every element is a box made of four layers: content, padding, border, and margin. Adjust each layer and see how much space the box really takes up.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-every-element-is-a-box","title":"Every element is a box","kind":"Read","text":"Open DevTools on any page and hover over an element. The browser highlights a rectangle. Every element is a box, even a heading or a link that looks like it's just text. The rules for how that box is built are called the box model, and knowing them is how you predict how big things will be. Each box has four layers, from the inside out: Content is the text or image itself. By default, width and height size only this part. Padding is space between the content and the border. It's inside the box, so it shows the background color. Border is the line around the padding. It can be any width, or none at all. Margin is space outside the border that pushes other boxes away. It's always transparent. Here's the part that surprises people. By default, width sets only the content, and padding and border are added on top. This card is 352px wide on the page, not 300px: .card { width: 300px; padding: 20px; /* + 20 on each side = 340px */ border: 6px solid; /* + 6 on each side = 352px */ } Add box-sizing: border-box and the browser does the math the other way: the whole box, padding and border included, is 300px, and the content shrinks to fit. That's much easier to plan with, which is why most stylesheets start with * { box-sizing: border-box; }. Tip In DevTools, the Computed tab draws the box model for whatever element you select, with the size of every layer. When a box is bigger than you expected, look there first. One more rule: when two vertical margins touch, they don't add up. They collapse into the larger one. A heading with margin-bottom: 30px above a paragraph with margin-top: 20px leaves 30px between them, not 50px. Play"},{"id":"sec-explore-the-box","title":"Explore the box","kind":"Play","text":"Change each layer and watch the math. Then switch box-sizing and see the same settings give a different size. .box box-sizing content-box border-box width padding border-width margin content Practice"},{"id":"sec-size-and-space-two-cards","title":"Size and space two cards","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix the cards' size without touching their width, padding or border, then space and center them."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"By default (content-box), width sets only the content. Padding and border are added on top. With border-box, width includes padding and border, so the box stays the size you asked for. Margin is space outside the border. It's transparent and never part of the width. Most developers start every stylesheet with * { box-sizing: border-box; }."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Setting width: 100% plus padding and getting a horizontal scrollbar. Using padding when you wanted space between boxes (that's margin or gap). Being surprised when two vertical margins collapse into one instead of adding together. Forgetting the browser's default margin on body, h1, and p. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["box-model/quiz"],"exercises":["box-model/ex-cards"]},{"slug":"display","num":"2.7","title":"Display Types","desc":"Block vs inline vs inline-block, and why width does nothing on a span.","props":["block","inline","inline-block"],"intro":"Every element is either block (starts on a new line, fills the width) or inline (flows inside text like a word). That's why width works on a div and does nothing on a span.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-block-or-inline","title":"Block or inline?","kind":"Read","text":"In normal flow, before you add any layout, every element is one of two kinds: A block element starts on a new line and stretches to fill the width of its parent. div, p, h1–h6, ul and section are blocks. An inline element flows inside a line of text, like a word. span, a, strong and em are inline. The difference explains a classic puzzle: why width works on a div but does nothing on a link. Inline elements take their size from their content, so they ignore width, height and top and bottom margins. The display property changes how an element behaves: .badge { display: inline-block; /* flows like a word, but accepts a size */ padding: 4px 10px; width: 80px; } inline-block is the middle ground: it sits in a line of text like a word, but takes width, height and margins like a block. It's handy for buttons and badges. display: flex and grid are different again: they change how an element's children are laid out, which you'll meet in the Layout part. display: none removes an element completely, and the space it took up closes. visibility: hidden and opacity: 0 hide it but keep its space. Note Changing display changes how an element looks, never what it means. A span with display: block is still a span, so choose elements for meaning first. Play"},{"id":"sec-switch-the-display-type","title":"Switch the display type","kind":"Play","text":"Change display on three boxes, then give them a width, height, margin and padding to see which ones listen. .box (three of them) display inline block inline-block width height margin (top and bottom) padding Pack water and snacks before you set out, and check the forecast because afternoon storms roll in fast above treeline. Hiding things"},{"id":"sec-display-none-vs-visibility-hidden-vs-opacity-0","title":"display: none vs visibility: hidden vs opacity: 0","kind":"Hiding things","text":"All three make card 2 invisible. Watch what happens to the space it took up. Visible display: none visibility: hidden opacity: 0 1 2 3 display: noneGone completely. The space closes up and screen readers skip it. visibility: hiddenInvisible, but its space stays. Screen readers skip it. opacity: 0Invisible but still there: it keeps its space, can be clicked, and screen readers still read it. Practice"},{"id":"sec-change-how-boxes-flow","title":"Change how boxes flow","kind":"Practice","text":"Three elements, three different display values."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Block by default: div, p, h1–h6, ul, section. Inline by default: span, a, strong, em, img. inline-block flows like a word but accepts width, height and vertical margins. It's handy for buttons and badges. display: flex and grid change how an element's children are laid out. The element itself acts like a block. Changing display never changes what an element means. A span with display: block is still a span."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Setting width or height on a link or span and wondering why nothing happens. Adding top margin to an inline element to push it down. It's ignored. Putting a div inside a p. The browser closes the paragraph early and breaks your layout. Mysterious small gaps between inline-block items. That's the space character between them in the HTML. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["display/quiz"],"exercises":["display/ex-display"]},{"slug":"sizing","num":"2.8","title":"Sizing Items","desc":"Fixed vs flexible widths, min and max limits, aspect-ratio and vh.","props":["max-width","min-height","aspect-ratio"],"intro":"Without a set size, a block fills the width it's given and is as tall as its content. Set sizes with care: fixed pixels break on small screens, while percentages and min/max limits adapt.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-sizes-that-adapt","title":"Sizes that adapt","kind":"Read","text":"Left alone, a block element is as wide as its parent and as tall as its content. That default is usually right, and most sizing bugs start when you override it with fixed numbers. A box with width: 1000px is 1000px wide on a laptop and on a phone that's only 390px wide, so it sticks out and the whole page scrolls sideways. The pattern you'll use constantly is this one: .content { width: 100%; max-width: 600px; } On a phone the box fills the screen. On a big screen it stops growing at 600px, which keeps lines of text short enough to read comfortably. min-width works the other way, setting a floor. Percentages are a share of the parent's width, so width: 50% is half of whatever contains it. Percentage heights are trickier: they only work if the parent has a set height, which it usually doesn't. Height deserves extra care. Text wraps onto more lines as the screen narrows, so a box with a fixed height eventually can't hold its text, and the text spills out. If you need a box to be at least a certain height, use min-height, which lets it grow. Two more tools help. aspect-ratio: 16 / 9 keeps a box's shape as its width changes, which is perfect for video and image placeholders. And vh is a percentage of the window's height, so min-height: 100vh fills the screen. On phones, where toolbars come and go, 100dvh adjusts for them. Tip Before setting any width or height, ask whether the content could decide instead. Fewer fixed sizes means fewer layouts that break on someone else's screen. Play"},{"id":"sec-width-fixed-flexible-and-limited","title":"Width: fixed, flexible and limited","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the parent's width and compare how each box responds. The numbers are measured live. parent width Watch the 400px box when the parent gets narrower than 400px. no width (auto) width: 50% width: 400px width: 100%; max-width: 400px width: 30%; min-width: 180px fit-content Play"},{"id":"sec-height-vs-min-height","title":"height vs min-height","kind":"Play","text":"Add more text and see which box copes. paragraphs of text height: 120px Content below the box min-height: 120px Content below the box Play"},{"id":"sec-aspect-ratio-and-viewport-units","title":"aspect-ratio and viewport units","kind":"Play","text":"aspect-ratio 16 / 9 4 / 3 1 / 1 3 / 4 width height in vh Practice"},{"id":"sec-size-boxes-that-adapt","title":"Size boxes that adapt","kind":"Practice","text":"Fixed sizes break on other screens. Make each of these flexible."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"width: 100%; max-width: 600px; is the go-to pattern: full width on phones, capped on big screens. Percentage widths are a share of the parent's width. Let height come from the content. Reach for min-height if you need a minimum. aspect-ratio keeps a box's shape as its width changes, great for video and image placeholders. vh is a percentage of the browser window's height. 100vh is the full screen."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Big fixed widths like width: 1000px that overflow on phones. A percentage height that does nothing because the parent has no set height. Fixed heights on text boxes, so text spills out. Using 100vh on phones, where browser toolbars make it slightly too tall. 100dvh adjusts for them. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["sizing/quiz"],"exercises":["sizing/ex-sizing"]},{"slug":"overflow","num":"2.9","title":"Overflow","desc":"Content that doesn't fit, and hunting down sideways scrolling.","props":["overflow","text-overflow","overflow-wrap"],"intro":"Overflow happens when content is bigger than its box. CSS doesn't hide it by default; it lets it spill out so you notice. Here's how to control it, and how to track down the element that makes a whole page scroll sideways.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-when-content-doesn-t-fit","title":"When content doesn't fit","kind":"Read","text":"Overflow is what happens when content is bigger than its box: a long paragraph in a short card, or a giant image in a narrow column. CSS's default is to let it spill out, visibly, so you notice the problem instead of silently losing content. The overflow property decides what happens instead: visible, the default, lets it spill out. auto adds a scrollbar only when it's needed. It's usually the right choice. hidden and clip cut the extra content off. Be sure nothing important gets lost. scroll always shows scrollbars, even with nothing to scroll, so it's rarely what you want. Two text problems come up constantly. A title that must stay on one line can end in an ellipsis instead of spilling: .title { white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; } And a long URL or word has no spaces to break at, so it pushes its box wider. overflow-wrap: anywhere lets it break where it needs to. The most common overflow bug is a whole page that scrolls sideways on a phone. Somewhere, one element is wider than the screen: a fixed width: 400px, a big image, a long link. The fix is to find that element and make it flexible, not to hide the scrollbar. Watch out overflow: hidden on the body hides the sideways scroll, but the content is still too wide and now some of it is unreachable. Find the cause with DevTools instead. Play"},{"id":"sec-make-the-content-fit","title":"Make the content fit","kind":"Play","text":"Change overflow and the box's height, then fix the long title and the long link. overflow visible hidden scroll auto clip height Use min-height instead of height Title: one line with an ellipsis (…) Long link: overflow-wrap: anywhere Chautauqua to Royal Arch via the Bluebell Mesa Trail Climb through ponderosa pines to a natural sandstone arch with views across Boulder. The last half mile is steep, rocky and has over 200 stone steps. https://example.org/trails/boulder/royal-arch-via-bluebell-mesa-trail-map Bring water: there's none on the trail. Practice"},{"id":"sec-find-what-s-making-the-page-scroll-sideways","title":"Find what's making the page scroll sideways","kind":"Practice","text":"This phone-sized page scrolls left and right, which is a classic bug. Click each part of the page to inspect its width, find the one wider than the screen, then fix it. Trailhead Fall hours: trailheads open at 6am. Parking fills by 8am on weekends. Trail · Miles · Gain · Difficulty · Dogs · Shade · Water MapSave Inspector Click a part of the page on the left. Practice"},{"id":"sec-tame-the-overflow","title":"Tame the overflow","kind":"Practice","text":"Three boxes, three kinds of overflow. Pick the right fix for each."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"overflow: auto adds a scrollbar only when needed. It's usually the best choice. hidden and clip cut content off. Make sure nothing important is lost. Prefer min-height to height for boxes with text, so they can grow. Long URLs and words don't wrap by themselves. overflow-wrap: anywhere lets them break."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Fixed pixel widths, like width: 380px, wider than a phone screen. Putting overflow: hidden on body to hide the sideways scroll instead of fixing the cause. Fixed height on a text box that later gets more text, translated text, or larger font settings. Using overflow: scroll, which shows scrollbars even when nothing overflows. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["overflow/quiz"],"exercises":["overflow/ex-overflow"]},{"slug":"colors-units","num":"2.10","title":"Colors & Units","desc":"px, rem, %, vw, and the many ways to write a color.","props":["rem","vw","hsl()"],"intro":"CSS gives you several ways to write the same color and many units for size. Mix a color with the sliders, then see how each unit turns into real pixels.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-writing-colors-and-sizes","title":"Writing colors and sizes","kind":"Read","text":"CSS gives you several ways to write the same color. These all mean the same teal: color: #0b6b5e; /* hex */ color: rgb(11 107 94); /* red, green, blue: 0 to 255 */ color: hsl(172 81% 23%); /* hue, saturation, lightness */ color: rgb(11 107 94 / 50%); /* the same teal, half transparent */ A hex color is three pairs of digits for red, green and blue, from 00 to ff. Designers hand you hex codes, so you'll see them often. HSL is the easiest to adjust by hand: the hue is a spot on the color wheel from 0 to 360, saturation runs from gray to vivid, and lightness from black to white. To make a lighter version of a color, raise the lightness. Sizes need a unit, and each unit is measured from something different: px is a fixed size. rem is a multiple of the root font size, usually 16px. em is a multiple of the parent's font size. % is a share of the parent, usually its width. vw and vh are a percentage of the window's width and height. Use rem for font sizes. Some people set a larger default text size in their browser so they can read comfortably. Sizes in rem grow with that setting; sizes in px ignore it. Use % or max-width for widths, so layouts can shrink on phones. Watch out Light gray text on white looks sleek, and many people can't read it. Check the contrast ratio of your text and background. DevTools shows it when you inspect a color. Play"},{"id":"sec-mixing-colors-with-hsl","title":"Mixing colors with HSL","kind":"Play","text":"HSL stands for hue, saturation and lightness. It's the easiest format to adjust by hand: keep the hue and change the lightness to get a darker or lighter shade of the same color. hue (the color wheel) saturation (gray → vivid) lightness (black → white) Named colors Aa on white Aa on black The same color, written three ways hex rgb hsl Contrast for text (needs 4.5 : 1 or more) Play"},{"id":"sec-units-how-big-is-12","title":"Units: how big is \"12\"?","kind":"Play","text":"Each bar below is the same number with a different unit. Change the settings and watch which bars move. html font-size (sets rem) parent font-size (sets em) parent width (sets %) Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-better-units","title":"Pick better units","kind":"Practice","text":"These pixel values ignore the reader's settings and the screen size. Swap in units that adapt."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Hex like #2340d9 is pairs of red, green and blue, from 00 to ff. Add transparency with a fourth value: rgb(35 64 217 / 50%). Use rem for font sizes so text grows when someone sets a larger default size in their browser. Use %, fr or max-width for widths so layouts can shrink on phones. em depends on the parent, so nested em sizes multiply. That's why most people prefer rem."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Light gray text on white. It looks sleek and fails contrast, so many people can't read it. Forgetting the # on a hex color, or the unit on a size: width: 200 does nothing. Setting width: 100vw, which ignores the scrollbar and causes sideways scrolling. Using px for everything, so the page ignores the user's font size setting. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["colors-units/quiz"],"exercises":["colors-units/ex-units"]},{"slug":"css-variables","num":"2.11","title":"CSS Variables","desc":"Store a value once and reuse it everywhere, including a dark theme.","props":["--brand","var()",":root"],"intro":"A CSS variable (custom property) stores a value once so you can reuse it everywhere. Change one variable here and every part of the site that uses it updates at once.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-store-a-value-once-use-it-everywhere","title":"Store a value once, use it everywhere","kind":"Read","text":"Say your site's brand color is teal, and it appears in forty places in your stylesheet: buttons, links, borders, headings. Then the client asks for a darker teal. With a CSS variable, that's a one-line change: :root { --brand: #0b6b5e; --space: 16px; } .btn { background: var(--brand); padding: var(--space); } You define a variable with two dashes at the start of its name, and use it with var(). Defining variables on :root, which is the <html> element, makes them available to the whole page. Variables follow the same inheritance as text styles: an element sees the value defined on itself or its nearest ancestor. That makes them powerful. Redefine --brand on one featured card, and only that card and its children see the new color, while everything else keeps the original. The same idea gives you a dark theme almost for free. Define your colors as variables, then redefine just those variables for dark mode: @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --bg: #10131d; --text: #e7e9f3; } } If a variable might not be defined, give a fallback: var(--sale, crimson) uses crimson when there's no --sale. Watch out color: --brand; does nothing. You always need var() to read a variable. And names are case-sensitive, so --Brand is a different variable. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-a-variable","title":"Change a variable","kind":"Play","text":"Change one variable and watch every part of the site that uses it update. Then try the dark theme and the overrides. Variables on #site --brand --accent --radius --space Try these too Dark theme (redefines --bg, --text, --card) Featured card overrides --brand Define --sale Pick a variable above to outline everything that uses it. Trailhead3 new trails Royal Arch 3.4 mi · Hard Guided hike: 20% off Save Mesa Trail 6.7 mi · Featured Save Chautauqua 1.2 mi · Easy Save Practice"},{"id":"sec-use-a-variable","title":"Use a variable","kind":"Practice","text":"One brand color is typed three times. Store it once, then make a sale card that changes it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Define variables with two dashes, usually on :root so the whole page can use them: --brand: #0b6b5e; Use them with var(): color: var(--brand); Variables inherit. Redefine one on a single element, and only that element and its children see the new value. Give a fallback for when a variable isn't defined: var(--sale, crimson). A dark theme can be as simple as redefining a few color variables."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting var(): color: --brand; does nothing. Only one dash, or a typo in the name. Variable names are case-sensitive. Doing math without calc(): use calc(var(--space) * 2). Defining the variable on an element that isn't a parent of where you use it. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["css-variables/quiz"],"exercises":["css-variables/ex-vars"]},{"slug":"backgrounds-borders","num":"2.12","title":"Backgrounds & Borders","desc":"Gradients, background images, layering, borders and rounded corners.","props":["linear-gradient","background-size","border-radius"],"intro":"Backgrounds can be a color, a gradient, an image, or several layered together. Borders frame a box and border-radius rounds its corners. Build a trail card from the pieces.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-fill-and-frame-a-box","title":"Fill and frame a box","kind":"Read","text":"A box's background can be a solid color, a gradient, an image, or several of those stacked together. A gradient counts as an image, so it goes in background-image, not background-color. Layering is the trick behind a classic look: white text on a photo, with a dark gradient over the photo so the text stays readable. .hero { background-image: linear-gradient(rgb(0 0 0 / 60%), transparent), url(\"../img/flatirons.jpg\"); background-size: cover; background-position: center; color: white; } Layers are separated by commas, and the first one is drawn on top. background-size: cover scales the image to fill the box, cropping as needed, and background-position chooses which part stays in view. The path inside url() starts from the CSS file's folder, not the page's. A border needs three things: a width, a style and a color, as in border: 2px solid #2340d9. Leave out the style and nothing appears, because the default style is none. border-radius rounds the corners: 10px for a gentle curve, and 50% on a square for a perfect circle. Watch out A background image never appears on an element with no height. Backgrounds fill the box, so an empty div with a background is invisible until you give it content, padding or a height. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-trail-card","title":"Build a trail card","kind":"Play","text":"Combine a background and a border into a card, then see how size, position and corner radius change it. Background type solid color linear-gradient radial-gradient image image + gradient overlay repeating image color 1 color 2 gradient angle background-size cover contain auto 50% background-position center top bottom left right background-repeat no-repeat repeat repeat-x Border border-width border-style solid dashed dotted double groove border-color border-radius Only round the top-left and bottom-right corners Mesa Trail 6.7 miles · Moderate Practice"},{"id":"sec-backgrounds-and-borders","title":"Backgrounds and borders","kind":"Practice","text":"A gradient banner, a round avatar and a rounded card."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Gradients are images, so they go in background-image (or the background shorthand), not background-color. Layer backgrounds with commas. The first one listed is drawn on top. A dark gradient over a photo keeps white text readable. border-radius: 50% on a square makes a circle. Background image paths in CSS start from the CSS file's folder."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A background image that never shows because the element has no height. Using a background image for content that matters. Screen readers can't see it and it has no alt text. White text on a busy photo with no overlay. Setting border-width without border-style. The default style is none, so nothing appears. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["backgrounds-borders/quiz"],"exercises":["backgrounds-borders/ex-bg"]},{"slug":"shadows","num":"2.13","title":"Shadows & Filters","desc":"box-shadow, text-shadow on photos, drop-shadow, and other filters.","props":["box-shadow","text-shadow","filter"],"intro":"Shadows make flat boxes look lifted off the page, and filters change how an element is drawn, like a photo editor. A little goes a long way: soft, subtle shadows look the most natural.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-depth-used-lightly","title":"Depth, used lightly","kind":"Read","text":"A box-shadow makes a flat box look lifted off the page. It takes up to five values: how far right, how far down, how much blur, how much spread, and a color. .card { box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 12%), 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 10%); } The best shadows are barely noticeable. Use a semi-transparent color rather than solid black, and let it fall slightly downward, as if light were coming from above. Stacking a tight, darker shadow with a wide, soft one, separated by a comma, looks more realistic than any single shadow. Shadows are painted on top of the layout, so they never push other elements around. text-shadow works the same way for text, without the spread value. Its best use is keeping big headings readable on a busy photo. Don't put it on body text, where it just looks blurry. A filter changes how an element is drawn, like a photo editor. blur(), grayscale(), brightness() and contrast() are all filters. One is a shadow: drop-shadow(). The difference from box-shadow is shape. A box-shadow always follows the element's rectangle, while drop-shadow() follows the actual visible shape, so it's the one to use on a transparent PNG or an SVG icon. Tip A shadow alone is a weak way to separate a card from a similar background, especially in dark mode where shadows barely show. Pair it with a subtle border. Play"},{"id":"sec-box-shadow","title":"box-shadow","kind":"Play","text":"A shadow has an x offset, a y offset, a blur, a spread and a color. Try the presets, then adjust them yourself. x offset y offset blur spread color opacity inset (shadow inside the box) Add a second, tighter shadow layer Mesa Trail 6.7 miles · Moderate · Dogs on leash Play"},{"id":"sec-text-shadow-for-readable-text-on-images","title":"text-shadow for readable text on images","kind":"Play","text":"blur shadow darkness Busy background Above Treeline Play"},{"id":"sec-box-shadow-vs-filter-drop-shadow","title":"box-shadow vs filter: drop-shadow()","kind":"Play","text":"box-shadow always follows the element's rectangle. The drop-shadow filter follows the actual shape, including transparent parts of an image. box-shadow The shadow outlines the image's square box. filter: drop-shadow() The shadow traces the mountain's shape. Play"},{"id":"sec-other-filters","title":"Other filters","kind":"Play","text":"filter none grayscale(1) sepia(1) blur(4px) brightness(0.6) contrast(1.6) saturate(2) hue-rotate(120deg) A common use: show photos in grayscale and bring the color back on hover. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-depth-with-shadows","title":"Add depth with shadows","kind":"Practice","text":"Make the card float with a soft, natural shadow, and lift the heading off the photo."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Use a semi-transparent shadow color like rgb(0 0 0 / 15%). Solid black looks harsh. Shadows coming slightly down (positive y) look natural, like light from above. Stack shadows with commas: a tight dark one plus a wide soft one looks realistic. Shadows don't take up space or move other elements. They're painted on top."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Heavy black shadows on everything, so nothing stands out. Relying on a shadow alone to separate a card from a similar background. Add a border too. Text shadows on body text. Save them for big text on images. Animating box-shadow on many elements, which can make scrolling choppy. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["shadows/quiz"],"exercises":["shadows/ex-shadow"]},{"slug":"typography","num":"2.14","title":"Typography","desc":"Fonts, sizes, line height, and making text easy to read.","props":["font-family","line-height"],"intro":"Most of a web page is text, so how it reads matters more than almost anything else. Adjust the settings and watch the readability checks respond.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-text-people-can-read","title":"Text people can read","kind":"Read","text":"Most of what's on a web page is text, so the way it reads matters more than almost any other design choice. A few settings make most of the difference: body { font-family: \"Source Sans 3\", Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 1.125rem; line-height: 1.6; } p { max-width: 65ch; } font-family takes a font stack: a list of fonts in order of preference. If the first isn't available, the browser tries the next. Always end with a generic family like sans-serif or serif, so there's a sensible fallback font no matter what. Put quotes around names with spaces. For body text, aim for at least 16px, which is 1rem, and a line-height between about 1.4 and 1.8. Write the line height without a unit, so it scales with the text size. Line length is the setting people forget. Lines that run the full width of a big monitor make readers lose their place jumping back to the start of the next line. About 45 to 75 characters per line reads best, and the ch unit, roughly the width of one character, makes that easy: max-width: 65ch. For headings, pick sizes that belong together. A type scale multiplies each level by the same ratio: with a 16px base and a ratio of 1.25, you get 20, 25, 31 and 39px. The sizes feel related instead of random. Tip Use two typefaces at most, one for headings and one for body text. Left-align long paragraphs: centered text is fine for a short heading, but hard to read for more than a line or two. Play"},{"id":"sec-tune-the-text","title":"Tune the text","kind":"Play","text":"Adjust each setting and watch the readability checks respond. font-family Atkinson Hyperlegible Lora (serif) Georgia (serif) system-ui (your OS font) Arial Times New Roman monospace font-size line-height max-width (line length) font-weight 300 400 700 letter-spacing text-align left center justify right Why the trail closes in spring Every April, the lower section of the Mesa Trail closes for about six weeks. Snowmelt turns the clay soil into deep mud, and hikers who step around the puddles widen the trail and trample the wildflowers growing at its edges. Rangers reopen the trail once the ground can hold a footprint without sinking. Until then, the upper loop stays open and drains much faster. Type scale"},{"id":"sec-heading-sizes-that-belong-together","title":"Heading sizes that belong together","kind":"Type scale","text":"Pick a base size and a ratio. Each heading level is the one below it multiplied by the ratio, so the sizes feel related instead of random. base size ratio 1.125 (subtle) 1.25 (balanced) 1.333 (clear) 1.5 (bold) 1.618 (dramatic) Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-it-readable","title":"Make it readable","kind":"Practice","text":"The text is set in one long, cramped line. Fix the font, the spacing and the line length."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Aim for 45 to 75 characters per line. Set max-width in ch on paragraphs. Body text reads best with a line-height between about 1.4 and 1.8, and no unit. Always end a font-family list with a generic fallback like sans-serif or serif. Use two typefaces at most: one for headings, one for body text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Lines stretching the full width of a big monitor, so readers lose their place. Centering long paragraphs. Center short headings only. Body text under 16px, which is hard to read on phones. Font names with spaces but no quotes, or a Google Font used without its <link> tag. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["typography/quiz"],"exercises":["typography/ex-type"]},{"slug":"web-fonts","num":"2.15","title":"Web Fonts","desc":"Loading fonts from Google Fonts, fallbacks, and what happens while they load.","props":["@font-face","font-display","fallback"],"intro":"Visitors only have a handful of fonts installed. A web font is downloaded along with your page so everyone sees the same type. Here's how to add one, what happens while it loads, and why the fallback still matters.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-fonts-that-travel-with-your-page","title":"Fonts that travel with your page","kind":"Read","text":"Every computer has its own set of installed fonts, and they're not the same. A web font is downloaded along with your page, so every visitor sees the same type. The easiest source is Google Fonts: choose a font and the weights you need, and it gives you <link> tags to paste into your head, above your own stylesheet. Then use it like any other font: body { font-family: \"Source Sans 3\", Arial, sans-serif; } You can also host the font files yourself, with an @font-face rule that names the font and points to a .woff2 file in your project. A web font is a download, and downloads take time or fail. Until it arrives, the browser uses the next font in your font stack. Pick a fallback that's similar in width and feel, so the page doesn't jump around when the real font swaps in. font-display decides what visitors see in the meantime; swap, which shows the fallback right away and switches when the font arrives, is a good default. Each weight and style is a separate file. Regular, italic, bold and bold italic are four downloads. Two families and three or four weights in total is plenty for most sites. Watch out If you use font-weight: 700 but only downloaded weight 400, the browser fakes a bold by smearing the regular letters. And a misspelled font name fails silently: CSS just falls back, with no error. Check DevTools to see which font actually rendered. The easy way: Google Fonts Find a font at fonts.google.com and choose the weights you need. Copy the <link> tags it gives you into your page's <head>, above your own stylesheet. Use the font in CSS with font-family, followed by fallbacks. Self-hosting with @font-face Put the .woff2 files in a fonts/ folder in your project. Add an @font-face rule naming the font and pointing to the file. Use that name in font-family like any other font. Play"},{"id":"sec-always-include-a-fallback","title":"Always include a fallback","kind":"Play","text":"If the font fails to load, or hasn't loaded yet, the browser uses the next font in your list. Pick one that's similar. web font Merriweather Playfair Display Roboto Slab Source Sans 3 Space Mono Pacifico fallback serif sans-serif monospace cursive Pretend the font failed to load Weekend Hikes Near Boulder Pack water, layers and a map. Afternoon storms roll in fast above treeline, so start early and turn back by noon. Play"},{"id":"sec-what-visitors-see-while-it-loads","title":"What visitors see while it loads","kind":"Play","text":"font-display decides what happens during the download. Pick a setting and simulate a slow connection where the font takes 2.5 seconds. font-display swap block fallback optional Simulate slow load Royal Arch Trail 0sfont arrives at 2.5s3s Press \"Simulate slow load.\" Play"},{"id":"sec-only-load-what-you-use","title":"Only load what you use","kind":"Play","text":"Every weight and style is a separate file to download. These sizes are rough estimates for a typical font. Extra download Practice"},{"id":"sec-load-a-web-font-properly","title":"Load a web font properly","kind":"Practice","text":"The font names and fallbacks here are broken. Fix them so text always shows, in the right font when it arrives."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Put font names with spaces in quotes: font-family: \"Source Sans 3\", sans-serif; End every font list with a generic family: serif, sans-serif or monospace. font-display: swap is a good default. Text shows right away and switches when the font arrives. Two families and three or four weights is plenty for most sites."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using a font name in CSS without the <link> or @font-face that loads it. It only works on computers that happen to have it installed. Asking for font-weight: 700 when only 400 was downloaded, so the browser fakes a blurry bold. Misspelling the font name. CSS silently falls back with no error. Fancy script fonts for body text, which are hard to read at small sizes. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["web-fonts/quiz"],"exercises":["web-fonts/ex-fonts"]},{"slug":"review-css","num":"2.16","title":"Review: CSS Basics","desc":"Style a trail card with variables, states and the box model. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole part together. Skim the recap, style a trail card from scratch, then test yourself with questions that mix every lesson.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part was about how the browser decides what each element looks like: which rules match, which one wins, and how boxes, colors and text are sized. How CSS Is Applied: A rule is a selector plus declarations, and one missing semicolon can break the next line. Selectors & Specificity: A space means inside, > means direct child, and IDs outrank classes, which outrank elements. Pseudo-classes & States: Style :hover and :focus-visible together, and use :nth-child for patterns. Pseudo-elements: ::before and ::after add decoration, and need a content property. Cascade & Inheritance: Text properties inherit, box properties don't, and later rules win ties. The Box Model: Padding and border add to the width unless box-sizing is border-box. Display Types: Block elements stack, inline elements flow, and inline-block flows but accepts a width. Sizing Items: width: 100% with a max-width adapts; min-height lets boxes grow. Overflow: Content that doesn't fit spills out; overflow and overflow-wrap control it. Colors & Units: rem for text, % or max-width for widths, and a fourth value for transparency. CSS Variables: Store a value once on :root, use it with var(), and redefine it to theme a part of the page. Backgrounds & Borders: Gradients are background images, and border-radius: 50% makes a circle. Shadows & Filters: Soft, semi-transparent shadows that fall downward look natural. Typography: Keep lines 45–75 characters long, with a unitless line-height around 1.5. Web Fonts: Quote font names with spaces, end every list with a generic family, and use font-display: swap. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-a-trail-card","title":"Mini project: a trail card","kind":"Practice","text":"Style a card from scratch with variables, the box model, states and good typography. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-css/quiz"],"exercises":["review-css/ex-project"]},{"slug":"flexbox","num":"3.1","title":"Flexbox","desc":"Line items up in a row or column, then space and align them.","props":["display: flex","justify-content","align-items","gap"],"intro":"Flexbox lays out items in one direction, a row or a column. You set the rules on the container, and its direct children (the flex items) follow them.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-one-direction-at-a-time","title":"One direction at a time","kind":"Read","text":"Putting boxes side by side used to take floats and workarounds. Flexbox does it in one line: add display: flex to a parent, and its direct children line up in a row. The parent becomes the flex container, and its children become flex items. Almost every rule goes on the container. That's also the most common mistake: display: flex goes on the parent, not on the items you want to move. Every flex container has two directions: The main axis runs the way items flow. With the default flex-direction: row, that's left to right. justify-content spaces items along it. The cross axis runs at a right angle to it, so up and down in a row. align-items lines items up along it. Switch to flex-direction: column and the axes swap. Now justify-content moves items up and down, and align-items moves them side to side. If a property seems to do the wrong thing, check which way your main axis runs. .site-nav { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; /* main axis: spread out */ align-items: center; /* cross axis: center vertically */ gap: 16px; /* space between items */ } Use gap for space between items instead of margins on each one. And remember that items don't wrap by default: when they run out of room, they shrink. Add flex-wrap: wrap to let them move onto a new line instead. Note Flexbox handles one direction at a time. For rows and columns together, like a photo gallery, use CSS Grid. Flexbox vs Grid builds the same layouts both ways. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-every-setting","title":"Try every setting","kind":"Play","text":"Change one property at a time and watch the items move. Then switch to a column and see which controls swap jobs. .container flex-direction row row-reverse column column-reverse justify-content flex-start center flex-end space-between space-around space-evenly align-items stretch center flex-start flex-end baseline flex-wrap nowrap wrap gap 12px Items number of items 4 .item:first-child { flex-grow } 0 (don't grow) 1 (fill extra space) Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-a-nav-bar","title":"Build a nav bar","kind":"Practice","text":"Most site headers are a flex row inside a flex row. Try it on a real nav, then press Check my work."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"justify-content works along the main axis, which is the direction set by flex-direction. align-items works along the cross axis, perpendicular to the main axis. Switch to column and the two swap: now justify-content moves things up and down. gap adds space between items without margins on each one."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Putting display: flex on the items instead of their parent. Expecting align-items: center to center vertically when the container has no extra height. Forgetting flex-wrap: wrap, so items squish instead of moving to a new line. Using Flexbox for a full two-dimensional grid. Reach for CSS Grid instead. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["flexbox/quiz"],"exercises":["flexbox/ex-nav"]},{"slug":"grid","num":"3.2","title":"CSS Grid","desc":"Rows and columns at the same time, with items that span cells.","props":["grid-template-columns","fr","span"],"intro":"Grid controls rows and columns at the same time. Define the columns on the container, and items fill the cells in order unless you tell one to span more space.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-rows-and-columns-at-the-same-time","title":"Rows and columns at the same time","kind":"Read","text":"Flexbox lines items up in one direction. Grid handles two at once: you define columns (and rows, if you need them) on the container, and the items fill the cells in order. .gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr 1fr; gap: 16px; } That makes three columns. The fr unit means \"a fraction of the leftover space\", so the first column is twice as wide as each of the others, whatever the container's width. repeat(3, 1fr) is a shortcut for three equal columns. Gap adds space between cells, just as it does in Flexbox. Items fill the grid left to right, top to bottom, one per cell. Any item can take up more room: .featured { grid-column: span 2; /* two columns wide */ grid-row: span 2; /* two rows tall */ } The other items flow around it. Spanning rows as well as columns is something only Grid can do. The most useful line of Grid makes a card gallery that adapts to any screen with no media queries at all: .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); } Read it as: \"fit as many columns as you can, each at least 220px wide, and share any extra space equally.\" On a phone you get one column, on a laptop four. Watch out Like Flexbox, display: grid and grid-template-columns go on the container, not on the items. The items only need rules when one of them should span or move. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-grid","title":"Build a grid","kind":"Play","text":"Change the columns, gap and alignment, then make the first item span more cells. .container grid-template-columns repeat(3, 1fr) repeat(4, 1fr) 1fr 2fr 1fr 200px 1fr repeat(auto-fit, minmax(120px, 1fr)) gap 12px justify-items stretch start center end align-items stretch start center end Items number of items 7 .item-1 { grid-column } span 1 span 2 span 3 .item-1 { grid-row } span 1 span 2 Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-a-photo-grid","title":"Build a photo grid","kind":"Practice","text":"Three equal columns, with one featured photo that takes up four cells."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"fr means \"a fraction of the leftover space.\" 1fr 2fr makes the second column twice as wide. repeat(3, 1fr) is shorthand for 1fr 1fr 1fr. grid-column: span 2 makes an item take up two columns. Other items flow around it. repeat(auto-fit, minmax(120px, 1fr)) makes a responsive grid with no media queries. Resize the window to see it."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Setting grid-template-columns on the items instead of the container. Spanning more columns than the grid has, which creates extra implicit columns. Using fixed px columns everywhere, so the layout overflows on phones. Mixing up justify-items (horizontal, inside each cell) with justify-content (the whole grid). Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["grid/quiz"],"exercises":["grid/ex-grid"]},{"slug":"flex-vs-grid","num":"3.3","title":"Flexbox vs Grid","desc":"Six real layouts built both ways, and which one fits each job.","props":["flex","grid","when to use"],"intro":"Both can build most layouts, but each has a sweet spot. Flexbox arranges items along one line and lets their content decide their size. Grid lays out rows and columns together. Pick a layout and compare.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-two-tools-different-jobs","title":"Two tools, different jobs","kind":"Read","text":"You can build most layouts with either Flexbox or Grid, which is why people argue about which is better. They're not competitors. Each has a sweet spot, and real sites use both, often on the same page. The key difference is dimensions. Flexbox is one-dimensional: it arranges items along a single row or column, and lets each item's content help decide its size. When flex items wrap onto a second line, that line is laid out on its own, so items in different lines don't line up with each other. Grid is two-dimensional: you define columns and rows, and items line up in both directions. A useful rule of thumb: Reach for Flexbox for a row of things whose sizes come from their content: a nav bar, a set of buttons, a logo next to a title, tags that wrap. Reach for Grid when columns must line up, or when you're defining the layout first and placing content into it: a card gallery, a page with a sidebar, a photo layout where one item spans several cells. The classic sign you picked wrong is a card gallery built with Flexbox: the last row has two cards, and they stretch to fill the whole width. With Grid, they keep the same width as the cards above. The two also nest freely. A page might use Grid for its overall layout, and each card inside might use Flexbox to line up its icon, title and button. Tip Ask yourself: \"Do I care about columns lining up?\" If yes, use Grid. If you just need things in a row, spaced nicely, use Flexbox. Play"},{"id":"sec-compare-the-same-layout","title":"Compare the same layout","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a layout and see it built both ways. Change the container width to see where each one struggles. container width Flexbox Grid Rule of thumb Content out: if the items' sizes should decide the layout (a row of buttons, tags, a navbar), use Flexbox. Layout in: if you want to draw the rows and columns first and drop items into them (a gallery, a page, a dashboard), use Grid. Real sites use both, often nested. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-right-tool","title":"Pick the right tool","kind":"Practice","text":"This page uses Flexbox for everything. One part should be Grid."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Flexbox is one-dimensional: a row or a column. Wrapped lines don't line up with each other. Grid is two-dimensional: columns line up from row to row. Only Grid can make one item span several rows and columns. It's normal to put a flex container inside a grid cell, or a grid inside a flex item."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Building a card gallery with Flexbox and getting a stretched last row. Adding lots of wrapper divs to fake a two-dimensional layout with Flexbox. Using Grid for a simple row of buttons and fighting fixed column widths. Thinking one is \"better.\" They solve different problems. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["flex-vs-grid/quiz"],"exercises":["flex-vs-grid/ex-choose"]},{"slug":"floats","num":"3.4","title":"Floats","desc":"Wrap text around an image, clear floats, and fix collapsing parents.","props":["float","clear","flow-root"],"intro":"A floated element moves to the left or right, and the text flows around it, like a photo in a magazine. That's still the job floats do best. Before Flexbox and Grid existed they were also used for whole-page layouts, so you'll see them in older code.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-wrapping-text-around-an-image","title":"Wrapping text around an image","kind":"Read","text":"A float moves an element to the left or right edge of its container, and lets the text that follows flow around it, like a photo in a magazine article. That's still the job floats do best: .article img { float: left; width: 40%; margin: 0 1rem 1rem 0; /* space between the photo and the text */ } Give a floated element a width, or it shrinks to fit its content. And give it a margin on the side facing the text, so words don't press right against the image. Sometimes the next part of the page should start below the float instead of wrapping beside it, like a new section heading. clear: both does that: the element moves down until it's past any floats above it. Floats have one famous side effect. A floated element no longer counts toward its parent's height, so if everything inside a box is floated, the box collapses to nothing and its background and border disappear. Add display: flow-root to the parent and it contains its floats again. Before Flexbox and Grid existed, floats were the only way to put boxes side by side, so whole page layouts were built from them, along with workarounds like the \"clearfix\" hack. You'll see that in older code and tutorials. For new layouts, use Flexbox or Grid, and keep floats for wrapping text. Tip On a phone, a floated image squeezes the text into a narrow strip. Use a media query to remove the float on small screens and let the image sit above the text. Play"},{"id":"sec-wrap-text-around-an-image","title":"Wrap text around an image","kind":"Play","text":"float none left right margin (space around it) Add clear: both to the next heading Round photo with shape-outside: circle() photo Royal Arch is one of the most rewarding short hikes near Boulder. The trail climbs steeply through ponderosa pine forest, gaining about 1,400 feet in under two miles. Getting there Park at Chautauqua and follow the Bluebell Road to the trailhead. The final stretch has over 200 stone steps, so take your time and save some energy for the way down. Play"},{"id":"sec-the-collapsing-parent","title":"The collapsing parent","kind":"Play","text":"A floated child no longer counts toward its parent's height. If everything inside is floated, the parent shrinks to almost nothing and the next element slides up underneath. Fix the parent with display: flow-root parent (dashed border) floated The next section Practice"},{"id":"sec-wrap-text-around-a-photo","title":"Wrap text around a photo","kind":"Practice","text":"Float the photo, start the next section below it, and fix a box that collapsed."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"A floated element needs a width, or it shrinks to fit its content. Give floats a margin on the side facing the text so words don't touch the image. clear: both makes an element start below any floats above it. display: flow-root on the parent makes it contain its floats. Older code used a \"clearfix\" hack for this. For side-by-side layout today, use Flexbox or Grid. Use floats for wrapping text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Building columns with floats, then fighting collapsed parents and uneven heights. Forgetting that floats don't work on flex or grid items. The float is ignored. On phones, a floated image squeezes the text into a narrow strip. Use a media query to remove the float on small screens. Floating something to push it right, when margin-left: auto or justify-content would be simpler. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["floats/quiz"],"exercises":["floats/ex-float"]},{"slug":"positioning","num":"3.5","title":"Positioning","desc":"relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky, and what each one is relative to.","props":["position","top","z-index"],"intro":"The position property decides what top and left are measured from, and whether the element still takes up space. Change the settings, then scroll inside the window to compare.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-taking-an-element-out-of-the-flow","title":"Taking an element out of the flow","kind":"Read","text":"Normally every element sits where the normal flow puts it, one after another. The position property lets you move an element from there. The question each value answers is: measured from where? static is the default. top and left do nothing. relative nudges the element from where it would have been. Its original space stays reserved, so nothing else moves. absolute takes the element out of the flow completely and places it inside its nearest positioned ancestor. fixed places it relative to the browser window, so it stays put while you scroll. sticky scrolls normally until it reaches its top value, then sticks there until its parent scrolls away. The pattern you'll use most combines two of them. To pin a \"New\" badge to the corner of a card, make the card the reference point with position: relative, then place the badge with absolute: .card { position: relative; } .card .badge { position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; } Without position: relative on the card, the badge looks for the next positioned ancestor up the page and often ends up in the corner of the whole page. When positioned elements overlap, z-index decides which is on top: higher numbers come forward. It only works on positioned elements (and flex and grid items). Watch out Don't build whole layouts with absolute positioning. Absolutely placed boxes don't make room for each other, so they overlap as soon as the text or screen size changes. Use Flexbox or Grid for layout, and positioning for overlays, badges and sticky headers. Play"},{"id":"sec-move-the-box","title":"Move the box","kind":"Play","text":"Change the position value and offsets, then scroll inside the window to see how each one behaves. #box position static relative absolute fixed sticky top left z-index .parent position: relative browser window .parent sibling 1 #box sibling 2 · z-index 1 Scroll inside the window above. The window has its own scrollbar. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pin-a-badge-to-a-card","title":"Pin a badge to a card","kind":"Practice","text":"Place a badge in each card's corner, so it stays put whatever the card's size."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"static is the default. top and left do nothing. relative nudges the element from where it would have been. Its original space stays reserved. absolute removes the element from the flow and places it inside the nearest positioned ancestor. That's why you put position: relative on the parent. fixed places it relative to the browser window, so it stays put while you scroll. sticky scrolls normally until it reaches the top value, then sticks until its parent scrolls away."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using absolute without a positioned parent, so the element flies to the corner of the page. Building whole layouts with absolute positioning. Use Flexbox or Grid, and save positioning for overlays and badges. Setting z-index on a static element. It only works on positioned (or flex and grid) items. Sticky that never sticks because a parent has overflow: hidden, or no top value is set. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["positioning/quiz"],"exercises":["positioning/ex-badge"]},{"slug":"review-layout","num":"3.6","title":"Review: Layout","desc":"Lay out a trail guide page with Flexbox, Grid and positioning. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole part together. Skim the recap, lay out a full page with the right tool for each part, then test yourself with mixed questions.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part was about arranging boxes. Each tool has a job: Flexbox for rows, Grid for rows and columns, floats for wrapping text, and positioning for things that overlap. Flexbox: display: flex on the parent lines its children up in a row, with justify-content and align-items to space and align them. CSS Grid: Grid defines columns and rows, fr shares out space, and items can span several cells. Flexbox vs Grid: Flexbox for one direction sized by content, Grid when columns must line up. Floats: Floats wrap text around an image, clear starts below them, and flow-root contains them. Positioning: absolute places an element inside its nearest positioned ancestor; sticky and fixed stay put while scrolling. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-a-trail-guide-layout","title":"Mini project: a trail guide layout","kind":"Practice","text":"Use the right layout tool for each part of the page. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-layout/quiz"],"exercises":["review-layout/ex-project"]},{"slug":"media-queries","num":"4.1","title":"Media Queries","desc":"Change a layout at specific screen widths with breakpoints.","props":["@media","min-width","breakpoints"],"intro":"A media query applies CSS only when a condition is true, usually \"the screen is at least this wide.\" Drag the slider to change the pretend screen width and watch which rules switch on.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-css-that-depends-on-the-screen","title":"CSS that depends on the screen","kind":"Read","text":"A media query wraps some CSS in a condition, so those rules apply only when the condition is true. Almost always, the condition is the screen's width: .cards { display: grid; gap: 16px; } @media (min-width: 600px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } } @media (min-width: 1000px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr; } } Read min-width: 600px as \"600px or wider\". On a phone, neither query applies and the cards stack in one column. At 700px, the first applies: two columns. At 1200px, both apply, and since they conflict, the later one wins: three columns. That's why queries are listed from smallest to largest. The width where a query kicks in is called a breakpoint. Don't choose breakpoints from a list of phone and tablet sizes; there are thousands of devices, and new ones every year. Instead, widen your browser window slowly, and add a breakpoint wherever your layout starts to look wrong. This approach, a simple base layout plus min-width queries that add complexity, is called mobile first, and it's the subject of Mobile-First Workflow. Watch out Media queries on phones depend on the viewport meta tag in your head. Without it, the phone pretends to be a 980px-wide desktop, and your phone styles never switch on. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-screen-width","title":"Change the screen width","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the slider to change the pretend screen width and watch which rules switch on. Screen width 375px Phone · 375 Tablet · 768 Laptop · 1280 Trailhead TrailsMapsGear Weekend hikes near you Royal Arch3.4 mi · Hard Mesa Trail6.7 mi · Moderate Chautauqua Loop1.2 mi · Easy Trail conditions Muddy below 7,000 ft. Bring layers. Practice"},{"id":"sec-rewrite-the-queries-mobile-first","title":"Rewrite the queries mobile first","kind":"Practice","text":"These queries start from the desktop and undo it. Flip them to start small and add."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Mobile first: write the phone layout with no media query, then add min-width queries as the screen gets bigger. @media (min-width: 600px) means \"600px or wider.\" Both queries are on at 1000px. Later rules win when they conflict, so list queries from smallest to largest. Pick breakpoints where your layout starts to look wrong, not by device names."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> in the <head>. Phones will ignore your queries without it. Missing the space or parentheses: @media(min-width:600px) is fine, but @media min-width: 600px is not. Putting the media query above the base rules, so the base rules override it. Mixing min-width and max-width queries until nobody can tell which applies. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["media-queries/quiz"],"exercises":["media-queries/ex-mq"]},{"slug":"responsive-images","num":"4.2","title":"Responsive Images","desc":"Images that shrink to fit and never overflow their container.","props":["max-width","srcset","object-fit"],"intro":"Images have a fixed size, but screens don't. Here's how to stop images from overflowing, crop them to fit a shape, and send phones a smaller file.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-images-that-fit-every-screen","title":"Images that fit every screen","kind":"Read","text":"An image has a fixed size in pixels, but your layout doesn't. Put a 1200px photo in a 360px-wide phone screen and it sticks out, pushing the whole page sideways. Two lines, which belong in nearly every stylesheet, fix that: img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } max-width: 100% lets an image shrink to fit its container but never stretch past its real size, and height: auto keeps its proportions. Keep the width and height attributes in your HTML too: the browser uses them to reserve the right space before the image arrives. Sometimes an image must fill a box of a different shape, like a square profile photo cut from a wide picture. Setting both width and height would squash it. object-fit crops instead: cover fills the box and trims the edges, while contain shows the whole image and leaves empty space. object-position chooses which part stays in view. Shrinking a big image with CSS still makes a phone download the whole big file. srcset lists several copies at different widths and lets the browser pick the smallest one that will look sharp: <img src=\"arch-800.jpg\" srcset=\"arch-400.jpg 400w, arch-800.jpg 800w, arch-1600.jpg 1600w\" alt=\"The sandstone arch at the top of the trail\"> The w after each file is its real width in pixels. The browser does the math, including the screen's pixel density. Tip When an image isn't full-width, add a sizes attribute so the browser knows how wide it will actually be. That's covered in Picture & Lazy Loading. Play"},{"id":"sec-make-it-shrink","title":"Make it shrink","kind":"Play","text":"This photo is 800 × 500 pixels. Make the container narrower and see what each setting does. container width max-width: 100% height: auto The img tag has width=\"800\" height=\"500\" attributes, which you should always include. Play"},{"id":"sec-fit-it-to-a-shape-with-object-fit","title":"Fit it to a shape with object-fit","kind":"Play","text":"When an image has to fill a box of a different shape, like a square profile photo or a wide banner, object-fit decides whether to stretch, shrink or crop it. box shape square tall wide object-fit fill contain cover none scale-down object-position center left right top bottom Play"},{"id":"sec-send-the-right-file-with-srcset","title":"Send the right file with srcset","kind":"Play","text":"With srcset you list several copies of the same image at different widths. The browser downloads the smallest one that still looks sharp on the visitor's screen. image shows at (CSS px) screen pixel density 1x 2x 3x Most phones are 2x or 3x: each CSS pixel uses 2 or 3 real pixels across. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-the-images-fit","title":"Make the images fit","kind":"Practice","text":"A big photo breaks the layout and an avatar gets squashed. Fix both, and offer smaller files."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Put max-width: 100% and height: auto on images in nearly every stylesheet. Keep the width and height attributes in the HTML. The browser uses them to reserve space before the image loads. object-fit: cover fills the box and crops the edges. contain shows the whole image and leaves empty space. Use object-position to keep the important part of the photo in view when it's cropped."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Uploading a 4000px photo straight from a phone. It can be 5 MB for something shown 400px wide. Setting both width and height in CSS without object-fit, which stretches the image. Using width: 100% on a small image, which blows it up and makes it blurry. Listing srcset widths that don't match the real files' widths. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["responsive-images/quiz"],"exercises":["responsive-images/ex-img"]},{"slug":"picture","num":"4.3","title":"Picture & Lazy Loading","desc":"The sizes attribute, different crops with picture, and loading images only when needed.","props":["sizes","<picture>","loading=\"lazy\""],"intro":"Three more ways to send the right image: tell the browser how wide it will be with sizes, swap in a different crop with <picture>, and skip images nobody scrolls to with loading=\"lazy\".","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-send-the-right-image-at-the-right-time","title":"Send the right image, at the right time","kind":"Read","text":"With srcset, the browser picks a file based on how wide the image will be. But the browser chooses which file to download before your CSS has loaded, so it doesn't know your layout. Unless you say otherwise, it assumes the image fills the whole window. The sizes attribute tells it: <img srcset=\"mesa-400.jpg 400w, mesa-800.jpg 800w, mesa-1600.jpg 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 900px) 33vw, 100vw\" src=\"mesa-800.jpg\" alt=\"Mesa Trail at sunrise\"> Read it as: \"on screens 900px and wider, this image is a third of the window wide; otherwise it's the full width.\" Now a small card image on a laptop downloads a small file. Sometimes a smaller copy isn't enough. A wide landscape shrunk onto a phone turns the arch into a speck. Showing a tighter crop on small screens is called art direction, and picture does it: <picture> <source media=\"(max-width: 600px)\" srcset=\"arch-tall.jpg\"> <img src=\"arch-wide.jpg\" alt=\"Royal Arch above the trees\"> </picture> The browser uses the first <source> that matches, or falls back to the <img>. The <img> is required: it's what actually shows the picture. Finally, loading=\"lazy\" waits to download an image until the visitor scrolls near it. On a long page, that saves data for everyone who never scrolls to the bottom. This is called lazy loading. Watch out Don't lazy-load the big image at the top of the page. It's the first thing visitors see, so it should start downloading as early as possible. Play"},{"id":"sec-tell-the-browser-the-size-with-sizes","title":"Tell the browser the size with sizes","kind":"Play","text":"The browser picks a file from srcset before your CSS has loaded, so it doesn't know how wide the image will be. Without sizes, it assumes the full window width. This layout shows 1, 2 or 3 cards per row. window width sizes (no sizes attribute) 100vw (min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw (min-width: 1100px) 33vw, (min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw screen pixel density 1x 2x Read sizes left to right: the first media condition that's true wins, and the last value has no condition, so it's the default. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-crop-with-picture","title":"Change the crop with <picture>","kind":"Play","text":"Sometimes a smaller copy isn't enough. A wide landscape shrunk onto a phone makes the arch a speck. <picture> lets you send a different crop at different widths. This is called art direction. window width The browser checks each <source> in order and uses the first one whose media matches. If none match, it uses the <img>. The <img> is required. It holds the alt text, and any CSS you write targets the img, not the picture. Play"},{"id":"sec-skip-what-nobody-sees-with-loading-lazy","title":"Skip what nobody sees with loading=\"lazy\"","kind":"Play","text":"Scroll the page on the left and watch the network log. With lazy loading, images download just before they scroll into view. loading eager (default) lazy The first image is above the fold, so it is always loaded right away. Lazy-loading it would only make the page feel slower. Reload the page trailclub.github.io/hikes/ Downloaded so far Practice"},{"id":"sec-send-the-right-image","title":"Send the right image","kind":"Practice","text":"Tell the browser how big one image is, swap the crop of another, and lazy-load the third."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Use srcset with w widths (trail-800.jpg 800w) plus sizes when the image isn't full-width. Use 1x/2x only for images with a fixed size, like a logo. sizes describes your layout. If your CSS changes the columns, update sizes to match. <picture> with media changes the crop. With type it offers a newer format and falls back: <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"arch.avif\">. Put loading=\"lazy\" on images and iframes further down the page, and keep width and height so the page doesn't jump when they arrive."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Leaving out sizes with w descriptors, so a small card image downloads at full-window size. Using <picture> only to switch sizes. srcset and sizes on a plain <img> already do that, and let the browser choose. Forgetting the <img> inside <picture>. Without it, nothing shows. Lazy-loading the big photo at the top of the page. It should load as early as possible. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["picture/quiz"],"exercises":["picture/ex-picture"]},{"slug":"mobile-first","num":"4.4","title":"Mobile-First Workflow","desc":"Write the phone layout first, then add complexity as screens grow.","props":["min-width","viewport meta"],"intro":"Build the phone version first, then add to it as the screen gets wider. Step through how one page comes together and watch it on three screen sizes at once.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-start-small-then-add","title":"Start small, then add","kind":"Read","text":"There are two ways to make a site responsive. You can design for a laptop and then squeeze it onto a phone, undoing styles as the screen shrinks. Or you can start with the phone and add to it as the screen grows. The second way is called mobile first, and it almost always means less CSS and fewer bugs. The reason is that the phone layout is usually the simplest: one column, everything stacked, full width. That's close to what HTML does with no CSS at all. So your base styles, outside any media query, describe the phone version. Then each min-width media query adds only what changes on wider screens: /* Phone: everything stacks */ .page { padding: 16px; } /* Tablet and up: two columns */ @media (min-width: 700px) { .page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; gap: 24px; } } Working this way, you never write a rule just to undo another one. And because the phone version is built first, it gets your full attention instead of being an afterthought. Mobile first also applies to content. If something is too much for a phone, ask whether it's needed at all, rather than hiding it on small screens. Phone visitors often want exactly the same information as everyone else. Tip Narrowing your browser window is a good first check, but test on a real phone too. Touch targets, real font sizes and the on-screen keyboard only show up on the real thing. DevTools' device mode is a good middle step. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-page-step-by-step","title":"Build a page step by step","kind":"Play","text":"Step through how one page comes together, and watch it on three screen sizes at once. Phone · 375px Tablet · 768px Laptop · 1280px How it looks now ← Previous step Next step → Before you hand it in"},{"id":"sec-responsive-checklist","title":"Responsive checklist","kind":"Before you hand it in","text":"Tick these off for your own project. Your checks are saved in this browser only. Practice"},{"id":"sec-flip-it-to-mobile-first","title":"Flip it to mobile first","kind":"Practice","text":"This page is built for the desktop and then undone for phones. Rewrite it to start with the phone."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The phone layout is usually the simplest: one column. Starting there means less CSS to undo later. Styles outside any media query apply everywhere. Media queries only add changes for wider screens. Use min-width queries, listed from smallest to largest. Add a breakpoint when the layout starts to look wrong as you widen the window, not at a specific device's width."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Designing for a laptop, then cramming it onto a phone with lots of max-width overrides. Only testing by shrinking the browser window. Check on a real phone too. Fixed widths like width: 960px on a wrapper, which cause sideways scrolling on phones. Hiding important content on mobile instead of rearranging it. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["mobile-first/quiz"],"exercises":["mobile-first/ex-mf"]},{"slug":"fluid-sizing","num":"4.5","title":"Fluid Sizing","desc":"Sizes that scale smoothly between limits with clamp(), min(), max() and calc().","props":["clamp()","min()","calc()"],"intro":"Media queries change things in jumps. clamp(), min(), max() and calc() let sizes change smoothly with the screen, inside limits you set, often with no media query at all.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-sizes-that-grow-smoothly","title":"Sizes that grow smoothly","kind":"Read","text":"Media queries change things in jumps: two columns, then suddenly three. For sizes like headings and padding, a smooth change often looks better. CSS has four functions that do math with sizes, so a value can grow with the screen inside limits you set. clamp() takes three values, always in this order: a minimum, a preferred value and a maximum. h1 { font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem); } The preferred value, 1rem + 3vw, grows as the window widens. clamp() keeps it between 28px and 56px, so the heading is never tiny on a phone or enormous on a big monitor. One line replaces several media queries. Mixing in rem also means the heading still grows when someone zooms in. min() and max() pick one of their values. width: min(100%, 600px) uses whichever is smaller, so the box fills the screen on phones but never grows past 600px. It works like max-width. max() sets a floor instead. The names feel backwards at first: min() sets an upper limit. calc() mixes units: calc(100% - 2rem) means \"the full width, minus 32px.\" Inside clamp(), min() and max() you can do math without writing calc(). Watch out calc(100%-2rem) is silently ignored. The + and - in calc() need a space on each side: calc(100% - 2rem). Play"},{"id":"sec-headings-that-grow-with-clamp","title":"Headings that grow with clamp()","kind":"Play","text":"clamp(MIN, PREFERRED, MAX) uses the preferred value, but never goes below the minimum or above the maximum. Drag the window width and compare three ways to size a heading. window width clamp( min, preferred, max ) min preferred: 1rem + ?vw max 1rem is 16px here. Keeping a rem in the preferred value means the heading still grows when someone zooms in. fixed remvw onlyclamp() Play"},{"id":"sec-columns-that-fit-with-min-and-max","title":"Columns that fit with min() and max()","kind":"Play","text":"min() picks the smallest of its values, max() the largest. They're perfect for \"this wide, but never wider than the screen\" and \"at least this much padding.\" page width .column { … } width: 640px max-width: 640px width: min(640px, 100% - 32px) padding 10px max(10px, 4vw) Play"},{"id":"sec-recipes-worth-keeping","title":"Recipes worth keeping","kind":"Play","text":"Copy these into your own stylesheet. Each one replaces a media query or two. Practice"},{"id":"sec-size-with-clamp-min-and-calc","title":"Size with clamp(), min() and calc()","kind":"Practice","text":"Replace fixed sizes with ones that grow smoothly, within limits."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem) means: grow with the window, but stay between 28px and 56px. calc() mixes units: calc(100% - 2rem) is \"full width, minus 32px.\" Inside clamp(), min() and max() you can do math without writing calc. min() sets an upper limit (like max-width), max() sets a lower limit (like min-width). The names feel backwards at first. These work anywhere a length works: font sizes, widths, padding, gap and grid tracks."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Leaving out the spaces: calc(100%-2rem) is ignored. The + and - need a space on each side. Sizing text in vw alone. It's tiny on phones, huge on big monitors, and doesn't grow when people zoom. Putting the values in the wrong order. clamp() is always min, preferred, max. Using vw for widths inside a page that has a scrollbar. 100vw includes the scrollbar, so it causes sideways scrolling. Use 100%. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["fluid-sizing/quiz"],"exercises":["fluid-sizing/ex-fluid"]},{"slug":"review-responsive","num":"4.6","title":"Review: Responsive Design","desc":"Build a responsive profile card. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole part together. Skim the recap, build a profile card that works from phone to desktop, then test yourself with mixed questions.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part was about one page working on every screen: layouts that change at breakpoints, images that fit and download efficiently, and sizes that grow smoothly. Media Queries: min-width queries add rules on wider screens, and later queries win when several apply. Responsive Images: max-width: 100% and height: auto keep images inside their containers; srcset offers smaller files. Picture & Lazy Loading: sizes tells the browser how wide an image will be, picture swaps the crop, and lazy loading waits. Mobile-First Workflow: Write the phone layout first, outside any query, then add for wider screens. Fluid Sizing: clamp(), min(), max() and calc() let sizes grow smoothly within limits. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-a-responsive-profile-card","title":"Mini project: a responsive profile card","kind":"Practice","text":"Build it mobile first, with an image and a heading that adapt to any width. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-responsive/quiz"],"exercises":["review-responsive/ex-project"]},{"slug":"transforms","num":"5.1","title":"Transforms","desc":"Move, rotate, scale and skew elements without disturbing the layout around them.","props":["translate","rotate","transform-origin"],"intro":"transform moves, turns, resizes or slants an element after the page is laid out. The element looks different, but the space it takes up doesn't change, so nothing around it moves.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-changing-how-a-box-looks-not-where-it-sits","title":"Changing how a box looks, not where it sits","kind":"Read","text":"A transform changes how an element is drawn after the page has been laid out. You can slide it, turn it, grow it or slant it: .badge { transform: translateY(-4px) rotate(-3deg) scale(1.1); } The four kinds of function are translate() (move), rotate() (turn, in deg), scale() (resize, where 1 is normal size) and skew() (slant). Each has X and Y versions, like translateX(). The key idea is that transforms don't affect layout. The browser keeps the element's original space reserved, so nothing around it moves. That's what makes transforms perfect for hover effects: a card can lift up 4px without pushing the rest of the page down. It's also why you shouldn't build layouts with them. A translated box leaves a gap where it was and overlaps whatever it lands on. You can list several functions in one transform, separated by spaces, and order matters: each function works in the direction left by the ones before it. Rotate and then translate, and the element moves along its new, tilted direction. transform-origin sets the point the element turns and scales around. The default is the center; transform-origin: top left makes it pivot from the corner, like a door on a hinge. Watch out A second transform replaces the first one completely. If a card has rotate(-2deg) and its hover state sets scale(1.05), the rotation disappears on hover. Repeat both: rotate(-2deg) scale(1.05). Play"},{"id":"sec-transform-a-card","title":"Transform a card","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the sliders and watch the dashed outline: that's the space the card still takes up in the layout. #box { transform } translateX translateY rotate scale skewX transform-origin center top left top right bottom left bottom center Reset sibling #box sibling Copy Order matters"},{"id":"sec-same-functions-different-order","title":"Same functions, different order","kind":"Order matters","text":"Transform functions run from right to left, and each one changes the direction the next one uses. Rotate first and \"move right\" points somewhere else. transform: translateX(140px) rotate(45deg); A Turn in place, then slide 140px right along the page. transform: rotate(45deg) translateX(140px); B Turn first, so \"right\" is now tilted 45° downward, and it slides along that tilted line. Play both In real sites"},{"id":"sec-four-everyday-uses","title":"Four everyday uses","kind":"In real sites","text":"Center anything centered .badge { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); } top/left: 50% puts the corner in the middle. The translate pulls it back by half its own size. Lift on hover Hover or Tab to me .card { transition: transform 0.2s; } .card:hover { transform: translateY(-6px); } Turn an arrow Trail conditions Icy above 7,000 feet. details[open] summary::before { transform: rotate(90deg); } Mirror an icon ➜➜ .icon-back { transform: scaleX(-1); } One arrow icon serves as both \"next\" and \"back.\" Practice"},{"id":"sec-transform-without-moving-the-layout","title":"Transform without moving the layout","kind":"Practice","text":"Tilt a stamp, lift a card on hover, and swing a door from its edge."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Transforms don't affect layout. Neighbors stay where they were, which is why they're perfect for hover effects and animation. List several functions in one transform, separated by spaces. A second transform declaration replaces the first entirely. transform-origin is the pin the element spins and scales around. The default is the center. translate percentages are a percentage of the element's own size, not its parent's. Transforms are smooth to animate. See Transitions & Animations."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Writing transform: rotate(10deg); and later transform: scale(1.1); on hover, and losing the rotation. Repeat both: rotate(10deg) scale(1.1). Transforming an inline element like a span. It's ignored: make it inline-block. Forgetting the unit: rotate(45) does nothing. Write 45deg. Using translate to build a layout. The original space stays reserved, so you get gaps and overlaps. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["transforms/quiz"],"exercises":["transforms/ex-transform"]},{"slug":"transitions","num":"5.2","title":"Transitions & Animations","desc":"Smooth state changes and keyframe animations, with timing curves.","props":["transition","@keyframes","ease"],"intro":"A transition smooths the change between two states, like on hover. An animation runs a sequence of keyframes by itself. Small, quick motion helps people notice changes; too much distracts.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-motion-between-states","title":"Motion between states","kind":"Read","text":"When a button changes color on hover, the change is instant by default. A transition tells the browser to blend from the old value to the new one over time: .btn { background: var(--trail); transition: background 200ms ease, transform 200ms ease; } .btn:hover { background: var(--trail-dark); transform: translateY(-2px); } Put the transition on the element's normal state, not on :hover, so it animates on the way in and the way out. Name the properties you mean rather than writing transition: all, which ends up animating things you didn't intend. A transition needs something to trigger it, like a hover or a class change. An animation runs by itself, through steps you describe with @keyframes: @keyframes pulse { from { transform: scale(1); } to { transform: scale(1.08); } } .new-badge { animation: pulse 800ms ease-in-out infinite alternate; } Keep interface motion short, about 150 to 400 milliseconds. Longer feels slow. Stick to animating transform and opacity where you can: the browser can draw those smoothly, while animating width or top makes it recalculate the layout on every frame. Some people get dizzy or sick from motion on screen, and they can tell their device to reduce it. Your CSS can listen for that setting with the prefers-reduced-motion media query. Tip Wrap big or looping motion in @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { … }. Then it only runs for people who haven't asked for less. Your device is set to reduce motion. This page plays its demos anyway so you can learn from them, but the rest of this site turns motion off for you. Your own sites should do the same with a prefers-reduced-motion media query. Play"},{"id":"sec-transitions","title":"Transitions","kind":"Play","text":"what changes position (left) color and shape size rotation (transform) transition-duration transition-timing-function ease linear ease-in ease-out ease-in-out back (overshoot) steps(5) transition-delay Change state Speed curve Same timing on hover Hover or Tab to me Play"},{"id":"sec-keyframe-animations","title":"Keyframe animations","kind":"Play","text":"animation-name pulse spin slide-in shake bounce animation-duration animation-iteration-count 1 3 infinite animation-direction normal alternate reverse Play animation box Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-motion-carefully","title":"Add motion carefully","kind":"Practice","text":"Smooth a hover, add a pulse, and respect visitors who've asked for less motion."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Put transition on the element's normal state, not the :hover state, so it animates both ways. Most interface motion should take 150–400ms. Longer feels sluggish. transform and opacity animate smoothly. Animating width, top or left makes the browser redo layout every frame. Wrap big motion in @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) so people who get motion sick are spared."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Trying to transition display: none. It can't fade; use opacity instead. Writing transition: all and animating things you didn't mean to. Forgetting the unit: transition-duration: 300 does nothing. Write 300ms or 0.3s. Infinite animations on content people are trying to read. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["transitions/quiz"],"exercises":["transitions/ex-motion"]},{"slug":"form-styling","num":"5.3","title":"Styling Forms","desc":"Turn a default form into a polished one, one layer at a time.","props":["font: inherit","accent-color",":user-invalid"],"intro":"Browser-default forms look cramped and inconsistent. A handful of rules fixes most of it. Add them one layer at a time and watch the same form improve.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-forms-that-feel-like-part-of-the-page","title":"Forms that feel like part of the page","kind":"Read","text":"Browsers style form controls their own way, and a little differently in each browser. The results look cramped and out of place. A handful of rules fixes most of it. Start with fonts. Inputs, selects, text areas and buttons don't inherit the page's font the way other elements do, so they show up smaller, in the browser's default face. One line brings them in line: input, select, textarea, button { font: inherit; } Next, layout. Put each label above its input and make the input full width. That's the easiest arrangement to scan, and it works on a phone without any media queries. Add padding so the text isn't pressed against the edge, and set box-sizing: border-box so width: 100% plus padding doesn't poke out of the container. accent-color recolors checkboxes, radio buttons and range sliders to match your site in one line. Then the states. Visitors need to see which field they're in, so give :focus-visible a clear outline rather than removing it. For errors, :user-invalid matches a field that breaks its rules, such as a required field left empty, but only after the visitor has interacted with it. Unlike :invalid, it won't paint the whole form red before anyone has typed a thing. Watch out Don't show errors with color alone. Some visitors can't tell red from green. Add a text message next to the field explaining what's wrong. Play"},{"id":"sec-improve-the-form-one-layer-at-a-time","title":"Improve the form one layer at a time","kind":"Play","text":"Step through the layers and watch the same form get easier to use. Try the fields at each step. Name Please enter your name. Email That doesn't look like an email address. Favorite trail Royal Arch Mesa Trail Chautauqua Loop Message Optional. Tell us about your last hike. Email me when new trails are added Join the club Practice"},{"id":"sec-style-the-form","title":"Style the form","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix the fonts, the overflow and the missing focus style."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Inputs, selects and buttons don't inherit fonts. Start every form with font: inherit. Put labels above inputs. It's easiest to scan and works on narrow screens. accent-color recolors checkboxes, radios and range sliders in one line. :user-invalid only shows errors after someone has interacted with a field, not as soon as the page loads."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Removing the focus outline and replacing it with nothing. width: 100% plus padding without box-sizing: border-box, so inputs poke out of their container. Showing errors in red only. Add a text message too, for people who can't tell the colors apart. Tiny checkboxes and buttons that are hard to tap on a phone. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["form-styling/quiz"],"exercises":["form-styling/ex-formstyle"]},{"slug":"accessibility","num":"5.4","title":"Accessibility Basics","desc":"Seven common problems on one page, and how to find and fix them.","props":["keyboard","focus","contrast"],"intro":"An accessible site works for people using a keyboard, a screen reader, zoom, or a phone in bright sun. This sample page has seven common problems. Click into it and press Tab to move through it, then compare it with the fixed version.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-building-for-everyone","title":"Building for everyone","kind":"Read","text":"Accessibility means people can use your site however they use the web: with a mouse, a keyboard, a screen reader that reads the page aloud, zoomed in to 200%, or on a phone in bright sun. Plenty of visitors depend on these, and an accessible site is easier for everyone else, too. Most of the work is done by choosing the right HTML element. A real <button> can be reached with Tab, pressed with Enter or Space, and is announced as \"button.\" A <div> with a click handler does none of that. The same goes for links, headings, lists and form labels: semantic HTML brings accessibility with it for free. Four checks catch most problems: Keyboard. Put your mouse away and press Tab through the page. Can you reach and use everything? Can you see where you are? Never remove the focus outline without adding a replacement. Names. Every control needs a name a screen reader can say: a label for each input, text inside each button, alt text on each meaningful image. Color. Text needs enough contrast with its background, at least 4.5 to 1 for normal text. And don't use color alone to carry meaning, like red for errors. Structure. Use one <h1> and headings in order, so people can jump around the page by heading. Tip Placeholder text isn't a label. It disappears as soon as someone starts typing, and it's usually too pale to read. Use a real <label>. Play"},{"id":"sec-find-the-seven-problems","title":"Find the seven problems","kind":"Play","text":"Click into the sample page and press Tab to move through it. Then switch to the fixed version and compare. Broken version Fixed version Keyboard log: what Tab reaches Clear Click the sample page, then press Tab. Your turn"},{"id":"sec-five-checks-for-your-own-project","title":"Five checks for your own project","kind":"Your turn","text":"Unplug your mouseUse Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter and Space. Can you reach and use everything? Can you always see where you are? Zoom to 200%Press Ctrl or Cmd and + a few times. Nothing should overlap or get cut off. Check contrastBody text needs at least 4.5 : 1 against its background. The Colors & Units page can check it. Read the headings aloneDo h1, h2 and h3 make a sensible outline of the page, in order? Turn images offDoes the alt text tell you what you'd miss? See the Images & Alt Text page. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-barriers","title":"Fix the barriers","kind":"Practice","text":"This sign-up box has four problems that lock people out. Fix each one."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Use the right HTML element first. Real buttons, links, labels and headings come with accessibility built in. Everything you can do with a mouse should work with a keyboard. Every control needs a name a screen reader can say: label text, button text or alt text. Don't use color alone to show meaning, like red for errors. Add text or an icon."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Clickable divs and spans. They can't be reached with Tab. outline: none with no replacement focus style. Placeholder text used as the only label. Pale gray text because it \"looks clean.\" Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["accessibility/quiz"],"exercises":["accessibility/ex-a11y"]},{"slug":"organizing-css","num":"5.5","title":"Organizing CSS","desc":"Sections, comments, purposeful class names and BEM, and variables.","props":["comments","BEM","naming"],"intro":"A stylesheet that works today can still be a nightmare to change next week. Watch one messy stylesheet get cleaned up in four steps. It styles exactly the same page at every step; only how easy it is to work with changes.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-a-stylesheet-you-can-come-back-to","title":"A stylesheet you can come back to","kind":"Read","text":"CSS doesn't care how your file is arranged. You do, the next time you need to change something and can't find it. A few habits keep a stylesheet easy to work with as it grows. Sections, always in the same order. A common order runs from general to specific: CSS variables first, then base styles for plain elements like body and a, then layout, then components like cards and buttons, then small utility classes. Mark each section with a comment heading. Because later rules win ties, this order also means the specific stuff naturally overrides the general. /* ===== Base ===== */ body { font-family: var(--font-body); } /* ===== Components ===== */ .trail-card { … } Names that describe purpose. .trail-card still makes sense after the redesign; .blue-box doesn't, once the box turns green. A popular naming pattern called BEM spells out how pieces relate: .card, .card__title, .card--featured. No repeated values. If you've typed the same color three times, make it a variable. Changing it later becomes a one-line edit. Low, even specificity. Style with single classes. IDs and long chains like #main .content ul li a are hard to override, which leads to even longer selectors, and eventually !important. Tip Delete rules you no longer use. Keeping them \"just in case\" makes every future change slower. If you need them back, they're in your version history. Play"},{"id":"sec-clean-up-a-stylesheet","title":"Clean up a stylesheet","kind":"Play","text":"Step through four cleanups. The page looks identical at every step; only the stylesheet changes. Practice"},{"id":"sec-where-does-this-rule-go","title":"Where does this rule go?","kind":"Practice","text":"A common order for a stylesheet: base styles for plain elements, then layout, then components, then small utility classes. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-better-class-name","title":"Pick the better class name","kind":"Practice","text":"Name classes for what something is, not how it looks. Looks change; purpose rarely does. Practice"},{"id":"sec-clean-up-the-stylesheet","title":"Clean up the stylesheet","kind":"Practice","text":"Rename a class by its purpose, store the repeated color once, and drop a heavy selector."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Group rules into sections with comment headings, always in the same order. Name classes after their purpose: .trail-card, not .blue-box. BEM is a popular naming pattern: .block, .block__element, .block--variation, like .card__title and .btn--large. Any color or size you type more than twice should probably be a CSS variable. Prefer classes over IDs and long chains of selectors. Low, even specificity is easier to override."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Adding every new rule to the bottom of the file. Names like .box2, .new or .left that stop making sense later. The same rule written twice in different places, with slightly different values. Leaving old, unused rules in \"just in case.\" Delete them; that's what version control is for."}],"quizzes":["organizing-css/quiz1","organizing-css/quiz2"],"exercises":["organizing-css/ex-organize"]},{"slug":"devtools","num":"5.6","title":"Debugging with DevTools","desc":"Read the Styles pane, spot crossed-out rules, and fix three real bugs.","props":["Inspect","Styles","box model"],"intro":"Every browser has developer tools that show exactly which CSS rules apply to an element and which ones lost. This practice version works like the real Elements panel. Use it to fix three bugs on the trail card.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-seeing-what-the-browser-sees","title":"Seeing what the browser sees","kind":"Read","text":"When your CSS doesn't do what you expect, guessing and reloading is slow. Every browser comes with DevTools, a set of panels that show exactly what the browser built from your code and why. Right-click anything on a page and choose Inspect. The Elements panel has two halves. On the left is the element tree: the page as the browser actually built it, which isn't always what you wrote. On the right, the Styles pane lists every rule that matches the selected element, with the winning rules at the top. Three things in the Styles pane solve most bugs: Crossed-out declarations lost to a stronger or later rule. Look higher in the list to find the winner, then fix the specificity or order. The file name and line number next to each rule tell you exactly where to make the fix. \"user agent stylesheet\" marks the browser's built-in defaults. You can't edit those, but your own CSS can override them. This is where mystery margins on headings and lists come from. If a rule you wrote doesn't appear at all, its selector doesn't match, or the stylesheet didn't load. Look for a typo or a broken link. You can also edit any value right in the Styles pane and see the result immediately. The box model diagram at the bottom shows the element's real margin, border, padding and size. Watch out Changes made in DevTools disappear when you reload. Once a fix works, copy it into your real CSS file. Play"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-trail-card","title":"Fix the trail card","kind":"Play","text":"This practice panel works like the real Elements panel. Complete the three missions. localhost/trail.html Royal Arch 3.4 miles · Hard · 1,400 ft of gain to a sandstone arch above Boulder. Save trail ElementsConsoleSourcesNetwork The real thing"},{"id":"sec-opening-devtools-in-your-browser","title":"Opening DevTools in your browser","kind":"The real thing","text":"Inspect one elementRight-click it on the page and choose Inspect. DevTools opens with that element selected. Open DevToolsF12 or Ctrl+Shift+I on Windows. Cmd+Option+I on a Mac. In Safari, turn on \"Show features for web developers\" in Settings first. Test on a phone sizeClick the device toolbar icon (a phone and tablet) at the top of DevTools to preview any screen width. Changes aren't savedEdits in DevTools disappear when you reload. Once you've found the fix, copy it into your CSS file. Practice"},{"id":"sec-debug-it-with-devtools","title":"Debug it with DevTools","kind":"Practice","text":"Three styles aren't applying. Right-click the preview and choose Inspect to find out why."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The Styles pane lists every rule that matches, with the winning ones at the top. A crossed-out declaration lost to a stronger or later rule. Look above it to find the winner. The file name and line number next to each rule tell you exactly where to fix it. \"user agent stylesheet\" means the browser's built-in defaults. You can't edit them, but your own CSS can override them. The box model diagram shows an element's real margin, border, padding and size."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Fixing the problem in DevTools and forgetting to copy the fix into the real file. Searching your CSS for a rule that doesn't exist, when it's the browser's default. Not noticing a rule is missing completely, which usually means a typo in the selector or a stylesheet that didn't load. Debugging the wrong element. Check which element is selected in the tree. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["devtools/quiz"],"exercises":["devtools/ex-devtools"]},{"slug":"validation","num":"5.7","title":"Validating Your Code","desc":"Use the W3C validators, spot common errors, and see how browsers repair bad HTML.","props":["W3C","errors","Elements panel"],"intro":"Browsers quietly guess their way around mistakes, so broken code often looks fine until it suddenly doesn't. A validator reads your code like a strict teacher and lists every problem with a line number.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-a-second-pair-of-eyes","title":"A second pair of eyes","kind":"Read","text":"Browsers are forgiving. If you forget a closing tag or misspell a property, they don't show an error. They guess what you meant and keep going. That sounds helpful, but it means broken code often looks fine in your browser until something unrelated changes, or until someone opens it in a different browser that guesses differently. A validator doesn't guess. It reads your code against the rules and lists every problem with a line number. There are free ones for HTML and CSS, and you can paste code in or upload a file. Validators sort their findings into two groups. Errors break the rules and should be fixed. Warnings are suggestions, worth reading but not always wrong. Always fix from the top down. One early mistake, like an unclosed <div>, can make everything after it look wrong to the validator, producing a long list of errors that disappear once you fix the first one. Fix it, validate again, and repeat. For CSS, the browser simply skips any declaration it doesn't understand. There's no message; the style just doesn't apply. That silent skip is exactly the bug you're hunting, and the CSS validator will point straight at it. When HTML is broken, the browser repairs it in ways that can surprise you, such as moving a <div> out of a <p>. DevTools shows the repaired version, so comparing it with your file reveals what the browser changed. Tip Validate before handing in any project, and any time something looks wrong and you can't see why. It takes a minute and catches mistakes your eyes skip over. Play"},{"id":"sec-the-tools","title":"The tools","kind":"Play","text":"Bookmark these. Then try spotting errors yourself below. HTML W3C Markup Validator Choose \"Validate by Direct Input.\" Paste your whole HTML file. Click Check. CSS W3C CSS Validator Choose \"By direct input.\" Paste your stylesheet. Click Check. How to read the results Fix the first error first. One missing bracket can cause ten more errors below it. Go to the line number it gives, then look at the line just above it too. Check again after each fix. Practice"},{"id":"sec-spot-the-error","title":"Spot the error","kind":"Practice","text":"Each snippet has exactly one line with a mistake. Click the line you think is wrong. HTML CSS Debugging HTML"},{"id":"sec-what-the-browser-actually-built","title":"What the browser actually built","kind":"Debugging HTML","text":"When HTML is broken, the browser silently repairs it, sometimes in surprising ways. DevTools' Elements panel shows the repaired version, not your file. Pick a snippet: the right side is your own browser's real result. Your HTML file What the Elements panel shows To see this in your own project Right-click the problem area and choose Inspect. In the Elements panel, compare the tree to your file. Wherever they differ, that's where your HTML has an error. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-what-the-validator-found","title":"Fix what the validator found","kind":"Practice","text":"A validator flagged these errors. The page looks fine, but each one is a real bug."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Validate before you hand in work, and whenever something looks wrong and you can't see why. \"Errors\" must be fixed. \"Warnings\" are suggestions worth reading. Indenting your code consistently makes missing closing tags and braces much easier to spot. Your code editor's highlighting is a validator too: if colors suddenly look wrong partway down, the mistake is just above."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Fixing errors from the bottom of the list up. Assuming code is fine because it looks right in one browser. Validating a single section instead of the whole file, which creates fake errors about missing tags. Ignoring the CSS validator because \"CSS just skips bad lines.\" That skipping is exactly the bug. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["validation/quiz"],"exercises":["validation/ex-validate"]},{"slug":"publishing","num":"5.8","title":"Project Structure & Publishing","desc":"Name and organize files, then publish with GitHub Pages.","props":["index.html","folders","GitHub Pages"],"intro":"A tidy folder and sensible file names prevent most \"it works on my laptop\" bugs. Clean up this messy project, then follow the steps to put a site online with GitHub Pages.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-from-your-laptop-to-the-web","title":"From your laptop to the web","kind":"Read","text":"On your own computer, a messy folder mostly works. Once it's on a web server, small sloppiness turns into broken pages. A tidy structure and sensible file names prevent most \"it works on my laptop\" bugs. Start with the home page. It must be called index.html and sit at the top of the project folder. When someone visits your site's address without a file name, the server sends index.html automatically. Group everything else by type: my-site/ index.html about.html css/style.css images/flatirons.jpg File names should be lowercase, with hyphens instead of spaces: trail-map.html, not Trail Map.HTML. Most web servers treat Photo.jpg and photo.jpg as different files, even though your laptop may not. That's the most common reason images show up locally and break online. Link with relative paths like images/flatirons.jpg, never paths to your Desktop or Downloads folder. Those only exist on your computer. GitHub Pages hosts plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript sites for free. You put your project in a repository, turn on Pages in its settings, and a minute later your site is live at username.github.io/repository-name. Watch out Upload the contents of your project folder, not the folder itself. Otherwise index.html ends up one level too deep and the site's address shows a 404. Play"},{"id":"sec-clean-up-a-messy-project","title":"Clean up a messy project","kind":"Play","text":"Pick the best new name for each file. The folder on the right updates as you go. Naming rules My Page.htmlmy-page.html Photo.JPGphoto.jpg final (2).cssstyle.css IMG_2041.jpgroyal-arch.jpg Lowercase, dashes instead of spaces, no special characters, and names that say what's inside. Play"},{"id":"sec-publish-with-github-pages","title":"Publish with GitHub Pages","kind":"Play","text":"GitHub Pages hosts plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript sites for free. Make a GitHub accountPick your username carefully. It becomes part of your site's address. Create a new repositoryClick New, give it a short lowercase name like trail-site, set it to Public, and create it. Upload your filesClick \"Add file\" then \"Upload files.\" Drag in the contents of your project folder so index.html sits at the top level, then click Commit changes. Turn on PagesGo to Settings, then Pages. Under \"Build and deployment,\" choose \"Deploy from a branch,\" pick main and / (root), and click Save. Wait, then visitIt takes a minute or two. Refresh the Pages settings to see your link. After each change you upload, give it another minute. Where will my site be? GitHub username Repository name If it doesn't work I get a 404 page Make sure there's a file named exactly index.html (all lowercase) at the top of the repository, not inside another folder. Then wait a couple of minutes and refresh. The page loads but has no styles or images Check your paths. Capitalization must match exactly, and paths starting with / point to the wrong place on project sites. Use relative paths like css/style.css. My changes don't show up Wait a minute after committing, then do a hard refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R, or Cmd+Shift+R on a Mac) so your browser doesn't show an old cached copy. It shows my README instead of my site GitHub Pages couldn't find index.html, so it fell back to the README. Check the file name and that it's at the top level. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-paths-before-publishing","title":"Fix the paths before publishing","kind":"Practice","text":"These links work on the author's laptop and nowhere else. Fix them to match the cleaned-up project."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The home page is always named index.html. Servers open it automatically. Group files by type: css/, images/, and subfolders for big sections. Keep one project in one folder, and open that folder in your code editor. Test the published site, not just your local copy. Servers are stricter about file names."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Uploading the project folder itself, so the site ends up at /my-project/index.html one level too deep. Image paths that point to your Desktop or Downloads folder. Two copies of a file, like style.css and style (1).css, and editing the wrong one. Making the repository private on a free account, which turns Pages off. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["publishing/quiz"],"exercises":["publishing/ex-publish"]},{"slug":"review-polish","num":"5.9","title":"Review: Polish & Workflow","desc":"Polish a sign-up section until it's accessible, consistent and smooth. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole part together. Skim the recap, polish a rough sign-up section, then test yourself with mixed questions.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part was about the difference between a page that works and one that's finished: motion, forms, accessibility, tidy code, and the tools that catch mistakes before visitors do. Transforms: Transforms move, rotate and scale without affecting layout. Transitions & Animations: Transitions smooth changes between states; animations run keyframes; respect reduced motion. Styling Forms: font: inherit, border-box inputs and a visible focus style fix most forms. Accessibility Basics: Real buttons and labels, keyboard access, enough contrast, and never color alone. Organizing CSS: Sections in a consistent order, names that describe purpose, variables, and low specificity. Debugging with DevTools: The Styles pane shows which rules apply, which lost, and where each one lives. Validating Your Code: Validators catch mistakes browsers silently repair. Fix from the top down. Project Structure & Publishing: index.html at the top, lowercase file names, relative paths, then GitHub Pages. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-polish-a-sign-up-section","title":"Mini project: polish a sign-up section","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix accessibility, form styling, motion and code quality in one component. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-polish/quiz"],"exercises":["review-polish/ex-project"]},{"slug":"js-intro","num":"6.1","title":"What Is JavaScript?","desc":"What JavaScript adds to HTML and CSS, and where the script tag goes so your code can find the page.","props":["<script>","defer","type=\"module\""],"intro":"HTML gives a page its structure, CSS gives it style, and JavaScript gives it behavior. Turn each layer on and off to see what it adds, then find out where the <script> tag needs to go.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-behavior-layer","title":"The behavior layer","kind":"Read","text":"A web page is built in three layers. HTML says what's on the page: a heading, a button, a form. CSS says how it looks. JavaScript says what it does: what happens when someone clicks the button, types in the form, or scrolls to the bottom. JavaScript is a programming language, and it runs right inside the browser. It can change text and styles after the page has loaded, show and hide things, check a form before it's sent, and fetch new data without reloading the page. Every interactive part of this textbook, from the quizzes to the sidebar, is JavaScript. Like CSS, JavaScript belongs in its own file, linked from the HTML: <head> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"> <script defer src=\"script.js\"></script> </head> That defer attribute matters. The browser reads your HTML from top to bottom, and without defer, it stops at the script and runs it straight away. A script in the <head> would run before the browser has even reached the <body>, so it can't find the button it's supposed to work with. defer says: download this now, but wait to run it until the whole page has been read. You'll also see type=\"module\", which waits in the same way. It's what MDN recommends, but it only works when the page is served by a web server, not when you double-click the file to open it. Tip JavaScript has nothing to do with Java. The similar name was a marketing decision in 1995, and it has confused people ever since. Play"},{"id":"sec-peel-back-the-layers","title":"Peel back the layers","kind":"Play","text":"Turn each layer off and on to see what it adds. Then try the script tag in different places. Layers HTMLStructure: there is a button, and it says something. CSSPresentation: colors, font, border, shape. JavaScriptBehavior: clicking the button lets you change the name. Each layer builds on the one before it. JavaScript can't add behavior to a button that doesn't exist. Play"},{"id":"sec-where-does-the-script-tag-go","title":"Where does the script tag go?","kind":"Play","text":"A script often uses elements on the page. If it runs before the browser has read those elements, it finds nothing and fails. Pick a placement and see what happens as the page loads. Placement <script src> in <head> <script defer src> in <head> <script> at the end of <body> type=\"module\", opened from your computer type=\"module\", on a web server Practice"},{"id":"sec-your-first-lines-of-javascript","title":"Your first lines of JavaScript","kind":"Practice","text":"Make the console say something. Press Run to see the output, then Check my work."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"JavaScript is a programming language that runs in the browser. It can change content, react to clicks and typing, and fetch new data without reloading the page. Put JavaScript in its own file and link it: <script defer src=\"script.js\"></script>. Keeping HTML, CSS and JS in separate files keeps each one easy to read. defer downloads the script while the HTML loads, then runs it once the whole page has been read. type=\"module\" works the same way and is what MDN recommends, but it only runs on a web server (or a local server), not from a file you double-clicked. Comments: // one line or /* several lines */."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A plain <script src> in the head with no defer: the elements don't exist yet, so you get null errors. Inline handlers like <button onclick=\"go()\">. MDN calls this bad practice. Use addEventListener in your JS file instead. Writing <script src=\"script.js\" />. A script tag always needs a closing </script>. A wrong path in src. Nothing happens, and the console shows a 404 for the file."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"What is JavaScript?The layer cake, adding scripts, and loading strategies. The <script> elementEvery attribute, including defer, async and type. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-intro/quiz"],"exercises":["js-intro/ex-first"]},{"slug":"js-errors","num":"6.2","title":"The Console & Errors","desc":"Read error messages, find the line that's really broken, and debug with console.log().","props":["console.log()","TypeError","SyntaxError"],"intro":"When JavaScript breaks, the page usually just stops working with no message. The explanation is in the browser's console. This guessing game has five bugs: use the console to track them down.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-where-javascript-tells-you-what-went-wrong","title":"Where JavaScript tells you what went wrong","kind":"Read","text":"When HTML or CSS has a mistake, you can usually see it: something's in the wrong place or the wrong color. When JavaScript breaks, the page often just stops responding, with no message at all. The explanation is waiting in the browser's console. Open DevTools and click the Console tab. Errors come in three kinds: A syntax error is a typo in the grammar of the code, like a missing bracket or quote. The browser can't understand the file, so none of the script runs. A runtime error happens while the code is running, like calling something that isn't a function. The script runs up to that line, then stops. A logic error shows no message at all. The code runs fine, but does the wrong thing: the score goes down instead of up. Every error message tells you what went wrong and where. game.js:21:13 means line 21, character 13. Click it and the browser jumps straight to that line. Read the message slowly: \"guess is not defined\" tells you that on that line, the name guess doesn't exist. Usually it's misspelled, or declared somewhere else. Fix the first error first. One error stops the code after it from running, so later problems can't even show up yet. Fix it, reload, and see what's next. For logic errors, use console.log() to print values as the code runs. Is the number what you expected? Is that line running at all? Each answer narrows down where the bug is hiding. Tip Label what you log: console.log(\"guess:\", guess). When there are five values in the console, you'll want to know which is which. Play"},{"id":"sec-debug-the-guessing-game","title":"Debug the guessing game","kind":"Play","text":"Play the game, then open the console to find the five bugs. Test the game 1. Load the page 2. Guess 50 and submit 3. Play 4 guesses: 50, 25, 12, 6 4. Add console.log(randomNumber), reload 5 times Open the console in your own browser with F12, or ⌥⌘J on a Mac (Chrome). In Safari, turn on Show features for web developers first. Start over with all bugs Run a test, read the console, then click the line of code you think is broken. Play"},{"id":"sec-error-message-decoder","title":"Error message decoder","kind":"Play","text":"These are the errors you'll see most often in Chrome. Firefox and Safari word some of them differently, but the type at the start (SyntaxError, TypeError, ReferenceError) is the same everywhere. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-it-yourself","title":"Fix it yourself","kind":"Practice","text":"This code has three bugs. Run it, read the first error, fix that one, and run it again. When it works, the console prints three lines. Practice"},{"id":"sec-debug-the-script","title":"Debug the script","kind":"Practice","text":"This script has a syntax error, a runtime error and a logic error. Fix them one at a time, starting with the first message."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Syntax errors are typos in the grammar of the code: a missing bracket or quote. The whole script won't run. Runtime errors happen while the code runs, such as calling something that isn't a function. Code inside a function only fails when that function is called. Logic errors don't show any message. The code runs but does the wrong thing. Use console.log() to check values as the code runs. Every error message tells you what went wrong and where: game.js:21:13 means line 21, character 13. Fix the first error first. One error often stops the rest of the code from running, so later errors can't appear yet."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Assuming the error is on the line it names. That's where the code crashed. The real cause can be earlier, such as a selector that matched nothing. Wrong capitalization: addeventListener, queryselector, textcontent. JavaScript is case-sensitive. Using = (assign) when you mean === (compare) inside an if. Forgetting to reload the page after saving, then debugging the old code."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"What went wrong? Troubleshooting JavaScriptThe same guessing game, with its bugs explained step by step. JavaScript error referenceAn explanation for every error message. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-errors/quiz"],"exercises":["js-errors/ex-debug"]},{"slug":"js-variables","num":"6.3","title":"Variables & Data Types","desc":"let vs const, naming rules, and the difference between 42 and \"42\".","props":["let","const","typeof"],"intro":"A variable is a labeled box that holds a value. let boxes can be refilled; const boxes can't. Step through the code and watch the boxes change.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-giving-values-a-name","title":"Giving values a name","kind":"Read","text":"A program needs to remember things: a player's score, a visitor's name, whether the menu is open. A variable is a name that holds a value, so you can use it later. const trail = \"Mesa Trail\"; let miles = 6.7; miles = miles + 1; There are two ways to make one. const creates a variable that can't be given a new value. let creates one that can. Use const by default, and let only when the value really needs to change, like a counter or a score. That way, anyone reading your code knows which values move. You declare a variable once, with let or const. After that, use just its name. Writing let miles a second time is an error. Names can contain letters, numbers, _ and $, but can't start with a number or contain spaces. JavaScript developers write multi-word names in camelCase: trailName, isOpen. Names are case-sensitive, so score and Score are two different variables. Every value has a type. The main ones are numbers (42, 6.7), strings of text in quotes (\"Mesa\"), and booleans, which are just true or false. Arrays and objects hold groups of values, and you'll meet them soon. typeof tells you a value's type. Watch out Quotes always make a string. \"42\" is text that happens to look like a number, and that difference causes surprising bugs, as the next lesson shows. Play"},{"id":"sec-watch-the-boxes-change","title":"Watch the boxes change","kind":"Play","text":"Step through the code one line at a time and watch each variable's value. ◂ Back Run next line ▸ Restart Variables in memory Play"},{"id":"sec-name-checker","title":"Name checker","kind":"Play","text":"Some names break your code, and others just make it hard to read. Type a variable name, or try one of the examples. Variable name Try it"},{"id":"sec-typeof","title":"typeof","kind":"Try it","text":"typeof tells you what type a value is. Quotes always make a string, even when the value looks like a number. Change the values and run it again. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-const-or-let","title":"Pick const or let","kind":"Practice","text":"Each of these variables is declared the wrong way. Fix them so the code runs and says what it means."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Use const when you can, and let when you have to. Use let only when the value needs to change, such as a score or a counter. Declare a variable once with let or const, then use just its name afterward. The main types: number (42, 3.4), string (\"text\"), boolean (true/false), plus arrays and objects for groups of values. JavaScript is dynamically typed: the value decides the type, and a let variable can hold a different type later. A const array or object can still have its contents changed. What can't change is which array or object the name refers to."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using var. It's the old way, with confusing rules. MDN says there's no reason to use it in new code. Writing let again when updating a value: let score = 1; then let score = 2; is an error. Numbers in quotes: \"5\" + 5 gives \"55\", not 10. Mixing up case: myAge and myage are two different variables."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Storing the information you need: Variableslet, const, naming rules and types. typeofEvery possible typeof result. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-variables/quiz"],"exercises":["js-variables/ex-vars"]},{"slug":"js-strings-numbers","num":"6.4","title":"Numbers & Strings","desc":"Math operators, why \"5\" + 5 is \"55\", template literals and string methods.","props":["+","`${ }`",".slice()"],"intro":"Math works the way you'd expect, until a number turns out to be text. Mix numbers and strings below to see when + adds and when it joins.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-when-adds-and-when-it-joins","title":"When + adds, and when it joins","kind":"Read","text":"Numbers work the way you'd expect: +, -, * and /, with parentheses to control the order. Two extra operators are handy. % gives the remainder after dividing, so 7 % 2 is 1, and ** raises to a power. The trouble starts with +, because it does two jobs. With two numbers, it adds. If either side is a string, it joins them instead, which is called concatenation: 5 + 5 // 10 \"5\" + 5 // \"55\" \"5\" * 2 // 10, because * always tries to make numbers This matters because anything a visitor types into a form arrives as a string. Add 5 to an input's value of \"10\" and you get \"105\". Convert it first with Number(input.value). If the text isn't a number, you get NaN, short for \"not a number.\" To build a sentence from values, a template literal is easier than joining pieces. Use backticks instead of quotes, and put any value inside ${ }: const miles = 6.7; const msg = `Mesa Trail is ${miles} miles long.`; Strings come with built-in methods: .toUpperCase(), .includes(\"Mesa\"), .trim() and many more. Methods return a new string; the original never changes. .length is a property, not a method, so it has no parentheses. Tip (0.1 + 0.2) gives 0.30000000000000004. Computers store decimals approximately. When showing money or distances, round for display with .toFixed(2). Play"},{"id":"sec-add-or-join","title":"Add or join?","kind":"Play","text":"Change a and b, and switch between numbers and text, to see what + does. a number \"string\" b number \"string\" Operator Values typed into a form field are always strings. Click \"string\" on one side to see what happens if you forget to convert them. Play"},{"id":"sec-useful-string-methods","title":"Useful string methods","kind":"Play","text":"Every character in a string has an index, starting at 0. Pick a method to see what it returns. Methods return a new value; the original string never changes. const text = Try it"},{"id":"sec-template-literals-and-math","title":"Template literals and Math","kind":"Try it","text":"Backticks let you put variables right inside a string with ${ }. It's easier to read than joining strings with +. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-don-t-join","title":"Add, don't join","kind":"Practice","text":"A value from a form arrives as text, so + joins instead of adding. Fix the total and tidy the output."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Arithmetic: + - * /, % (remainder) and ** (power). Use parentheses to control the order. If either side of + is a string, it joins instead of adding. The other operators always try to convert to numbers. Convert text to a number with Number(\"12\"), and a number to text with String(12) or .toFixed(2). Shortcuts: score += 10, count++, count--. Template literals use backticks: `Hi ${name}`. They can also span several lines. .length is a property (no parentheses). .toUpperCase() is a method (with parentheses)."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Adding form values without converting them: \"2\" + \"3\" is \"23\". Using normal quotes for a template: \"Hi ${name}\" prints the ${name} literally. It needs backticks. Expecting a method to change the original: text.trim(); on its own does nothing useful. Save the result: text = text.trim(); Off-by-one indexes: the first character is [0], and the last is [text.length - 1]. Expecting 0.1 + 0.2 to be exactly 0.3. Round for display with .toFixed()."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Basic math in JavaScriptOperators, precedence, and increment and decrement. Handling text: stringsQuotes, escaping, and template literals. Useful string methodslength, indexes, slice, replace and more. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-strings-numbers/quiz"],"exercises":["js-strings-numbers/ex-strings"]},{"slug":"js-arrays","num":"6.5","title":"Arrays","desc":"Lists of values with zero-based indexes, and the methods that add, remove and search.","props":["[0]",".push()",".length"],"intro":"An array stores a list of values under one name, in order, with a numbered index starting at 0. Some methods change the array, and others just hand you back an answer. Try them below.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-a-list-under-one-name","title":"A list under one name","kind":"Read","text":"When you have several related values, like the trails in a guide, you don't want trail1, trail2, trail3. An array stores a whole list under one name, in order: const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\", \"Chautauqua\"]; trails[0] // \"Mesa\" trails.length // 3 trails[trails.length - 1] // \"Chautauqua\" Each item has an index, its position, and counting starts at 0. So the first item is [0], and the last is at length - 1. Asking for an index that doesn't exist gives undefined, not an error. To change an item, assign to its index: trails[1] = \"Flagstaff\". Arrays come with methods for adding and removing items. push() and pop() add and remove at the end. unshift() and shift() work at the start. splice() removes items from the middle. Some methods change the array itself; others leave it alone and hand you an answer. push() changes it. includes(\"Mesa\") just answers true or false, and slice() and join() return something new. Knowing which kind you're using saves a lot of confusion. Arrays can hold any type of value: numbers, strings, even objects or other arrays. And split() and join() convert between strings and arrays. Tip A const array can still change. const only means the name always points to the same array; you can still push and pop its contents. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-array","title":"Change the array","kind":"Play","text":"Try each method and watch which ones change the array and which just return a value. New item Add and remove Read and search Convert Reset array Try it"},{"id":"sec-pick-something-at-random","title":"Pick something at random","kind":"Try it","text":"MDN's silly story challenge uses this pattern to choose a random item from an array. Math.random() gives a number from 0 up to 1. Multiply it by the length and round down to get a valid index. Practice"},{"id":"sec-change-the-list","title":"Change the list","kind":"Practice","text":"Add and remove trails with array methods, then read from the end of the list."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Create an array with square brackets: const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"]; Read an item by its index: trails[0] is the first item. The last one is trails[trails.length - 1]. Change an item: trails[1] = \"Flagstaff\"; push() and pop() work at the end; unshift() and shift() work at the start. splice() removes from the middle. Arrays can hold any type, including other arrays and objects. Convert between strings and arrays with split() and join()."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Starting to count at 1. trails[1] is the second item. Reading trails[trails.length]. It's one past the end, so you get undefined. Saving what push() returns and expecting the array: const list = trails.push(\"x\"); gives the new length. Thinking indexOf() returns false when nothing is found. It returns -1. Use includes() for a true/false answer."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"ArraysCreating, reading, adding and removing items. Challenge: Silly story generatorPut variables, strings and arrays together. Array referenceEvery array method. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-arrays/quiz"],"exercises":["js-arrays/ex-arrays"]},{"slug":"js-conditionals","num":"6.6","title":"Conditionals","desc":"if, else if and else, comparing with ===, combining with && and ||, and truthy values.","props":["if / else","===","&&"],"intro":"An if statement lets code make decisions. JavaScript checks each condition from top to bottom, runs the first block that is true, and skips everything else.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-making-decisions","title":"Making decisions","kind":"Read","text":"Programs make choices: show the error or don't, say good morning or good evening. A conditional runs code only when something is true. if (temp >= 85) { msg = \"Hike early. It'll be hot.\"; } else if (temp >= 50) { msg = \"Perfect hiking weather.\"; } else { msg = \"Bring layers.\"; } JavaScript checks each condition from top to bottom and runs the first block whose condition is true. Then it skips everything else, even if later conditions would also be true. That's why order matters: put the most specific conditions first. If the >= 50 check came first, a 95-degree day would get \"perfect hiking weather.\" Compare with <, >, <=, >=, and === for \"equal.\" Always use three equals signs. The two-equals version converts types first, so \"5\" == 5 is true, which hides bugs. \"5\" === 5 is false. And remember that a single = assigns a value; it never compares. Combine conditions with && (both must be true), || (at least one must be true) and ! (flips true and false): if (isWeekend && !isRaining). A condition doesn't have to be a comparison. Any value works. Six values count as false: false, 0, \"\", null, undefined and NaN. These are called falsy, and everything else is truthy. So if (name) means \"if name isn't empty.\" Watch out if (x = 5) doesn't compare. It sets x to 5, and since 5 is truthy, the block always runs. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-temperature","title":"Change the temperature","kind":"Play","text":"Change the value and watch which branch runs. Then flip the order of the conditions. const temp = ; Put the conditions in the wrong order Play"},{"id":"sec-and-or-not","title":"AND, OR, NOT","kind":"Play","text":"Combine conditions with && (both must be true), || (at least one must be true) and ! (flips true and false). const isWeekend = ; const isRaining = ; Operator && || Play"},{"id":"sec-truthy-and-falsy","title":"Truthy and falsy","kind":"Play","text":"A condition doesn't have to be a comparison. Any value works: six values count as false, and everything else counts as true. This is how if (name) checks whether someone typed anything. const value = Try it"},{"id":"sec-the-ternary-and-switch","title":"===, the ternary and switch","kind":"Try it","text":"Always compare with ===. The ternary condition ? a : b is a short if/else that produces a value. switch checks one value against many exact matches. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-decision","title":"Fix the decision","kind":"Practice","text":"This function gives the wrong advice on hot days and ignores the rain. Reorder and add to it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"if (condition) { … } else if (other) { … } else { … }. Only the first block whose condition is true runs. Comparisons: === equal, !== not equal, <, >, <=, >=. Use ===, not ==. Triple equals also checks the type, so \"5\" === 5 is false. Order matters: put the most specific conditions first. Falsy values: false, 0, \"\", null, undefined, NaN. Everything else is truthy."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"if (x = 5): a single = assigns instead of comparing, so the condition is always true. if (day === \"Sat\" || \"Sun\"): each side needs its own comparison: day === \"Sat\" || day === \"Sun\". Putting temp < 60 before temp < 32. The second can never run. Forgetting break in a switch, so the code falls through into the next case. A semicolon after the condition, as in if (ok); {, makes the block always run."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Making decisions in your code: conditionalsif...else, logical operators, switch and the ternary operator. FalsyThe full list of falsy values. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-conditionals/quiz"],"exercises":["js-conditionals/ex-cond"]},{"slug":"js-loops","num":"6.7","title":"Loops","desc":"Step through for, for...of and while loops, then let map() and filter() do the looping.","props":["for...of","for",".map()"],"intro":"A loop runs the same code again and again, usually once for each item in a list. Step through each kind of loop to see exactly what runs, and in what order.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-doing-the-same-thing-for-every-item","title":"Doing the same thing for every item","kind":"Read","text":"Say you have a list of 50 trails and want to show each name. You could write console.log() 50 times, or write it once and let a loop repeat it. Loops are how programs work through lists: every product in a cart, every button on a page, every row of data from a server. JavaScript has several kinds of loop. You'll use these three most: for...of goes through every item in an array, one at a time. It's the simplest, so reach for it first. A classic for loop counts: start at 0, keep going while a condition is true, add 1 each time. Use it when you need the index, the item's position in the list. while repeats as long as its condition is true. Use it when you don't know in advance how many times to repeat. const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\", \"Flagstaff\"]; for (const trail of trails) { console.log(trail); } // Mesa // Royal Arch // Flagstaff Each pass through a loop is one iteration. Inside this loop, trail holds the current item: \"Mesa\" the first time, then \"Royal Arch\", then \"Flagstaff\". When the list runs out, the loop ends and the code after it runs. A loop can also build something up as it goes. Start a variable before the loop, like let total = 0;, and change it on every iteration. After the loop, it holds the result. You'll do exactly that in the practice below. Watch out Every loop needs a way to stop. A while loop whose condition never becomes false runs forever and freezes the page. If that happens, close the tab, then check what changes the condition on each pass. Play"},{"id":"sec-step-through-a-loop","title":"Step through a loop","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a loop and press Step to run it one piece at a time. The two \"bug\" versions show the most common loop mistakes. Loop for (classic) for...of while for with <= (bug) while without i++ (bug) Step ▸ Auto-play Restart Play"},{"id":"sec-map-and-filter","title":"map() and filter()","kind":"Play","text":"Arrays have methods that do the looping for you. You pass in a small function, and the method calls it once for each item. Try it"},{"id":"sec-break-and-continue","title":"break and continue","kind":"Try it","text":"break stops the loop completely. continue skips the rest of this pass and moves on to the next item. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-up-the-miles","title":"Add up the miles","kind":"Practice","text":"Adding up a list is one of the most common jobs for a loop: start a total at 0, then add each item to it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"for...of is the simplest way to go through every item: for (const trail of trails) { … }. Use it when you don't need the index. A classic for loop has three parts: (initializer; condition; final-expression). Use it when you need to count, or need the index. while repeats as long as its condition is true. Good when you don't know in advance how many times to repeat. map() makes a new array of changed items. filter() makes a new array of only the items that pass a test. querySelectorAll() results work with for...of too. That's how you add a listener to every button on a page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"i <= arr.length runs one time too many and reads undefined. Use <. Forgetting to change the counter in a while loop. The condition never becomes false, and the page freezes. Using const for a classic loop counter: for (const i = 0; …; i++) fails because i++ reassigns it. (const is fine in for...of.) Forgetting to return in a map() function that uses curly braces, which gives an array of undefined."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Looping codefor...of, map, filter, for, while, break and continue. Array.prototype.map() and filter()Full reference. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-loops/quiz"],"exercises":["js-loops/ex-total"]},{"slug":"js-functions","num":"6.8","title":"Functions","desc":"Parameters, arguments and return values, arrow functions, and where variables can be seen.","props":["function","return","=>"],"intro":"A function is a named, reusable block of code. You pass values in, it does some work, and it can hand a value back. Change the arguments and follow the values through the function.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-reusable-blocks-of-code","title":"Reusable blocks of code","kind":"Read","text":"A function is a named block of code that you can run whenever you need it, as many times as you like. You pass values in, it does some work, and it can hand a value back. function hikeTime(miles, pace = 2) { return miles / pace; } const hours = hikeTime(6, 3); // 2 The names in the definition, miles and pace, are parameters: placeholders waiting for values. The values you pass when you call it, 6 and 3, are arguments. pace = 2 is a default, used when no second argument is given. To run a function, you call it with parentheses: hikeTime(6, 3). Without the parentheses, hikeTime just refers to the function itself without running it, which is exactly what you want when you pass it to an event listener, but a bug everywhere else. return sends a value back to wherever the function was called, and ends the function right there. Forget the return and the function still runs, but its return value is undefined. You'll see functions written several ways. The arrow function is the short version: const hikeTime = (miles, pace = 2) => miles / pace;. It does the same thing. Variables declared inside a function only exist inside it. That's called scope, and it's a good thing: two functions can each have their own total without interfering with each other. Tip A good function does one job and is named with a verb that says what: hikeTime(), showError(), addTrail(). Play"},{"id":"sec-follow-the-values","title":"Follow the values","kind":"Play","text":"Change the arguments and follow each value through the function. Then try the two common mistakes. Arguments miles pace (leave empty to use the default) Common mistakes Forget the return Call it without () Play"},{"id":"sec-three-ways-to-write-a-function","title":"Three ways to write a function","kind":"Play","text":"All three do the same thing. You'll see all of them in code online. MDN recommends arrow functions for short functions, especially ones you pass to another function. Play"},{"id":"sec-scope-where-can-a-variable-be-seen","title":"Scope: where can a variable be seen?","kind":"Play","text":"A variable declared inside a function only exists inside that function. One declared outside every function (global) can be used anywhere. Click a cell to try using that variable from that place. Click a cell. Try it"},{"id":"sec-build-your-own-function","title":"Build your own function","kind":"Try it","text":"Change the arguments, add a default, or write a new function that calls these. Practice"},{"id":"sec-write-functions-that-return","title":"Write functions that return","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix a function that forgets to return, give it a default, and write a second one as an arrow function."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Parameters are the names in the definition: function hikeTime(miles, pace). Arguments are the values you pass in when you call it: hikeTime(6.7, 2.5). Calling a function needs parentheses. Without them, you're just referring to the function, not running it. return sends a value back to wherever the function was called, and ends the function right there. Default parameters fill in missing arguments: function greet(name = \"friend\"). Functions inside objects are called methods, like text.toUpperCase() or console.log()."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using console.log() where you meant return. The value is printed, but the caller gets undefined. Passing a function call instead of the function: btn.addEventListener(\"click\", go()) runs go right away. Use go with no parentheses. Using a variable declared inside a function from outside it: ReferenceError. Putting code after return and expecting it to run."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Functions: reusable blocks of codeParameters, defaults, anonymous and arrow functions, scope. Build your own functionA step-by-step exercise. Function return valuesHow return works, and why it matters. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-predict-the-output","title":"Predict the output","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-functions/quiz"],"exercises":["js-functions/ex-func"]},{"slug":"js-objects-json","num":"6.9","title":"Objects & JSON","desc":"Group values under named keys, read nested data, and spot invalid JSON.","props":["{ key: value }","obj.key","JSON.parse()"],"intro":"An object groups related values under named keys, such as a trail's name, length and location. JSON is the same idea written as text, and it's how most data travels between websites and servers.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-grouping-values-under-names","title":"Grouping values under names","kind":"Read","text":"An array is a list in order. An object groups related values under names, called keys, like everything you know about one trail: const hike = { name: \"Royal Arch\", miles: 3.4, dogs: true, tags: [\"steep\", \"views\"], }; hike.name // \"Royal Arch\" hike[\"miles\"] // 3.4 hike.tags[1] // \"views\" Read a value with a dot and the key's name. Square brackets with the key as a string do the same thing, and you need them when the key is stored in a variable: if key holds \"miles\", then hike[key] works but hike.key looks for a key literally named \"key\". Values can be anything, including arrays and other objects. Real data is often nested several levels deep, so read it one step at a time: trails[0].tags[1] means the first trail, then its tags, then the second tag. To add or change a value, assign it: hike.rating = 5. JSON is the same idea written as plain text. It's how most data travels between websites and servers. It has stricter rules than JavaScript: keys need double quotes, strings use double quotes too, and there are no trailing commas or comments. Because JSON is text, you convert it before using it. JSON.parse(text) turns JSON text into an object you can read with dots. JSON.stringify(obj) turns an object back into text, ready to save or send. Watch out One missing comma or a single quote makes JSON invalid, and JSON.parse() throws an error. If the error mentions an unexpected token, check the JSON text first. Play"},{"id":"sec-read-the-object","title":"Read the object","kind":"Play","text":"Click any key to see the code that reads it. Click a key to read it Play"},{"id":"sec-dot-or-brackets","title":"Dot or brackets?","kind":"Play","text":"When the key is stored in a variable (for example, chosen from a dropdown), you have to use square brackets. A dot always looks for a key with that exact name. const key = \"name\" \"miles\" \"open\" \"rating\" Play"},{"id":"sec-json-checker","title":"JSON checker","kind":"Play","text":"JSON looks like a JavaScript object but has stricter rules. Edit the text, or click a button to make a common mistake. The status uses the browser's own JSON.parse(). Break it Reset to valid JSON Keys and strings in \"double quotes\" No trailing commas No comments No functions or undefined Allowed: strings, numbers, true, false, null, arrays, objects Try it"},{"id":"sec-parse-and-stringify","title":"parse and stringify","kind":"Try it","text":"This is MDN's superheroes example. JSON.parse() turns JSON text into an object you can use. JSON.stringify() turns an object into text, for saving or sending. Practice"},{"id":"sec-read-and-write-json","title":"Read and write JSON","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn JSON text into an object, pull values out of it, and turn it back into text."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"An object is a set of key: value pairs in curly braces. Values can be anything, including arrays, other objects and functions (methods). Read values with a dot (hike.miles) or brackets (hike[\"miles\"]). Use brackets when the key is in a variable. Chain through nested data one step at a time: squad.members[1].powers[2]. Add or change a value just by assigning it: hike.rating = 5; JSON is text. JSON.parse(text) makes an object from it; JSON.stringify(obj) makes text from an object. fetch() responses have a .json() method that parses for you."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"hike.key when key is a variable. It looks for a key literally named \"key\". Use hike[key]. Single quotes, trailing commas or comments in a .json file. Any one of them makes the whole file fail to parse. Using JSON text as if it were an object: text.members is undefined until you parse it. Forgetting the commas between properties in an object literal."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Object basicsDot and bracket notation, methods, and this. Working with JSONThe JSON rules, the superheroes example, parse and stringify. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-objects-json/quiz"],"exercises":["js-objects-json/ex-objects"]},{"slug":"review-js","num":"6.10","title":"Review: JavaScript Basics","desc":"Write the functions behind a trail stats panel. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole part together. Skim the recap, write a set of functions that summarize trail data, then test yourself with mixed questions.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part was the JavaScript language itself: storing values, making decisions, repeating work and packaging it into functions. None of it touches the page yet; that's the next part. What Is JavaScript?: JavaScript adds behavior; link it with a script tag and defer. The Console & Errors: The console shows errors with a line number. Fix the first one first. Variables & Data Types: const by default, let when the value changes. Numbers & Strings: + joins if either side is a string; convert form values with Number(). Arrays: Arrays are ordered lists indexed from 0, with methods to add and remove items. Conditionals: if runs the first true branch only, so put specific conditions first, and compare with ===. Loops: for...of visits every item; a total starts at 0 and grows inside the loop. Functions: Parameters take values in, return sends one back, and scope keeps variables local. Objects & JSON: Objects group values under keys; JSON.parse and JSON.stringify convert to and from text. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-trail-stats","title":"Mini project: trail stats","kind":"Practice","text":"Write three functions that describe and summarize a list of trails. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-js/quiz"],"exercises":["review-js/ex-project"]},{"slug":"js-dom","num":"7.1","title":"Selecting & Changing Elements","desc":"Find elements with CSS selectors, explore the DOM tree, and change text, classes and styles.","props":["querySelector()","classList","textContent"],"intro":"The browser turns your HTML into a tree of objects called the DOM (Document Object Model). JavaScript finds elements in that tree with the same selectors you use in CSS, then changes them.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-page-as-javascript-sees-it","title":"The page as JavaScript sees it","kind":"Read","text":"When the browser reads your HTML, it builds a tree of objects in memory, one for each element. That tree is the DOM, the Document Object Model. JavaScript can't change your HTML file, but it can change the DOM, and the page updates immediately. The DOM uses the same family words as CSS. A parent element directly contains its children; children of the same parent are siblings; and anything inside an element, at any depth, is a descendant. To change an element, first find it. querySelector() takes any CSS selector and returns the first match: const title = document.querySelector(\".card h2\"); title.textContent = \"Mesa Trail (closed)\"; const cards = document.querySelectorAll(\".card\"); for (const card of cards) { card.classList.add(\"dimmed\"); } querySelectorAll() returns every match as a list you can loop over. If nothing matches, querySelector() returns null, and using null gives the error \"Cannot read properties of null.\" That almost always means a typo in your selector, or a script that ran before the element existed. textContent reads or replaces an element's text. For appearance, use classList: add(), remove() and toggle() switch classes on and off, while the actual styles stay in your CSS file, where they belong. You can also set el.style.backgroundColor directly; note the camelCase, since hyphens aren't allowed in JavaScript names. Tip Write the selector in your CSS file first and make sure it styles the right element. Then use the same selector in JavaScript. Play"},{"id":"sec-select-elements","title":"Select elements","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a method, type a selector and see what it matches. Method querySelector() querySelectorAll() getElementById() Selector Play"},{"id":"sec-the-dom-tree","title":"The DOM tree","kind":"Play","text":"This is the mini page above as the browser sees it. Matches from your selector are highlighted. Click any node to see how it relates to the others. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-what-you-selected","title":"Change what you selected","kind":"Play","text":"These act on whatever your selector matched in Part 1. Try them on .featured, then on h2 with querySelectorAll. Reset the mini page Pick a change. Practice"},{"id":"sec-change-a-card-with-the-dom","title":"Change a card with the DOM","kind":"Practice","text":"Write a function that finds parts of a trail card and updates them."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"document.querySelector(\".card\") returns the first match, or null if nothing matches. document.querySelectorAll(\".card\") returns every match as a NodeList. Loop over it with for...of. Change text with el.textContent = \"…\". Prefer el.classList.add(), .remove() and .toggle() with styles in your CSS file over setting el.style directly. It keeps CSS and JS separate. In el.style, CSS property names become camelCase: background-color → backgroundColor. Family words: a parent directly contains its children; siblings share a parent; descendants are anything inside, at any depth."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting the . or #: querySelector(\"card\") looks for a <card> element and returns null. Adding a # with getElementById: getElementById(\"#title\") returns null. It takes the plain id. Treating a NodeList like one element: cards.classList.add() fails. Loop over the list. Selecting elements before they exist, because the script isn't deferred."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"DOM scripting introductionThe DOM tree, selecting, changing and creating elements. Document.querySelector() and Element.classListFull reference. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-dom/quiz"],"exercises":["js-dom/ex-dom"]},{"slug":"js-events","num":"7.2","title":"Events","desc":"React to clicks, typing and form submits, and see how events bubble up to parents.","props":["addEventListener","preventDefault()","event.target"],"intro":"Events are signals the browser sends when something happens: a click, a key press, a form being submitted. You listen for them with addEventListener and run a function in response.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-reacting-to-what-people-do","title":"Reacting to what people do","kind":"Read","text":"Most JavaScript on a page waits for something to happen: a click, a key press, a form being submitted. Each of those is an event, a signal the browser sends. You listen for one with addEventListener(): const btn = document.querySelector(\"#save\"); btn.addEventListener(\"click\", saveTrail); function saveTrail(e) { console.log(\"Clicked:\", e.target); } Give it the event's name as a string, then the function to run. Pass the function itself, without parentheses: saveTrail, not saveTrail(). With parentheses, the function runs immediately, once, and its result is passed instead. The browser hands your function an event object, usually named e. event.target is the element the event happened on. Useful event names include click, input (every change to a field), submit and keydown. Some events come with a default action. Submitting a form reloads the page; clicking a link navigates away. preventDefault() stops that, so your code can handle it instead. Events also travel. A click on a button inside a card also counts as a click on the card, then on its parent, all the way up to the document. This is event bubbling, and it makes a powerful trick possible. Instead of adding a listener to each of 50 items, add one to their container and check e.target to see which item was clicked. That's event delegation, and it even works for items added later. Watch out Listen for submit on the form, not click on its button. Pressing Enter in a field submits the form without clicking anything. Play"},{"id":"sec-listen-for-events","title":"Listen for events","kind":"Play","text":"Pick an event type and interact with the elements to see when it fires. Listen for e.preventDefault() on submit Clear log Click or double-click me Move the pointer in and out of this box Trail name (type, tab in and out, press Enter) Submit Play"},{"id":"sec-bubbling","title":"Bubbling","kind":"Play","text":"When you click the button, the click doesn't stop there. It bubbles up through every ancestor, and each one's click listener runs in turn. This is the setup from MDN's bubbling example. e.stopPropagation() in the button's listener { capture: true } on body and div body div#container Click me Click the button, then click in the div outside the button. Play"},{"id":"sec-event-delegation","title":"Event delegation","kind":"Play","text":"Instead of adding a listener to each of the 16 tiles, add one listener to their container. Clicks on any tile bubble up to it, and event.target tells you which tile was clicked. Ignore clicks that aren't on a tile Click some tiles, then click in a gap between them. Reset tiles Practice"},{"id":"sec-respond-to-events","title":"Respond to events","kind":"Practice","text":"Wire up a click counter and a form that doesn't reload the page."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"el.addEventListener(\"click\", handler): the event type as a string, then the function to run. Pass the function itself, without (). The handler receives an event object, usually named e or event. e.target is the element the event happened on. e.preventDefault() stops the browser's default action: a form reloading the page, or a link navigating. Useful types: click, input (every change to a field), change, submit, keydown, focus, blur, mouseover, mouseout. Events bubble from the target up through its ancestors. Delegation uses this: one listener on a parent handles all its children, even ones added later. Remove a listener with removeEventListener, passing the same named function."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"addEventListener(\"click\", go()) runs go immediately. Pass go. \"onclick\" as the event name. The type is just \"click\". Inline HTML handlers like onclick=\"…\". MDN says never to use them: they mix languages and are often blocked by security settings. Listening for click on a form's submit button instead of submit on the form, which misses submissions made by pressing Enter. Forgetting preventDefault(), so the page reloads and your changes vanish."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Introduction to eventsaddEventListener, the event object and preventDefault. Event bubblingBubbling, capture, target vs currentTarget, and delegation. Element eventsA list of event types. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-events/quiz"],"exercises":["js-events/ex-events"]},{"slug":"js-create-elements","num":"7.3","title":"Creating & Removing Elements","desc":"Build MDN's shopping list: create elements, add them to the page, and remove them.","props":["createElement()","append()","remove()"],"intro":"JavaScript can build new elements and add them to the page, then take them away again. This shopping list is MDN's DOM scripting exercise. Add and delete items and watch which lines of code run.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-building-the-page-from-code","title":"Building the page from code","kind":"Read","text":"So far you've changed elements that were already in the HTML. JavaScript can also build new ones, which is how a to-do list grows when you add an item, or how search results appear. It takes three steps: const li = document.createElement(\"li\"); // 1. make it li.textContent = \"Trail mix\"; // 2. fill it li.classList.add(\"item\"); list.append(li); // 3. attach it createElement() makes an element in memory. It isn't on the page yet, and it won't show up anywhere until you attach it to something that is. Forgetting that last step is the most common reason a new element never appears. append() adds it as the last child; prepend() adds it as the first. You'll also see the older appendChild(), which works the same way. To take an element away, call remove() on it: li.remove(). An element can only be in one place at a time. Appending an element that's already on the page moves it rather than copying it. To make a copy, use cloneNode(true). There's a shortcut you'll see a lot: setting innerHTML to a string of HTML. It's handy for building markup you wrote yourself. But never use it for text a visitor typed. If someone types <img src=x onerror=…>, innerHTML turns it into a real element, and the code inside runs. textContent shows exactly what was typed and never runs it. Tip After adding an item to a list, clear the input and put focus back in it with input.focus(), so people can type the next one straight away. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-shopping-list","title":"Build a shopping list","kind":"Play","text":"Add and delete items and watch which lines of code run. Then try each mistake. Try a mistake Leave out event.preventDefault() Forget list.appendChild(listItem) Use innerHTML instead of textContent Add a few items, then turn on a mistake and add another. For the innerHTML one, try typing <b>Milk</b>. My shopping list Enter a new item Add item Add an item. Play"},{"id":"sec-created-isn-t-the-same-as-on-the-page","title":"Created isn't the same as on the page","kind":"Play","text":"createElement() makes an element in memory. It doesn't show up anywhere until you attach it to something that's already on the page. Next line ▸ Restart In memory (not on the page) On the page Trail mix Water Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-and-remove-list-items","title":"Build and remove list items","kind":"Practice","text":"Write two functions: one adds an item to a list, the other clears out finished ones."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Make an element: const li = document.createElement(\"li\"); Fill it: li.textContent = \"Milk\";, li.classList.add(\"item\"); Put it on the page: list.appendChild(li), or list.append(li) (which can also take text). prepend() adds at the start. Take it off: li.remove(). (MDN's exercise uses the older list.removeChild(li), which works the same.) Appending an element that is already on the page moves it. Use cloneNode(true) to make a copy. Use textContent for anything a person typed. It shows the text exactly as typed and never runs it as HTML."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Creating an element but never appending it, so nothing appears and there's no error. Using innerHTML with user input. Someone can type HTML that runs code on your page (cross-site scripting). Forgetting preventDefault() on the form, so the page reloads and the list is wiped. Adding the delete listener to the wrong thing, such as the whole list instead of each item's button."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Active learning: a dynamic shopping listThe original exercise, step by step. Document.createElement() and innerHTML securityWhy textContent is safer. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-create-elements/quiz"],"exercises":["js-create-elements/ex-create"]},{"slug":"js-fetch","num":"7.4","title":"Fetching Data","desc":"Load JSON from a server with fetch(), handle 404s and failures, and update the page.","props":["fetch()","async / await","response.ok"],"intro":"fetch() asks a server for data, usually JSON, and lets you update part of the page without reloading it. The answer takes time to arrive, so your code has to wait for it and be ready for things to go wrong.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-asking-a-server-for-data","title":"Asking a server for data","kind":"Read","text":"Many pages update part of themselves without reloading: new search results, the latest weather, more posts as you scroll. They ask a server for data with fetch(), usually in JSON format, then use the DOM to show it. The answer takes time to arrive, from a few milliseconds to several seconds. JavaScript doesn't stop and wait; the code after fetch() keeps running right away. Instead, fetch() gives you a promise: a placeholder for a value that will arrive later. async / await lets you write code that waits for it: async function loadTrails() { try { const response = await fetch(\"trails.json\"); if (!response.ok) throw new Error(response.status); const trails = await response.json(); showTrails(trails); } catch (err) { status.textContent = \"Couldn't load trails.\"; } } await pauses that function, and only that function, until the promise settles. response.json() reads the body and parses it, and it's a promise too, so it needs its own await. You'll also see the older style, with .then() for success and .catch() for failure. It does the same thing. Plan for things to go wrong. The network can drop, and try/catch catches that. But a 404 \"not found\" is a completed request as far as fetch() is concerned, so it won't land in catch by itself. Check response.ok and throw an error when it's false. Watch out Pages opened by double-clicking the file (file://) can't fetch. Use a local server, such as VS Code's Live Server, or test on GitHub Pages. Play"},{"id":"sec-watch-a-request-happen","title":"Watch a request happen","kind":"Play","text":"Send a request and follow it to the server and back. Try a slow network and a missing file. What happens on the server Code style .then() async / await Send request ▸ Boulder trails Press Send request. Network panel Play"},{"id":"sec-the-file-being-fetched","title":"The file being fetched","kind":"Play","text":"The server sends back plain text in JSON format. response.json() parses it into an array of objects, which the code loops over to build the list. trails.json showTrails() Practice"},{"id":"sec-handle-the-response","title":"Handle the response","kind":"Practice","text":"Write the pieces a fetch needs: a check for failed responses, and a function that pulls out the data you want."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"fetch(url) returns a promise: a placeholder for a value that will arrive later. .then() runs when it does, and .catch() runs if something fails. async/await is another way to write the same thing. await pauses that function until the promise settles, and try/catch handles errors. Code after fetch() keeps running right away. It doesn't wait for the response. A 404 is not an error to fetch. Check response.ok yourself and throw if it's false. response.json() reads the body and parses it as JSON (it also returns a promise). Pages opened as file:// can't fetch. Use a local server, such as VS Code's Live Server, or test on GitHub Pages."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using the data on the line after fetch(). It isn't there yet. Use it inside .then() or after await. Forgetting return before response.json() in a .then(), so the next .then() gets undefined. Skipping the response.ok check, then trying to parse a 404 error page as JSON. Using await outside an async function (only allowed at the top level of a module). Opening the HTML file directly and wondering why every request fails with Failed to fetch."},{"id":"sec-on-mdn","title":"On MDN","kind":"notes","text":"Making network requests with JavaScriptThe fetch() promise chain, response.ok and catch. Challenge: Building a house data UIFetch JSON, filter it, and render it. How do you set up a local testing server?Needed for fetch and modules. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["js-fetch/quiz"],"exercises":["js-fetch/ex-fetch"]},{"slug":"review-browser","num":"7.5","title":"Review: JavaScript in the Browser","desc":"Build an interactive trail list: render, filter and mark items done. Then a mixed quiz on the whole part.","props":["recap","mini project","10 questions"],"intro":"Put the whole book together. Skim the recap, build an interactive trail list, then test yourself with mixed questions.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-you-ve-covered","title":"What you've covered","kind":"Read","text":"This part connected JavaScript to the page: finding elements, reacting to what people do, building new elements, and loading data from a server. Selecting & Changing Elements: querySelector finds elements with CSS selectors; textContent and classList change them. Events: addEventListener runs a function when something happens; preventDefault stops the browser's own action; events bubble. Creating & Removing Elements: createElement, then fill it, then append it. Use textContent for anything a person typed. Fetching Data: fetch returns a promise; await it, check response.ok, parse the JSON, and catch errors. How to use this page Try the project without looking back at the lessons. When you get stuck, or miss a quiz question, the lesson it comes from is linked above. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mini-project-an-interactive-trail-list","title":"Mini project: an interactive trail list","kind":"Practice","text":"Render a list from data, filter it as someone types, and mark trails done with one listener. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-mixed-quiz","title":"Mixed quiz","kind":"Check yourself","text":"Ten questions from across the part, in no particular order."}],"quizzes":["review-browser/quiz"],"exercises":["review-browser/ex-project"]}],"terms":[{"term":"!important","id":"term-important","cat":"css","def":"A flag that makes a declaration beat every normal rule, regardless of specificity. Avoid it: it makes styles hard to override later.","ex":"color: red !important;","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Absolute path","id":"term-absolute-path","cat":"html","def":"A full web address including the domain. Use it to link to other websites.","ex":"https://developer.mozilla.org","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"Accessibility (a11y)","id":"term-accessibility-a11y","cat":"workflow","def":"Making sites usable by everyone, including people using screen readers, keyboards, zoom, or captions. \"a11y\" is short for the 11 letters between a and y.","ex":"","page":"accessibility.html"},{"term":"Alt text","id":"term-alt-text","cat":"html","def":"The alt attribute on an image: a text description read by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load. Use alt=\"\" for decorative images.","ex":"<img src=\"arch.jpg\" alt=\"Royal Arch above the trees\">","page":"images.html"},{"term":"Animation","id":"term-animation","cat":"css","def":"Motion defined with @keyframes that runs by itself, as opposed to a transition, which runs when a style changes.","ex":"animation: pulse 1s infinite;","page":"transitions.html"},{"term":"aspect-ratio","id":"term-aspect-ratio","cat":"css","def":"Keeps an element's width-to-height shape as its size changes.","ex":"aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;","page":"sizing.html"},{"term":"Attribute","id":"term-attribute","cat":"html","def":"Extra information in an element's opening tag, written as name=\"value\".","ex":"<a href=\"about.html\">","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Attribute selector","id":"term-attribute-selector","cat":"css","def":"Selects elements by their attributes, using square brackets.","ex":"a[href$=\".pdf\"]","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"BEM","id":"term-bem","cat":"workflow","def":"A class-naming pattern: Block, Element, Modifier. It makes it clear which classes belong together.","ex":".card__title, .btn--large","page":"organizing-css.html"},{"term":"Block element","id":"term-block-element","cat":"css","def":"An element that starts on a new line and fills the available width, like a div, p or h1.","ex":"display: block;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"Border","id":"term-border","cat":"css","def":"The line around an element's padding. Part of the box model, between padding and margin.","ex":"border: 2px solid gray;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"border-radius","id":"term-border-radius","cat":"css","def":"Rounds an element's corners. 50% on a square makes a circle.","ex":"border-radius: 8px;","page":"backgrounds-borders.html"},{"term":"box-shadow","id":"term-box-shadow","cat":"css","def":"Draws a shadow around an element's box, set by x and y offset, blur, spread and color.","ex":"box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgb(0 0 0 / 15%);","page":"shadows.html"},{"term":"box-sizing","id":"term-box-sizing","cat":"css","def":"Decides whether width includes padding and border. border-box is easier to work with, and most stylesheets set it on everything.","ex":"* { box-sizing: border-box; }","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Box model","id":"term-box-model","cat":"css","def":"Every element is a rectangle made of four layers: content, padding, border and margin.","ex":"","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Breakpoint","id":"term-breakpoint","cat":"layout","def":"A screen width where a media query changes the layout.","ex":"@media (min-width: 600px)","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Browser cache","id":"term-browser-cache","cat":"workflow","def":"Saved copies of files your browser keeps to load pages faster. A hard refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R, or Cmd+Shift+R on a Mac) skips it to show your latest changes.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Cascade","id":"term-cascade","cat":"css","def":"The rules the browser uses to choose between conflicting declarations: importance, then specificity, then source order.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Character entity","id":"term-character-entity","cat":"html","def":"A code for a character that would otherwise be read as HTML, or is hard to type.","ex":"&lt;  &amp;  &copy;","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Child","id":"term-child","cat":"html","def":"An element directly inside another element. The outer one is its parent.","ex":"<ul> <li>child</li> </ul>","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Class","id":"term-class","cat":"css","def":"A reusable label added with the class attribute and selected in CSS with a dot. Many elements can share a class.","ex":".trail-card  →  <div class=\"trail-card\">","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"clear","id":"term-clear","cat":"layout","def":"Makes an element start below any floated elements before it.","ex":"clear: both;","page":"floats.html"},{"term":"Combinator","id":"term-combinator","cat":"css","def":"The part of a selector that describes a relationship: space (descendant), > (child), + (next sibling), ~ (later sibling).","ex":".card > a","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Comment","id":"term-comment","cat":"html","def":"Notes in your code that the browser ignores. HTML and CSS use different syntax.","ex":"<!-- HTML -->   /* CSS */","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Contrast ratio","id":"term-contrast-ratio","cat":"workflow","def":"How different text and background colors are in brightness. Body text needs at least 4.5 : 1.","ex":"","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Cross axis","id":"term-cross-axis","cat":"layout","def":"In Flexbox, the direction perpendicular to the main axis. align-items works along it.","ex":"","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"CSS","id":"term-css","cat":"css","def":"Cascading Style Sheets: the language that controls how HTML looks, from colors and fonts to layout.","ex":"","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"CSS variable","id":"term-css-variable","cat":"css","def":"A reusable value with a name starting with two dashes, read with var(). Also called a custom property.","ex":"--brand: #2340d9;  color: var(--brand);","page":"css-variables.html"},{"term":"Declaration","id":"term-declaration","cat":"css","def":"One property and its value, ending in a semicolon.","ex":"color: navy;","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Descendant","id":"term-descendant","cat":"html","def":"Any element inside another one, at any depth: children, grandchildren and so on.","ex":".card p","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"DevTools","id":"term-devtools","cat":"workflow","def":"Tools built into every browser for inspecting the HTML and CSS of a page. Open with F12, or Cmd+Option+I on a Mac.","ex":"","page":"devtools.html"},{"term":"display","id":"term-display","cat":"css","def":"The property that sets how an element is laid out: block, inline, inline-block, flex, grid or none.","ex":"display: inline-block;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"DOCTYPE","id":"term-doctype","cat":"html","def":"The first line of every HTML file. It tells the browser to use modern standards mode.","ex":"<!DOCTYPE html>","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"DOM","id":"term-dom","cat":"js","def":"Document Object Model: the tree of elements the browser builds from your HTML. JavaScript reads and changes the page through it, and DevTools' Elements panel shows it after the browser has repaired any errors.","ex":"document.querySelector(\"h1\")","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Element","id":"term-element","cat":"html","def":"A piece of a web page: an opening tag, its content and a closing tag.","ex":"<p>Hello</p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"em (unit)","id":"term-em-unit","cat":"css","def":"A length relative to the current element's font size. Nested em sizes multiply.","ex":"padding: 1em;","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Empty element","id":"term-empty-element","cat":"html","def":"An element with no content and no closing tag. Also called a void element.","ex":"<img>  <br>  <input>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Fallback font","id":"term-fallback-font","cat":"css","def":"The next font in a font-family list, used when the first isn't available. Always end with a generic family like serif or sans-serif.","ex":"font-family: \"Lora\", Georgia, serif;","page":"web-fonts.html"},{"term":"figure / figcaption","id":"term-figure-figcaption","cat":"html","def":"Wraps an image, chart or code sample with its visible caption.","ex":"<figure><img …><figcaption>…</figcaption></figure>","page":"images.html"},{"term":"filter","id":"term-filter","cat":"css","def":"Applies visual effects like blur, grayscale or drop shadows to an element.","ex":"filter: grayscale(1);","page":"shadows.html"},{"term":"Flexbox","id":"term-flexbox","cat":"layout","def":"A layout method for arranging items in one row or column, with control over spacing and alignment.","ex":"display: flex;","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"Float","id":"term-float","cat":"layout","def":"Moves an element to the left or right so text wraps around it.","ex":"float: left;","page":"floats.html"},{"term":"Focus","id":"term-focus","cat":"workflow","def":"The element that keyboard input goes to. Tab moves focus; :focus-visible styles show where it is.","ex":"","page":"pseudo-classes.html"},{"term":"Font stack","id":"term-font-stack","cat":"css","def":"A list of fonts in font-family, tried in order.","ex":"font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;","page":"typography.html"},{"term":"fr (unit)","id":"term-fr-unit","cat":"layout","def":"A fraction of the free space in a grid container.","ex":"grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;","page":"grid.html"},{"term":"Gap","id":"term-gap","cat":"layout","def":"Space between items in a flex or grid container, without margins on each item.","ex":"gap: 16px;","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"GitHub Pages","id":"term-github-pages","cat":"workflow","def":"A free service that publishes the HTML, CSS and JavaScript files in a GitHub repository as a website.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Gradient","id":"term-gradient","cat":"css","def":"A smooth blend between colors, used as a background image.","ex":"linear-gradient(135deg, teal, navy)","page":"backgrounds-borders.html"},{"term":"Grid","id":"term-grid","cat":"layout","def":"A layout method for rows and columns at the same time.","ex":"display: grid;","page":"grid.html"},{"term":"Head","id":"term-head","cat":"html","def":"The part of an HTML document with information about the page, like its title and stylesheet links. Not shown on the page.","ex":"<head> … </head>","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"Heading","id":"term-heading","cat":"html","def":"h1 to h6 elements that give a page its outline. Use one h1 and don't skip levels.","ex":"<h2>Conditions</h2>","page":"text-semantics.html"},{"term":"Hex color","id":"term-hex-color","cat":"css","def":"A color written as # followed by pairs of red, green and blue values from 00 to ff.","ex":"#2340d9","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"HSL","id":"term-hsl","cat":"css","def":"A color written as hue, saturation and lightness. Easy to adjust by hand.","ex":"hsl(225 72% 50%)","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"HTML","id":"term-html","cat":"html","def":"HyperText Markup Language: the language that describes the structure and meaning of web page content.","ex":"","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"ID","id":"term-id","cat":"css","def":"A unique name for one element, selected with #. IDs have very high specificity, so prefer classes for styling.","ex":"#featured  →  <div id=\"featured\">","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"index.html","id":"term-index-html","cat":"workflow","def":"The name for a site's home page, or the main page in any folder. Servers open it automatically.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Inheritance","id":"term-inheritance","cat":"css","def":"Some properties, mostly text-related ones like color and font, pass from a parent to its children automatically.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Inline element","id":"term-inline-element","cat":"css","def":"An element that flows within a line of text, like span, a or strong. Width and height don't apply.","ex":"display: inline;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"Inline style","id":"term-inline-style","cat":"css","def":"CSS written in an element's style attribute. It beats stylesheet rules, so avoid it.","ex":"<p style=\"color: red\">","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Label","id":"term-label","cat":"html","def":"Text connected to a form control. Its for attribute matches the input's id.","ex":"<label for=\"email\">Email</label>","page":"forms.html"},{"term":"Landmark","id":"term-landmark","cat":"html","def":"A major page region, like header, nav, main or footer, that screen reader users can jump to.","ex":"","page":"semantic-elements.html"},{"term":"Main axis","id":"term-main-axis","cat":"layout","def":"In Flexbox, the direction items are laid out in, set by flex-direction. justify-content works along it.","ex":"","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"Margin","id":"term-margin","cat":"css","def":"Transparent space outside an element's border that pushes other elements away.","ex":"margin: 0 auto;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Media query","id":"term-media-query","cat":"layout","def":"A condition that applies CSS only when it's true, usually based on screen width.","ex":"@media (min-width: 900px) { … }","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Mobile first","id":"term-mobile-first","cat":"layout","def":"Writing the phone layout first, then adding min-width media queries for larger screens.","ex":"","page":"mobile-first.html"},{"term":"Nesting","id":"term-nesting","cat":"html","def":"Putting elements inside other elements. Inner ones must close before outer ones.","ex":"<p><strong>…</strong></p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Normal flow","id":"term-normal-flow","cat":"layout","def":"How elements are laid out with no layout CSS: blocks stack down the page, inline content flows in lines.","ex":"","page":"display.html"},{"term":"object-fit","id":"term-object-fit","cat":"css","def":"Decides how an image fills a box of a different shape: stretch, fit inside, or crop to cover.","ex":"object-fit: cover;","page":"responsive-images.html"},{"term":"Overflow","id":"term-overflow","cat":"css","def":"What happens when content is bigger than its box: visible, hidden, or scrollable.","ex":"overflow: auto;","page":"overflow.html"},{"term":"Padding","id":"term-padding","cat":"css","def":"Space inside an element, between its content and its border.","ex":"padding: 16px;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Parent","id":"term-parent","cat":"html","def":"The element directly containing another element.","ex":"","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Placeholder","id":"term-placeholder","cat":"html","def":"Hint text shown in an empty input. It disappears when typing starts, so it can't replace a label.","ex":"placeholder=\"you@example.com\"","page":"forms.html"},{"term":"position","id":"term-position","cat":"layout","def":"Controls how an element is placed: static, relative, absolute, fixed or sticky.","ex":"position: absolute;","page":"positioning.html"},{"term":"Property","id":"term-property","cat":"css","def":"What a declaration changes, like color, margin or font-size.","ex":"font-size","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Pseudo-class","id":"term-pseudo-class","cat":"css","def":"A keyword with one colon that selects elements in a state or position.","ex":":hover  :focus-visible  :nth-child(2)","page":"pseudo-classes.html"},{"term":"Pseudo-element","id":"term-pseudo-element","cat":"css","def":"A keyword with two colons that styles part of an element or adds decorative content.","ex":"::before  ::first-letter","page":"pseudo-elements.html"},{"term":"Relative path","id":"term-relative-path","cat":"html","def":"Directions to a file starting from the current file's folder. ../ goes up one folder.","ex":"../images/logo.png","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"rem (unit)","id":"term-rem-unit","cat":"css","def":"A length relative to the root (html) font size, usually 16px. Good for font sizes.","ex":"font-size: 1.25rem;","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Repository","id":"term-repository","cat":"workflow","def":"A project folder tracked by Git, with its full history of changes. On GitHub, it's often called a repo.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Responsive design","id":"term-responsive-design","cat":"layout","def":"Building pages that adapt to any screen size, using flexible layouts, images and media queries.","ex":"","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Root-relative path","id":"term-root-relative-path","cat":"html","def":"A path starting with /, measured from the top of the domain. Breaks on GitHub Pages project sites.","ex":"/images/logo.png","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"Rule","id":"term-rule","cat":"css","def":"A selector plus its block of declarations. Also called a ruleset.","ex":"h1 { color: navy; }","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Screen reader","id":"term-screen-reader","cat":"workflow","def":"Software that reads a page aloud for people who are blind or have low vision. It relies on semantic HTML and alt text.","ex":"","page":"accessibility.html"},{"term":"Selector","id":"term-selector","cat":"css","def":"The part of a rule that picks which elements to style.","ex":".card h2","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Semantic HTML","id":"term-semantic-html","cat":"html","def":"Choosing elements for what content means, like nav, article or button, rather than how it looks.","ex":"","page":"semantic-elements.html"},{"term":"Sibling","id":"term-sibling","cat":"html","def":"Elements that share the same parent.","ex":"h2 + p","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Source order","id":"term-source-order","cat":"css","def":"Where a rule appears in the CSS. When everything else ties, the later rule wins.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Specificity","id":"term-specificity","cat":"css","def":"A score for how targeted a selector is: IDs beat classes, classes beat elements. Higher specificity wins a conflict.","ex":"#nav a  beats  .link","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"srcset","id":"term-srcset","cat":"html","def":"A list of image files at different widths. The browser downloads the smallest one that will still look sharp.","ex":"srcset=\"trail-400.jpg 400w, trail-800.jpg 800w\"","page":"responsive-images.html"},{"term":"Stylesheet","id":"term-stylesheet","cat":"css","def":"A .css file of rules, linked from the head of each HTML page.","ex":"<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Tag","id":"term-tag","cat":"html","def":"The text in angle brackets that marks where an element starts or ends.","ex":"<p>  </p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Transition","id":"term-transition","cat":"css","def":"Smoothly animates a change from one style to another, such as on hover.","ex":"transition: background 0.2s ease;","page":"transitions.html"},{"term":"Type selector","id":"term-type-selector","cat":"css","def":"A selector that matches every element of one kind by its tag name.","ex":"p  h2  button","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Unit","id":"term-unit","cat":"css","def":"What a number measures in, like px, rem, %, vw or s. Most values except 0 need one.","ex":"16px  1.5rem  50%","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"URL","id":"term-url","cat":"html","def":"A web address, like https://example.com/trails/index.html.","ex":"","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"User agent stylesheet","id":"term-user-agent-stylesheet","cat":"css","def":"The browser's built-in default styles, such as margins on headings and blue underlined links.","ex":"","page":"devtools.html"},{"term":"Validator","id":"term-validator","cat":"workflow","def":"A tool that checks HTML or CSS against the official rules and reports errors with line numbers.","ex":"","page":"validation.html"},{"term":"Value","id":"term-value","cat":"css","def":"The setting given to a property in a declaration.","ex":"navy, 2rem, auto","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Viewport","id":"term-viewport","cat":"layout","def":"The visible area of the browser window. vw and vh are percentages of it.","ex":"","page":"sizing.html"},{"term":"Viewport meta tag","id":"term-viewport-meta-tag","cat":"html","def":"Tells phones to use the real screen width instead of shrinking a desktop-sized page.","ex":"<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"Web font","id":"term-web-font","cat":"css","def":"A font downloaded with the page, for example from Google Fonts, so everyone sees the same typeface.","ex":"","page":"web-fonts.html"},{"term":"Whitespace","id":"term-whitespace","cat":"html","def":"Spaces, tabs and line breaks. In HTML, any run of whitespace shows as a single space.","ex":"","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"z-index","id":"term-z-index","cat":"layout","def":"Controls which positioned elements appear on top when they overlap. Higher numbers come forward.","ex":"z-index: 10;","page":"positioning.html"},{"term":"Client and server","id":"term-client-and-server","cat":"workflow","def":"The browser is the client: it asks for files. The server is the computer that stores the site and sends them back.","ex":"","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Domain name","id":"term-domain-name","cat":"workflow","def":"The human-friendly name of a server, like github.io. DNS looks up the numeric address behind it.","ex":"trailclub.github.io","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"HTTP status code","id":"term-http-status-code","cat":"workflow","def":"A number the server sends back with every response. 200 means OK, 404 means not found.","ex":"404 Not Found","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Local server","id":"term-local-server","cat":"workflow","def":"A small web server running on your own computer, like VS Code's Live Server, so you can test pages the way the real web serves them.","ex":"http://127.0.0.1:5500","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Favicon","id":"term-favicon","cat":"html","def":"The small icon in the browser tab and bookmarks, linked from the head.","ex":"<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Meta description","id":"term-meta-description","cat":"html","def":"A one-sentence summary of a page in the head. Search engines may show it under your title.","ex":"<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Open Graph","id":"term-open-graph","cat":"html","def":"og: meta tags that control the preview card when a link is shared in chats and social posts.","ex":"<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://…\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Lossy / lossless","id":"term-lossy-lossless","cat":"workflow","def":"Lossy formats like JPG throw away some detail to make files smaller. Lossless formats like PNG keep every pixel.","ex":"","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"SVG","id":"term-svg","cat":"html","def":"Scalable Vector Graphics: an image made of shapes described in code, so it stays sharp at any size. Best for logos and icons.","ex":"<img src=\"logo.svg\" alt=\"Trail Club\">","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"WebP","id":"term-webp","cat":"html","def":"A modern image format that's usually smaller than JPG or PNG at the same quality, and supports transparency.","ex":"photo.webp","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"iframe","id":"term-iframe","cat":"html","def":"An element that shows another web page inside yours. Used for YouTube videos, maps and other embeds.","ex":"<iframe src=\"…\" title=\"Trail map\"></iframe>","page":"embeds.html"},{"term":"Embed","id":"term-embed","cat":"html","def":"Content from another site shown on your page, usually with an iframe the other site gives you.","ex":"","page":"embeds.html"},{"term":"sizes","id":"term-sizes","cat":"html","def":"Tells the browser how wide an image will display at different window widths, so it can pick the right file from srcset.","ex":"sizes=\"(min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw\"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"picture","id":"term-picture","cat":"html","def":"Wraps several source elements and an img, so the browser can choose a different crop or format.","ex":"<picture><source …><img …></picture>","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"Art direction","id":"term-art-direction","cat":"html","def":"Showing a different crop of an image at different screen sizes, not just a smaller copy.","ex":"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"Lazy loading","id":"term-lazy-loading","cat":"html","def":"Waiting to download an image or iframe until the visitor scrolls near it.","ex":"loading=\"lazy\"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"clamp()","id":"term-clamp","cat":"css","def":"A value that grows with the screen but stays between a minimum and a maximum: clamp(min, preferred, max).","ex":"font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"min() / max()","id":"term-min-max","cat":"css","def":"min() uses the smallest of its values, max() the largest. min() works like a max-width.","ex":"width: min(640px, 100% - 2rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"calc()","id":"term-calc","cat":"css","def":"Does math with mixed units. The + and - need spaces around them.","ex":"width: calc(100% - 2rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"Transform","id":"term-transform","cat":"css","def":"Moves, rotates, scales or skews an element after layout, without moving anything around it.","ex":"transform: rotate(10deg) scale(1.1);","page":"transforms.html"},{"term":"transform-origin","id":"term-transform-origin","cat":"css","def":"The point an element rotates and scales around. The default is its center.","ex":"transform-origin: top left;","page":"transforms.html"},{"term":"addEventListener()","id":"term-addeventlistener","cat":"js","def":"Runs a function whenever a certain event happens on an element. Pass the function itself, without parentheses.","ex":"btn.addEventListener(\"click\", openMenu);","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Argument","id":"term-argument","cat":"js","def":"A value you pass into a function when you call it. It fills in one of the function's parameters.","ex":"hikeTime(6.7, 2.5)","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Array","id":"term-array","cat":"js","def":"An ordered list of values stored under one name. Items are numbered from 0.","ex":"const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"];","page":"js-arrays.html"},{"term":"Arrow function","id":"term-arrow-function","cat":"js","def":"A short way to write a function. With one expression and no braces, it returns that value automatically.","ex":"const double = (n) => n * 2;","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"async / await","id":"term-async-await","cat":"js","def":"A way to write code that waits for a promise, such as fetch(), as if it ran top to bottom. await only works inside an async function (or a module).","ex":"const res = await fetch(url);","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"Boolean","id":"term-boolean","cat":"js","def":"A value that is either true or false. Comparisons produce booleans.","ex":"const isOpen = miles < 5;","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"classList","id":"term-classlist","cat":"js","def":"An element's list of classes, with add(), remove() and toggle() methods. The usual way to change how an element looks from JavaScript.","ex":"menu.classList.toggle(\"open\");","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Concatenation","id":"term-concatenation","cat":"js","def":"Joining strings together with +. If either side is a string, + joins instead of adding.","ex":"\"5\" + 5 // \"55\"","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Conditional","id":"term-conditional","cat":"js","def":"Code that only runs if a condition is true: if, else if and else.","ex":"if (temp < 32) { … } else { … }","page":"js-conditionals.html"},{"term":"console.log()","id":"term-console-log","cat":"js","def":"Prints a value to the browser's console. The quickest way to check what your code is doing.","ex":"console.log(\"score is\", score);","page":"js-errors.html"},{"term":"const","id":"term-const","cat":"js","def":"Declares a variable that can't be reassigned. Use it by default; switch to let only when the value must change.","ex":"const name = \"Ava\";","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"createElement()","id":"term-createelement","cat":"js","def":"Makes a new element in memory. It appears on the page only after you append it somewhere.","ex":"const li = document.createElement(\"li\");","page":"js-create-elements.html"},{"term":"defer","id":"term-defer","cat":"js","def":"A script attribute that downloads the file while the page loads and runs it after the HTML is read, so your code can find every element.","ex":"<script defer src=\"script.js\"></script>","page":"js-intro.html"},{"term":"Event","id":"term-event","cat":"js","def":"A signal that something happened, such as a click, a key press or a form being submitted.","ex":"click, input, submit, keydown","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event bubbling","id":"term-event-bubbling","cat":"js","def":"After an event fires on an element, it travels up through each ancestor, running their listeners too.","ex":"","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event delegation","id":"term-event-delegation","cat":"js","def":"Putting one listener on a parent to handle events from all its children, using event.target to tell them apart.","ex":"list.addEventListener(\"click\", (e) => { … });","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"event.target","id":"term-event-target","cat":"js","def":"The element an event actually happened on. Compare event.currentTarget, the element whose listener is running.","ex":"e.target.textContent","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"fetch()","id":"term-fetch","cat":"js","def":"Requests a file or data from a server and returns a promise for the response. Doesn't work on pages opened from file://.","ex":"fetch(\"trails.json\")","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"Function","id":"term-function","cat":"js","def":"A named, reusable block of code. It can take parameters and return a value.","ex":"function greet(name) { return `Hi ${name}`; }","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Index","id":"term-index","cat":"js","def":"An item's position in an array or a character's position in a string, counting from 0.","ex":"trails[0]","page":"js-arrays.html"},{"term":"JavaScript","id":"term-javascript","cat":"js","def":"The programming language of the web. It adds behavior to pages: responding to clicks, changing content and loading data.","ex":"","page":"js-intro.html"},{"term":"JSON","id":"term-json","cat":"js","def":"JavaScript Object Notation: a text format for data that looks like a JavaScript object, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no comments).","ex":"{ \"name\": \"Mesa\", \"miles\": 6.7 }","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"let","id":"term-let","cat":"js","def":"Declares a variable whose value can be changed later.","ex":"let score = 0; score = 10;","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Loop","id":"term-loop","cat":"js","def":"Code that repeats, usually once for each item in a list: for...of, for, or while.","ex":"for (const trail of trails) { … }","page":"js-loops.html"},{"term":"Method","id":"term-method","cat":"js","def":"A function that belongs to an object, called with a dot.","ex":"text.toUpperCase()","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"NaN","id":"term-nan","cat":"js","def":"\"Not a Number\": the result of math that failed, usually from trying to convert text that isn't a number.","ex":"Number(\"abc\") // NaN","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"null / undefined","id":"term-null-undefined","cat":"js","def":"Two ways of saying \"no value\". undefined means nothing was ever set; null means it was set to nothing on purpose. querySelector returns null when nothing matches.","ex":"","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Object","id":"term-object","cat":"js","def":"A group of related values stored under named keys.","ex":"const hike = { name: \"Mesa\", miles: 6.7 };","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"Parameter","id":"term-parameter","cat":"js","def":"A name in a function's definition that stands for a value passed in when it's called.","ex":"function hikeTime(miles, pace = 2)","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"preventDefault()","id":"term-preventdefault","cat":"js","def":"Stops the browser's default action for an event, like a form reloading the page or a link navigating away.","ex":"e.preventDefault();","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Promise","id":"term-promise","cat":"js","def":"A placeholder for a value that isn't ready yet, such as a server's response. Use .then() and .catch(), or await, to get the result.","ex":"fetch(url).then((res) => res.json())","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"querySelector()","id":"term-queryselector","cat":"js","def":"Finds the first element that matches a CSS selector, or null. querySelectorAll() finds every match.","ex":"document.querySelector(\".card\")","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Return value","id":"term-return-value","cat":"js","def":"The value a function sends back to where it was called, using return. Without return, it's undefined.","ex":"return miles / pace;","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Scope","id":"term-scope","cat":"js","def":"Where a variable can be used. Variables declared inside a function or block only exist there.","ex":"","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"String","id":"term-string","cat":"js","def":"Text, written in quotes: \"double\", 'single' or `backticks`.","ex":"\"Royal Arch\"","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Syntax error","id":"term-syntax-error","cat":"js","def":"A typo in the structure of the code, such as a missing bracket or quote. The whole script stops before it runs.","ex":"SyntaxError: missing ) after argument list","page":"js-errors.html"},{"term":"Template literal","id":"term-template-literal","cat":"js","def":"A string in backticks that can include variables with ${ }.","ex":"`${trail} is ${miles} miles`","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"textContent","id":"term-textcontent","cat":"js","def":"The text inside an element. Setting it replaces the contents with plain text, which is safer than innerHTML for anything a user typed.","ex":"heading.textContent = \"Hello\";","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Truthy / falsy","id":"term-truthy-falsy","cat":"js","def":"How a value behaves in an if. false, 0, \"\", null, undefined and NaN are falsy; everything else is truthy.","ex":"if (name) { … }","page":"js-conditionals.html"},{"term":"typeof","id":"term-typeof","cat":"js","def":"Tells you a value's type as a string.","ex":"typeof \"42\" // \"string\"","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Variable","id":"term-variable","cat":"js","def":"A named container for a value, declared with let or const.","ex":"const city = \"Boulder\";","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"=== (strict equality)","id":"term-strict-equality","cat":"js","def":"Checks whether two values are the same, including their type. Use it instead of ==.","ex":"\"5\" === 5 // false","page":"js-conditionals.html"}]};
